101.
We Can Run Away Together.


Normally, as soon as his head hit the pillow, Bill fell asleep and didn’t wake up until morning. This time, however, he was awoken in the middle of the night by a nightmare. He had dreamt that he had lost Layla. He
felt so disturbed by the bad dream, he didn’t want to think about it.
“What’s a horrible dream like that doing in my head?” he grumbled, sitting up in bed.
As he stared angrily into the darkness, he cast an even darker shadow on the floor.
He lay down again, hoping to get back to sleep, but immediately his head started swimming with unpleasant memories of the nightmare.
He didn’t understand where the bad thoughts were coming from. He and Layla were going to live a happy lite i n the capital, where she was going to go to college, thanks to the support of the House of Herhardt. Of course, he was a

little concerned about the fact that she and Kyle would be attending the same university. However, whether the two of them ended up getting back together or not, he was ready to accept her decision. With nothing but good things on the horizon for her, why had he had a bad dream about her?
Feeling very angry at the nightmare, he sat up in bed. He felt ready to pour a basin of ice water over his head, if it would clear his minds of these thoughts. Throwing off the covers, he headed to the kitchen to get a glass of water. Before he could turn on the faucet, however, he froze at the sight of something he saw through the window.
There was a slender young woman dressed in white walking towards the cottage on the forest path. Having just had a nightmare, he wondered if he was seeing things. Just as he was beginning to worry if there was something wrong with his brain, he recognized the woman.
“Layla…?”
The woman walking through the dark forest like a ghost was unmistakably Layla. However, she looked extremely tired. Her face bore the expression of a sad, tired old woman. What was going on with her?
He felt like running outside to see if it was really her, then he changed his mind and hurried back to bed. Shortly thereafter he heard the front door open, followed by the sound of careful, quiet steps. Not wanting to believe that it was her, he stayed in bed.
It was just a ghost, he told himself, practically wishing that it was true. It wasn’t her, it was just a ghost.







As if his wish had been granted, the Layla he saw at breakfast that morning looked like her usual self Having already gotten ready for work, she was busy preparing the food. Once everything was on the table, she sat down across from him, smiling. She looked brighter and livelier than an early-spring morning.
“Is something on your mind, Uncle Bill?” she asked, noticing the unusual look on his face.
“Huh? N-no,” he stammered, waving his hands in the air. “Why would I have something on my mind? Nope, I sure don’t…”
“Okay…” she replied, tilting her head as she served him some eggs and a piece of bread. His obstinate denial made her feel even more certain that something was wrong. “You really look like you have something to say. Did something happen?”
He shook his head adamantly. “What are you talking about?! Nothing happened.”
“Are you feeling well…?”
“Stop treating me like an old geezer. I’m still as strong and healthy as I ever was. I could lift you over my head right now. Want me to show you?”
“No, no, you don’t have to do that,” she replied, giggling. “I’m glad to hear you’re feeling well, though.”
The sight of her smiling face was so lovely, he felt a tinge of pain in his otherwise calm heart. It was only after he had adopted the young Layla that he

had come to realize that deep love always included a certain amount of sadness.
“You should eat up, or soon you’ll be as skinny as a rail again,” he said, spreading butter thickly on a piece of bread and holding it out to her.
As always, she took the food he was offering her with a gentle smile and obediently took a bite of it. Somehow, seeing her do this made him want to cry.
I really am getting old, he wistfully thought. He had already been getting on in years when she had shown up at his cottage as a little girl. Now she was all grown up.
He tucked into his breakfast, and by the time he had cleared his plate, his feelings of weakness had passed. He knew that he had to stay hearty and healthy for Layla’s sake. He needed to be around to see her grow wings and fly off to success, and to stop any bad guys from snatching her away. One day she was going to give birth to babies that looked just like her. He could only imagine how cute those grandchildren would be…
Suddenly he realized that he had grown very sentimental. He shook his head once again to clear his mind both of the sentimentality and of the lingering bad feelings from the nightmare.
After breakfast, instead of going out early to the garden like usual, he waited around so that he could see Layla off. She seemed moved by this simple gesture. As she left, she kept looking over her shoulder and waving. TH see you after work!” she shouted from amid the fresh smell of the early-spring plants.
He watched for a long time as she got on her bicycle and rode off into the distance. When we move to Ratse, I should buy her a new bike, he thought. Just then, he felt another pang in his heart. He ran his hands over his face, then

walked over to the wheelbarrow and started loading it up with tools. I’m turning into a crybaby in my old age, he thought.







“Do you really have to throw it away like that?” Riette asked. “You loved that thing?
Claudine glanced up at him with a look that showed she had no intention of changing her mind. Sh e then casually tossed the bracelet she was holding into the fire that was blazing in the fireplace. The torn-up letter followed close behind.
“As far as I’m concerned, anything that she has touched is defiled?” she said.
Riette doubted his ears for a moment. He couldn’t believe that she was talking about her fiance’s mistress in the casual tone of voice one would use to comment on the weather.
Putting his hand to his aching forehead, he stood up and walked towards the fireplace. Claudine watched as the fire devoured the items that had been tainted by Layla’s touch.
When Claudine’s maid had brought in a small package for her, Riette had paid it little mind.
Claudine was the most envied lady in Karlsbar’s high society, and she was constantly receiving letters and gifts from ladies who wanted to curry favor with her. He was taken aback, however, when he had learned that the package was from Layla Llewellyn. Especially once he saw what was inside.

The broken bracelet that Claudine had dropped on the ground in the garden had been repaired. It looked good as new. Apparently Layla had paid for this out of her own pocket. She had also included a letter, in which she had apologized, and had confirmed that her relationship with Matthias was exactly as Claudine had guessed it to be.
Riette had already assumed this to be the case, so it hadn’t surprised him. Instead, what had shocked him was the reaction of Claudine, his beloved cousin.
She had proceeded to tell him with casual indifference about all the things she had done to Layla —how she had gotten Kyle Ettman to confront her about her relationship with the duke, and how when that didn’t work, she had met with her herself and acted cruelly towards her in order to scare her from doing anything more. These cold, underhanded tricks seemed unbefitting of the dignified Claudine he knew.
“Isn’t this something that you could talk over with Matthias?” he asked. “Duke Herhardt doesn’t know about it. And he must never find out about
it.” She sauntered back to where she had been sitting and sat down again. ‘Do you think that’s cowardly of me?” she asked, grinning at Riette. “Sure, rather than messing with my unassailable fiance. I’m focusing my efforts on his mistress. Is that just? Maybe not. But so what? Picking on a lowly opponent may be cowardly, but it’s not especially unfair, is it?”
“What?” he asked.
“Once Layla is gone, everything else will go back to the way it was. There’s no need to confront Duke Herhardt and risk causing bigger problems.”
“If Layla goes away, do you really expect that the powerful Duke Herhardt won’t be able to find her again?” he asked, sighing.

In the letter Layla had sent to Claudine, she had said that she would be leaving Matthias before the day of the wedding, and had begged Claudine to keep it a secret from him until then. She had said she was sorry for the unpardonable wrongs she had committed, and that even though she knew she could never make up for it, she was disappearing from their lives as a way of seeking atonement.
To Riette’s surprise, the letter made it seem like Layla wasn’t going to miss Matthias at all. Rather, she seemed desperate to get away from him by whatever means necessary. He knew, however, that if the affair had begun out of Matthias’s lust and obsession, then Layla wouldn’t be able to end it simply by leaving.
“After he and I get married,” Claudine said, “all I have to do is bear him a son who will be his heir. Other than that. I don’t particularly want much to do with him”
“Is that all the meaning that there is to your life? Just to become duchess and give birth lo the next Duke Herhardt? Once you’ve completed that task, is your life over?”
“It’s not the only meaning… I’d say it’s probably about half. But I never want to witness Duke Herhardt enamored with that lowly girl again.” Her eyes slowly narrowed. “I’d rather that she die. If she vanished from this world forever, my fiance would never again be reduced to the vulgar, disgusting man he becomes in her presence. He would be a perfect gentleman for the rest of his life-just what I want.
“Claudine!”
“I’m serious, Riette. Every day, I kill that girl in my mind. I realize that the whole thing is Matthias’s fault, not hers, but what can I do? He has to become

my husband. It would be dreadfully inconvenient if he were to die on me,” she said, sneering.
Riette’s eyes sunk with concern as he watched her. Duke Herhardt was the key to Claudine living the life she had always wanted for herself, so he couldn’t step between them. However, he could plainly see that she was no longer acting like herself.
He felt indignant. Why does he get you instead of me? Why does that man who makes you act like someone else, who doesn’t even love you, get to have you? He stood up. “As prestigious as the title of duchess may be, it’s not worth destroying yourself for, Claudine.” He started slowly walking towards her. “You should break off your engagement.”
“What…?” she replied, taken aback.
“Don’t sell your soul for the promise of honor,” he said, getting down on one knee in front of her.
She looked closely at his face to try to tell if he was joking, but he looked dead serious.
He had told her that she should leave Matthias and marry him countless times before, but had always acted like it was no more than a joke. Thus she had always laughed it off. It was an unwritten rule between the two of them that he would only ever make such pronouncements in jest. But now he was blatantly breaking the rule.
“If I break off my engagement with the duke, do you really think that you and I could just get married instead?” she asked derisively.
However, his will remained firm. “Nothing’s impossible, Claudine“
“Get a grip, Marquess Lindman. Think of what people will say! ‘Lady Claudine Brandt tried out one of her cousins before changing her mind and marrying the other.’ ‘Marquess Lindman betrayed his best friend by stealing

his fiancee.’ Don’t you see? Our reputations would be dragged through the mud, when it’s Matthias and Layla who are the disgraceful ones’”
He let out a long sigh and took her by the hand. “I don’t care.“
“My family would never accept it,” she said. “Neither would yours.” “I know.”
“If we chose to be together, we would be all but banished from the world. Do you really love me enough for it to be worth that?” Thick tears started to fall from her bloodshot eyes.
A faint grin appeared on his lips as he nodded. “Yes, I do.” He gently squeezed her hands. “I love you, Claudine. So please, marry me, and we can run away together.”




102.

A Completely Different Direction.






Starting early in the morning, the servants were busy packing up Katharina and Elise von Herhardt’s luggage.
Every year in early spring, the empire’s most prominent noblewomen gathered in the capital to participate in the empress’s birthday celebrations. It was the unofficial start of the social season, so all families that were invited took great pains to prepare for the festivities.
Hessen quietly approached Dowager and Lady Herhardt and said, “The carriage is ready for departure.”
Smiling elegantly, the two former duchesses rose from their seats and started walking. Their maids followed quietly behind. Meeting them at the top of the grand staircase that led to the mansion’s main hall, Matthias escorted his grandmother down the stairs.

“It would have been nice if you could have departed with us,” Katharina said, looking at her grandson.
Due to his packed schedule, Matthias wasn’t going to be able to make it to the capital until the day of the birthday party.
“I know you’re busy, but you mustn’t let business take up all your time and energy. Your social life matters, too. You know better than anyone how important it is for the head of the household to show his face at social events whenever possible,” she said in a slightly worried tone.
While it was true that the world was changing and aristocrats increasingly had to rely on business to make their living, this only made one’s position and reputation in social circles all the more important. While capitalists without aristocratic titles were mingling more and more with aristocrats, this had actually made the invisible wall between the two groups even higher and stronger.
“Yes, Grandmother,” Matthias replied, smiling politely.
While Elise and Katharina were relieved to see him acting like his usual self, some uneasiness remained in their eyes as they exchanged glances.
“See you in Ratse, Matthias,” Elise said, hugging her son briefly before climbing into the carriage.
Matthias maintained perfect decorum as he saw them off.
“Mr. Stauf will be coming to Arvis this afternoon, Your Grace,” Hessen quietly told Matthias as he followed him back into the main hall.
Matthias nodded to show that he was listening.
The Stauf family had managed the House of Herhardt’s Internal legal Issues for generations.
“Mr. Stauf also said that if you would like more detailed advice about the matter, you may want to bring the company’s legal counsel into the discussion

as well.
“I’ll decide about that after today’s meeting.”
“Yes, Your Grace. I’ll relay that to Mr. Stauf.” Hessen then walked off towards the hallway.
Matthias stopped in the middle of the large, marble-floored hall and slowly raised his head. His blue eyes looked coldly at the large chandelier, and at the Herhardt family crest imprinted on the ceiling.
Lowering his gaze, he continued walking across the hall with a more relaxed gait.
“Elise, do you really think that’s what’s going on?”
Elise and Katharina’s eyes filled with worry as soon as the carriage had left the estate. Looking tired, Elise removed her gloves. “It’s hard to believe that Matthias of all people would do that, but we mustn’t forget that he’s a man. On the face of it, it’s hard to understand the kindness he’s showing to Bill Remmer. But with Layla Llewellyn added to the picture, it all makes sense. Don’t you agree?”
Katharina looked out the carriage window, neither agreeing nor disagreeing. She had been a highly capable and influential duchess in her day, as had her daughter-in-law. She knew it wasn’t worth raising a fuss over things like this.
“Why not just ask him about it after he arrives in Ratse?” she asked Elise.
“I thought about that, but I don’t think it’s a good idea,’ Elise replied. “Matthias seemed like he was waiting for us to ask him. Surely you know what it would mean if he has made up his mind that way.”
Dowager Herhardt understood her daughter-in-law’s point at once. If their surmise proved true, the situation was a thorny one.

There was clearly something going on between Matthias and the gardener’s adopted daughter. They had already suspected that this was the case on the afternoon when Matthias had informed them of his unexpected decision to relocate the gardener and his daughter to Ratse. Now it was no longer a matter of merely suspecting. Matthias was being open about their relationship, while his mother and grandmother were assiduously feigning ignorance.
“If we’re right about Matthias, what can we do to protect the family?” Elise asked.
“I think the best thing to do is to continue to feign ignorance, Elise,” Katharina replied, whispering even though she knew that no one was listening in on their conversation.
Matthias and Claudine’s wedding was right around the corner. Ensuring that the ceremony was not marred by any incidents or scandals was of prime importance.
“Don’t you think that’s for the best, though? The fact that Matthias wants to send Layla Llewellyn to the capital shows that he has no intention of letting his marriage be ruined.”
“Sure, I suppose the most important thing right now is making sure that the wedding goes off without a hitch’
Elise felt like sighing, but she stopped herself and just nodded. If it were up to her, she would question Matthias. Knowing how much time and effort both families had put into the preparations for the wedding, she would see to it that Layla was removed from the situation before she could get in the way of the wedding. However, she knew that trying to do this would only make things worse. Thus, the best way to avoid ruining the wedding seemed to be simply to ignore Layla.

“I’m a little afraid, though,” she muttered, rubbing her temples. “Matthias seems like he’s trying to get caught. What if by some chance this leads to his engagement getting broken off, and then he declares his intention to, God forbid, marry Layla Llewellyn?”
Katharina, who was as proud as could be of her grandson, frowned at her daughter-in-law’s words. “Do you really think our Matthias would do such a thing?”
“I’m worried precisely because it’s Matthias,” Elise replied. The veins in her pale hands were starting to bulge.
Matthias had been an extremely well-behaved child. He had always fulfilled his duties perfectly and had never talked back. However, Elise had never felt that her son acted this way out of obedience to her or anyone else.
He had behaved himself out of a desire to become the perfect successor to the House of Herhardt. If his desire were to shift, then he was liable to stop acting like a perfect successor at the drop of a hat. He always behaved with flawless decorum, but at the same time, he was arrogant and firm-willed. She knew he wouldn’t show obedience to anyone if doing so meant going against his own desire.
“But I suppose you’re right,” she said, trying to suppress the ominous feeling inside her, “he probably wouldn’t do such a thing. It’s Matthias, after all.”







After she finished grading her students’ quizzes, Layla rubbed her stiff shoulders and stood up from her bedroom desk.
It was already well past nine o’clock. Earlier in the evening, Phoebe had returned to the cottage without a note. Fortunately, it seemed that the duke would not be summoning her tonight.
Letting out a long sigh of relief, she opened the door off her closet. Just then, there was a tapping sound at her window. She knew that Phoebe was inside her cage. She had checked that the door was securely locked several times.
As she was wondering if she had imagined it, she heard more tapping, louder than the first time. It definitely wasn’t Phoebe. There was no way that pigeon’s tiny beak could make such a loud sound. What could be causing the sound, then…?
Half in doubt, she opened the curtains. She was relieved not to seethe duke there, but her suspicion only increased. If it wasn’t him, who could it have been?
After gazing at the darkness outside the window for a moment, she cautiously opened it. Just as she was about to conclude that she had just been hearing things, she noticed a long shadow on the ground outside the window.
She followed the shadow with her eyes until she let out a yelp when saw its source. “Ah…!” If she hadn’t quickly put her hands over her mouth, she might have screamed loudly enough to wake Uncle Bill.

“Good evening, Layla.” the duke said, chuckling quietly as he leaned languidly against the wall next to the window.
“Wh-what are you doing here, Your Grace?” “Good question. Why do you think?”
“Go away! Right now!” She looked uneasily back and forth between the closed door of her room and the man standing outside the window. Uncle Bill was usually asleep by this hour, but that didn’t bring her much relief. “Please hurry up and leave. I hate it when you-”
Before she could finish, he shut her up by putting his lips to hers. He proceeded to give her a long, slow kiss as he firmly held her face against his. She felt even more anxious as she heard him starting to laugh.
Finally letting her go, he said, “Come outside.”
Her brow slowly furrowed as she stared at his wet lips. “No!” “Then I’ll come inside.”
“Why are you doing this?” she asked, reaching out to grab his arm in order to stop him from walking around to the cottage’s front door. “Please, just go! I can’t leave home right now.”
“Why not?”
“Uncle Bill might still be awake.”
“So what?” He glanced at her face, then at the windowsill, then at her face again.
When she understood what he was getting at, she sighed in frustration. “You want me to climb out the window? Right now? Why should I?” “Because I want you to,” he replied, flashing a warm but arrogant smile at
her.
She pulled down her sleeves and rubbed the saliva from her lips. She knew

that this wild man was not going to give up. Causing a ruckus would only

make things more dangerous for her.
After glaring at him, she hurriedly locked the door to her room and returned to the window. “It’s not very gentlemanly of you to ask a lady to climb out a window.”
“I wouldn’t expect such a reproach to come from a lady who’s as good at climbing trees as you.”
“I haven’t climbed trees in a long time!” With that, she deftly climbed up onto the windowsill.
He approached the window to help her down, but she didn’t need any help.
She nimbly climbed down in an instant.
“Seems like you haven’t lost the hang of it,” he muttered, chuckling. He then closed the window and grabbed her hand.
The two of them passed through the backyard towards the forest path. Once they had gone far enough to be out of sight of the cottage, she breathed a sigh of relief.
After a moment, she realized that they weren’t on the path that led to his cabin. “Um… we’re going the wrong way, aren’t we?” she asked.
“This is the way,” he casually replied. He was taking her in a completely different direction.
The moonlight peeking through the branches shone on their hands as he Intertwined his fingers with hers.
“I have something to show you,” he whispered. His voice was as soft and gentle as the early- spring night.




103.

Defection.






After standing nearby for a while, waiting for their chance, two young ladies hurried up to Claudine. “Hello, Lady Claudine,” one of them said.
“We met a long time ago at a party at the Herhardt mansion. Do you remember?”
The other lady shook her head and said, “It was such a brief meeting, she probably doesn’t remember us. Let’s introduce ourselves. My name is-”
“Of course I remember you. Lady Escher,’ Claudine said, gently cutting the young lady off.
Escher started blushing immediately.
“And I remember you as well, Lady Diane.”
The other lady, the shorter of the two, started blushing as well.
The sisters were practically jumping up and down with excitement. Their mother, a viscountess, was frowning at them from a few paces away, but this

didn’t stop the immature girls.
They showered Claudine with an endless stream of praise and told her how much they were looking forward to her wedding.
Claudine had already chatted with dozens of other young ladies who had said much the same things to her, but she still reacted as if they were saying the most fascinating things in the world to her. This was one of the rules of etiquette that she had learned early in life, and by now it was second nature to her. She had also learned to memorize the name of everyone she met at social events.
After chatting with Escher and Diane, she met countless more noblewomen and young ladies desperate to form an acquaintance with her.
The party proceeded in this manner. There was the usual conversation and laughter, with beautiful music playing in the background, and a lovely floral scent in the air. It was a perfect soiree.
After chatting with every other important noblewoman, Claudine concluded her rounds by exchanging pleasantries with the crown princess. After that, she went to the lounge and sat on the sofa by the window for some well-earned rest.
Countess Brandt, who had been watching her daughter from afar, walked up to her, smiling brightly. “Since the duke’s mother and grandmother couldn’t attend, you’re the social queen of the empire tonight, Claudine.”
“Mother…” Claudine said, frowning at her mother.
Countess Brandt wasn’t going to stop, however. “Hey, It’s true!” she said, her eyes full of pride In her beloved daughter.
While a family’s power was the husband’s domain, it fell to the lady of the house to maintain a dignified reputation commensurate with that power. No matter how high-ranking or wealthy a family was. if the lady of the house

wasn’t well-respected in high society, the family would never rise above a middling level of prestige.
This was how the House of Herhardt had established itself at the top of aristocratic society. While the family’s long, honorable history had given it the authority to dominate the aristocracy, each generation’s Duchess Herhardt had been treated like the queen of high society. No one could deny the roles these duchesses had played in maintaining the internal stability of the family.
Thinking proudly about how her own daughter was soon going to take on this mantle. Countess Brandt eventually returned to the group of noblewomen she had been mingling with. Feeling refreshed after a bit of rest. Claudine soon also returned to the party to continue fulfilling her role.
The baroness who was serving as the party’s host approached her with a warm smile on her face. “It’s a shame that the duke’s mother couldn’t attend, since she is still the official duchess, but I can’t tell you how delighted I am to see you, the future duchess, in attendance.”
“They just arrived in Ratse this evening, so they were sadly unable to attend. They were so disappointed!” Claudine replied, shaking her head.
The baroness smiled even more brightly at this response.
Claudine and Matthias’s upcoming wedding would serve not only as the beginning of their marriage, but also as her coronation as Duchess Claudine von Herhardt. In preparation for that day, she was already behaving with utmost elegance and dignity.
“No,”she had replied to Riette on that sunny afternoon, pulling her hand away from his.
Tears had continued to fall from her eyes, but the expression on her face had remained cold and determined.

“We couldn’t survive if we left the world of high society. You know that; Riette,” she had said, smiling bitterly.
Riette truly loved her. She knew this better than anyone. More than anything, this was why she couldn’t hold his hand-she knew she couldn’t stand seeing his heart break.
She had been born and raised in the lavish, ornate world of aristocratic high society. This world was her whole life, and she loved it. Riette was no different.
The two of them were like delicate flowers that needed to be carefully cultivated in a greenhouse. If they left the greenhouse of high society, would they be able to survive merely on their love for one another?
She already knew the answer to this question. As she reflected on it, the anguish she was feeling settled down deep in her heart. To hide the resentment and disillusionment she was feeling, she smiled even more brightly.
Meanwhile, she once again imagined killing Layla, so that the most perfect person in the aristocratic world, Duke Matthias von Herhardt, would return to his old ways.







Layla finally started to catch her breath after she heard Matthias firmly shut the door to the master suite. From the moment she had entered the mansion,

her legs had been so shaky with nervousness, she had almost fallen over several limes. Luckily, the duke had caught her each time.
“Don’t laugh,” she said, glaring at the duke as he giggled like a mischievous boy. “Why are you laughing?’ Her voice had started to shake.
He set her down on the sofa, then walked at a leisurely pace over to the window that looked out onto the garden. He opened the curtains and the window, and a cool breeze blew into the room.
Having gotten her bearings, she looked around the room with fear and curiosity. Every room in the mansion was large, high-ceilinged, and lavishly decorated, but this was especially the case in the duke’s master suite. Looking around, she felt dizzy and overwhelmed.
When they had started down the unfamiliar path in the forest, she couldn’t have imagined that he was taking her to the mansion. The reality hadn’t sunken in for her until the moment they stepped into the main hall.
She couldn’t believe that someone in their right mind would do such a thing. She had soon remembered that the duke was not at all in his right mind.
“If you keep struggling like that, servants are going to come rushing in to see what the noise is about “ he had said. “Not that I care.”
She could tell that he was telling the truth, so she had stopped resisting and let him lead her further, fearing what would happen if she had been caught in this state by the servants.
Her memories after that were hazy. All she could remember clearly was that her whole body was trembling with anxiety, and her heart felt like it was going to explode. Fortunately, it was late at night, and the mansion was still, allowing them to reach the master suite without encountering a single servant. This was her first time entering the mansion through the main entrance and ascending the central staircase since the summer night two years earlier

when she had attended the soiree that Claudine had invited her to. The atmosphere was completely different from that of the staff entrance in the back of the mansion that she was used to.
After fretting nervously for a while, she finally spoke. “So, what in the world is it that you wanted to show me here?’
Sitting on the windowsill, Matthias turned his head towards her. His movements were as slow as the smoke rising from his cigarette. “Are you feeling more relaxed now?”
“No. But I’d like you to show me whatever it is you have to show me as soon as possible.”
“Why?”
“Well, I don’t think I’m ever going to feel relaxed around you, so you might as well show me the thing, so I can leave.”
He responded to her earnest plea by bursting into laughter. She had been wondering if her plan to seduce him had been a mistake, but it now seemed like things weren’t going as badly as they might have been.
Wanting to appear less foolish, she held her head high and caught her breath. However, she had been clutching her skirt so tightly, it was now thoroughly wrinkled and looked ridiculous.
Matthias took a drag of his cigarette, then walked over to her and held out his hand. After staring at his hand for an extended moment, she hesitantly wrapped her hand around the tip of his index finger. Based on the innocent look on his face, she didn’t think that he was up to any tricks.
He then gently took her hand and made her hold onto his arm. As she watched this in a daze, her brow slowly furrowed and she said, “Th-this is weird.”
“What is?”

“Holding onto your arm. It feels a little, you know…”
She quickly let go of his arm, but he wasn’t going to give up that easily. “Our naked bodies have intermingled countless times. Why are you getting
so worked up over linking arms?” he asked. “H-how can you say such a thing?”
She started blushing with embarrassment, but he didn’t blink.
“If you don’t want to hear me say that kind of thing, then take my arm.”
To stop him from saying anything more, she grudgingly followed his command. He then started walking.
