Layla, you came into my world just like a flower coming into bloom, he thought.
A faint smile crept onto his face.
It looked like Layla hadn’t left for work yet. Perhaps he would be able to catch a brief, distant glimpse of her.
Before leaving his bedroom, he walked over to the birdcage. His canary was sitting on its perch, bobbing its head. He tapped gently on the one of the bars of the cage with his finger, and the bird chirped in response Just like every other day, his bird was happy in its cozy cage
After that short greeting, he left his bedroom. Just as it was occurring to him that he had forgotten to ask Layla if she had decided on a birthday present, he reached the mansion’s front door.
“Huh? Mr. Remmer?” his assistant said, stepping forward towards the gardener, who was standing in the driveway, gripping a dirty shovel.
Matthias, who had been about to get in the car, turned his head. “What’s the matter?”

Hessen, the butler, tried to intervene, but Bill kept staring straight at the duke. Bill had always been a little rough around the edges, but he had never displayed this level of blatant rudeness. The other staff members who knew him well exchanged concerned glances.
“Hey, Bill.”
Bill gulped. Trying to stay calm, he said, “N-never mind…”
The knuckles of the hand with which he was gripping the shovel were white. As he thought back to Layla down on her knees, begging him not to do anything to the duke, he was finally able to take a step back.
Gritting his teeth, he bowed subserviently to the man he wanted to kill. The duke nodded and got into the car. He looked just like the perfect gentleman Bill had always known, the Duke Herhardt that everyone admired.
After the car drove off, Hessen approached Bill with a stem expression on his face. “Bill, this isn’t like you. What on earth is going on?”
Bill took a deep breath to stifle the urge he was feeling to swing the shovel at Hessen. As the butler, Hessen knew everything that went on in the House of Herhardt. There was no way he was unaware of what was going on between the duke and Layla. He had probably helped the duke get to her, thinking that it didn’t matter what sorts of horrendous things he did, as long as they were for his master…
However, Bill once again mustered all his self-control to suppress his anger, and simply replied, “It’s nothing.”
Everything felt meaningless to him now. His coworkers, who had been like family to him for decades; Arvis’s garden, which he had grown old tending to; he was fed up with all of it.
“I… I just wanted to bid His Grace farewell. What’s the matter with that?”

After giving this uncharacteristically wordy response, he hurried back to the garden. When he reached the flowerbed he had been working on when little Layla had first arrived in the mail coach, intense anger and despair surged up inside of him.
He flung the shovel to the ground and left the garden. Startled by his unusual behavior, his helpers grew concerned, but none of them dared ask him what was wrong.
When he reached the cottage’s front yard, the mailman walked up to him, smiling. “Oh, Mr. Remmer! You’re here.”
With trembling hands, Bill took the telegram that the mailman was holding out to him. It was a response to a telegram he had sent a few days earlier. Once the mailman left, he hurriedly opened the envelope and read the telegram. After a brief feeling of relief, his eyes grew stony with renewed determination.
He was going to protect Layla. All other thoughts faded away from his mind, leaving only that singular resolution. He was willing to lose everything else for the sake of protecting her. There was nothing in his life as precious or important to him as that girl.







“Miss Llewellyn, are you feeling sick?” Monica asked, looking worriedly at her princess teacher.
“Miss Llewellyn…?!” As her teacher continued not to respond, Monica’s eyes started to fill with tears.

Layla kept staring blankly at her desk until Monica reached out and shook her arm. “Oh… I’m sorry, MonicaRubbing her bloodshot eyes, she quickly picked up her glasses and put them back on. ‘Why haven’t you gone home yet, dear?”
Seeing her teacher smiling again, Monica was relieved. Her princess teacher was back. ‘Because you looked like you were sick. Don’t die, Miss Llewellyn,” she said, bursting into tears.
Seeing Monica in this state snapped Layla out of her daze.
“That’s what happened to my mommy,” Monica said. “She looked sick like you, and then she went up to heaven.”
“Don’t worry, Monica. I’m not sick. I’m fine. I promise. Okay?“ Layla said, quickly rising from her seat and embracing the crying girl.
I couldn’t even hide my feelings from a child, she thought. How pathetic.
Once Monica had finished crying, she said “Here, I made this for you.” She held out a bouquet of wildflowers she had gathered. They were already wilted, but the little yellow flowers warmed Layla’s heart.
“Thank you, Monica.” “Don’t get sick, okay?’ “Okay, I won’t.”
“You promise?”
“I promise,” Layla replied, nodding her head.
Monica smiled. Layla took out her handkerchief and wiped away Monica’s tears, then tidied her messy hair.
Monica then left, leaving the classroom in silence, the way it usually was in the late afternoon. Layla sat at her desk for a while longer, then grabbed her bag and stood up.

That morning, after several days of silence, Uncle Bill had finally spoken, saying, ’Let’s go, Layla.”
She had been about to leave for work when he had walked up to her with a determined look on his face and continued, saying, I won’t let him have you for another day. Let’s go somewhere far away, where the duke can never find you.” As he said this, his eyes had been filled with cold rage.
Layla had found his proposal daunting, but she had immediately nodded. She had already been planning to leave, anyway. She had never intended for Uncle Bill to have to suffer the misfortune of finding out about everything, but there was no way to undo that now that it had happened.
Now she only had one remaining wish, and that was to leave the duke. She could go anywhere in the world, as long as he wasn’t there. She wanted to completely remove him from her life, so that she and Uncle Bill could live an ordinary life together again.
Rubbing her bloodshot eyes, she quickly climbed onto her bicycle. As she left the city and turned onto the road leading to Arvis, she remembered the afternoon when she had ridden in the mail coach to meet Uncle Bill.
With her arms wrapped around her worn-out suitcase, she had practiced her smile and earnestly prayed that Uncle Bill would like her. She had promised herself that if he did, she would be a good girl.
She had tried so hard to keep that promise, but in the end she had broken it. She had committed an unforgivable wrong, and broken Uncle Bill’s heart.
I never should’ve come here, she thought. Instead of crossing the border from Lovita to Berg, I should’ve gone to an orphanage. Then I never would’ve met the duke, and I wouldn’t have had to endure ah this pain. More importantly, Kyle and Uncle Bill wouldn’t have had their hearts broken.

However, she realized with self-disgust that even if she were able to do it all over again, she still would have held out hope and gone to see Uncle Bill.
Just then, a car turned around the bend in the road. It was the duke’s car.




110.

Forever and Ever.






At the duke’s direction, the chauffeur pulled the car over on the side of Platanus Road. The deep green leaves were rustling in the evening
breeze.
Matthias got out of the car and sauntered across the shadowy road towards his Layla, who was standing in the shade of a tree.
The two of them looked at each other for a while, saying nothing. The only sound was that of the leaves in the trees.
Glancing at his watch, Matthias finally broke the silence, asking, “Have you made up your mind yet?”
His departure had been delayed because of a lunch meeting. He couldn’t miss the evening train, or he wouldn’t make it to Ratse in time for the empress’s birthday party.
“About the birthday present you want, I mean,” he added.

“Oh, that,” she replied. As she stood there, gripping her bicycle’s handlebars tightly, she looked even smaller and more youthful than usual. She gazed at him, saying nothing more.
He enjoyed watching her silently gather her thoughts. Even more, he enjoyed when she finally thought of something to say, and he got to hear her soft, pleasing voice.
He liked so much about her. He liked the way she thought and the way she spoke. He liked the shadows that her long eyelashes cast on her face. He liked her smooth, round cheeks, and her pretty, smiling lips. He liked the tough, defiant attitude she sometimes took with him, and the vibrant look in her eyes when she spoke her mind. He liked her rosy complexion and quick breathing, and the way she no longer trembled in fright when she saw him. He liked the way her smooth, petite body hung onto his, as if the two of them were the only people in the world. All these things gave him great pleasure.
“I’ve made up my mind,” she finally said, smiling. Only then did the sickly pallor leave her face, to be replaced by a healthy glow. “Should I tell you?”
“Yes, tell me what it is.”
“If I tell you, will you give it to me?” she asked in a pestering tone. The cold look in his eyes softened. “Sure,” he replied, smiling. “I will.”
The smile quickly faded from his lips, but it left a trace of warmth behind.
She gazed at him with an expression as calm and peaceful as the spring breeze.
She knew that ha was too sat in his ways to immediately break down over the loss of a mere mistress. Most likely, he would experience some brief anger, and then continue on with his happy life and become Claudine’s husband.
Since she knew that losing his mistress wouldn’t have much of an effect in the short term, she prayed to God that his yearning for her would become a

long-term illness.
To that end, she slowly opened her lips and said, “I want you to love me.” Her hair fluttered in the cool breeze.
“I would like it so much if you loved me,” she said, smiling brightly at him.
She hoped that this request would lodge itself deep inside him, and start to fester there, like a wound that wouldn’t heal. She hoped that he would go along with his life, thinking that he was doing fine, until one day the wound would be too much for him, and he would finally collapse.
Then, perhaps, he would finally cry over her, just like she had cried so many times because of him.
The hard-to-read expression on his face was gently illuminated by the golden light of the evening sun. She couldn’t believe it, but standing there with him was making her tear up. To hide this, she made herself smiling even more brightly. This was easy to do when she thought about how he had ruined her life without remorse, and she thought about Kyle’s tears, or Uncle Bill’s sobs.
“I want you to love me deeply. Forever and ever,” she sweetly whispered. Goodbye; she thought. Goodbye forever.







Standing next to the open rear door of the car, Mark Evers looked nervously at his watch. The servants standing behind him looked just as worried.
The train’s departure time was fast approaching, but the duke was showing no sign of being ready to leave. The contours of his face stood out in the darkening evening sunlight.

Finally, deciding that there was no time left to delay, Mark politely said, “Your Grace?”
Matthias then finally turned his head towards the car. He looked more or less like he always did, with a calm expression on his face, but there was a subtle difference. He looked like someone who had lost something important but didn’t know it yet.
“The train will be departing soon, Your Grace. We need to leave at once,” Mark said.
“Of course,” Matthias quietly replied. “Let’s go.” He walked in his usual, restrained manner back towards the car and got in. His servants got in after him.
The train station was crowded, but fortunately, no one blocked the duke’s path as he walked towards the platform. As a result, the train to Ratse was still there when he arrived at the platform.
He quickly stepped back to allow the crowd of other passengers to rush into the train first. He was one of the last passengers to board. Shortly after he did, the train started huffing and puffing and slowly began to move.
Sitting in his seat, he squinted as he watched the scenery flowing past outside the window. He reminded himself that going to the capital was an ordinary part of his life as an aristocrat and a businessman. And this was only going to be a short, week-long trip. There was no reason for him to be feeling as anxious about it as he was.
He stopped looking out the window and leaned back into his seat. As soon as he did, Layla’s bright-green eyes flashed into his mind. They were the same color as the new spring leaves on the trees.
He couldn’t believe that she had asked him to love her. He had not expected that this would be her birthday present request. Perhaps that was

why he was gripped by this strange feeling that he couldn’t get rid of. He hadn’t known what to say in response.
He had just stared at her after she made her request. He hadn’t wanted to stay silent, but hearing her utter the word love out of the blue had left him at a loss for words.
For a moment, they had been immersed in a sweet silence. It had felt like the rest of the world had melted away. That moment had quickly passed, however. His assistant, not daring to approach them, had cleared his throat a few times, to indicate that they urgently had to leave. Finally, Matthias had taken the hint.
Before turning away from her, however, he had taken one last look into her beautiful, green eyes, and let out a bitter chuckle. For all he knew, she was trying to manipulate him, but he didn’t care.
He felt like the world was caving in underneath him, but he wasn’t falling. It was as if she had wings. Even as she was destroying his life, he felt like she was saving him.
When he opened his eyes again, he saw her face superimposed over the dark sky.
When he had started to walk back towards the car, she hadn’t tried to stop him. She had remained standing where she was, watching him walk away. She had remained standing there long after the car had started driving away.
She had grown smaller and smaller in the distance, until she was no longer visible. Even then, however, he had looked over his shoulder several more times, clenching his fists and gulping nervously each time. There was a feeling of irritation deep in his chest that grew more and more intense the farther away he was from her.

Sitting in the train, he glanced again over his shoulder at the scenery growing distant behind them, trying to comfort himself with the thought that she was somewhere back there.







These moments continued after he arrived in Ratse. Even as he was faithfully carrying out his duties as Duke Herhardt, he kept glancing over his shoulder from time to time. He did this in the imperial palace’s magnificent banquet hall. He did it as his car drove past the natural history museum in downtown Ratse. He did it each time he passed by a petite young woman with blonde hair. Each time this happened, the image of Layla’s face at their last meeting grew clearer in his memory. The brightness of her smile made him anxious. It pained him to think that this radiant young woman would soon be confined to
the shadows.
Out of habit, he rose from his seat, walked over to the window, and opened the curtains. Only when he saw the scenery outside the window did he remember that he wasn’t in Arvis. He gazed down at the city that would soon be Layla’s new home.
She had claimed that everything was easy for him. He had readily agreed with this. He had an easy life. When he wanted something, he took it. When he attempted something, he achieved it. All without worrying what anyone else thought.
This utterly simple, straightforward life of his had been infiltrated by emotions that were anything but simple. Thinking back on it, this seemed to

have begun the previous summer, on that sunny afternoon when Layla had fallen from her bicycle on Platanus Road.
He felt like he could still hear the sound of the overturned bicycle’s wheels spinning in the air, along with the rustling of the leaves, and the pounding of his heart.
Yes, everything in his life was easy. Everything but her.
He let out a deep sigh as he leaned against the window frame.
The Brandt family was going to be visiting the Herhardt Ratse mansion today, to discuss the final preparations for the wedding.
He was going to be married very soon. That had nothing to do with Layla. It couldn’t have anything to do with her. Thus he needed to stop looking over his shoulder. He needed to keep walking forwards towards the future, towards the life he was supposed to lead.
But was such a life really possible?
Just as he was flooded with a sudden feeling of intense doubt, he heard a knock at his door. It was a maid informing him that the Brandt family had arrived early.







Wearing a newly made spring dress, Claudine looked as pretty and vibrant as a just-bloomed flower. The duke’s mother and grandmother looked at her with unabashed satisfaction.
At the party at the imperial palace the previous night, Claudine had received more attention than any other lady in attendance.

In aristocratic social circles, expectations were extremely high for the next Duchess Herhardt, but Claudine had more than enough charm and beauty to meet those expectations. Even the haughty empress acknowledged her merits, despite the fact that Claudine had won out over the empress’s own daughter for the role of Duke Herhardt’s wife All that remained now was the wedding for which the Herhardt and Brandt families had been preparing for so long. Then Claudine would be a Herhardt, and she would pass that honor onto the next generation.
“Oh, Duke Herhardt, you’re here!’ Claudine exclaimed as soon as she saw Matthias enter the drawing room.
Matthias bowed politely, then walked towards her with unhurried steps.
He looked like his usual self, but Claudine felt like something was somehow awry. She had felt this way the previous night as well. Even as she stood next to her flawless fiance, he hadn’t seemed to really see her. Not even when they were making eye contact.
After greeting everyone, Matthias suddenly asked Claudine, “Would you care to go for a stroll with me in the garden?”
Not only Claudine but everyone present was surprised to hear this unprecedented proposal.
“A stroll…? Just the two of us?” she asked.
“Yes, my lady. I have something to talk to you about.’
In that moment, she finally felt like he was seeing her. However, she didn’t like it one bit. She had an ominous feeling that she wasn’t going to like hearing whatever it was he wanted to talk to her about. She sensed that it was something he would be better off saving at least until the end of the summer to bring up.

Just as she was about to turn down his offer, a servant came rushing into the drawing room.
“Y-your Grace!”
It was the duke’s assistant.
“What is the meaning of this. Evers? We have guests,” Elise von Herhardt said, frowning.
The duke’s assistant didn’t back down, however. It was clear that he had urgent news.
“What’s the matter?” Matthias asked, sensing that something out of the ordinary was afoot.
All eyes were on the assistant. After gulping nervously, he replied, “An urgent message has just arrived from Arvis.”




111.

Such a Trivial Matter.






“Amessage? What’s the message?” “Well…”
“Spit it out, Evers,” the duke said, frowning.
Mark Evers gulped nervously and wiped the sweat from his brow, then stammered, Th-the gardener and his adopted daughter have d-disappeared.”
Without meaning to, Claudine clasped her hands together in excitement.
Luckily for her, no one noticed this.
“Apparently, they left Arvis in the middle of the night, as if they were running away,” Mark added.
“Bill Remmer ran away?! I can’t believe it…” Dowager Herhardt exclaimed.
Lady Herhardt and Count and Countess Brandt looked just as surprised by the completely unexpected news.

“Goodness, Layla ran away? Why would she do that?” Claudine asked, forcing a look of surprise as she turned towards Matthias.
I guess the gardener received my fetter, she thought. She had to draw on all her years of aristocratic deportment training to stop herself from bursting into delighted laughter.
“You’ll probably want to deal with this situation right away, so perhaps we should put off discussing the wedding until next time,” she said, looking with pity at her fiance. She felt like praising Layla for her irritating self-pride.
Look at you, Duke Herhardt, she thought, noticing that Matthias wasn’t saying anything. What’s the matter, cal got your tongue? She gazed at him with partly sincere sympathy.
Unlike his mother and grandmother, who were visibly flustered, his face remained cold and still. Claudine could tell, however, that something was different inside of him.
“I’m sorry,” he finally said, rising from his seat. Even in this urgent moment, he stood with perfect posture. “I will have to attend to the situation at the Arvis estate, so I won’t be able to join you for lunch today.”
Although he said this in a flat voice devoid of emotion, his anxiousness was visible in the way he was fiddling with his jacket button.
As soon as Claudine noticed this, she decided to forgive Layla for everything.
Her fiance was finally experiencing the hurt of losing someone he loved, just like she was with Riette. It was a highly satisfying conclusion.







The train crossed the border in the dead of night. It was too dark to see much of the scenery out the window, but Layla was able to picture it all. She still remembered traveling in the other direction on this same route all those years ago, when she had left her hometown and ventured to a foreign country, all alone.
She held her breath as she gazed out the window, just as she had done as a little girl. Her reflection in the dark window melded in her mind into the image of herself as a child, which made her laugh bitterly. That little girl’s eyes had been twinkling with trepidation and excitement as she crossed the border into the Berg Empire. Now she was a young woman with eyes full of fatigue and a heart full of pain as she crossed back into Lovita.
“Look at me instead, kiddo,“ Uncle Bill said.
Having thought that he was asleep, she jumped at the sound of his voice and turned to look at him.
“We still have a long way to go. I guess you probably know that, though. It’s your home country, after all.” His face looked exhausted, but he was smiling warmly.
She smiled back at him. She felt grateful for the sound of the train chugging along, and for the sound of the various people snoring around them in the compartment. If it hadn’t been for the noise, she might have found the silence unbearable. She wrapped the blanket Uncle Bill had given her around herself and closed her eyes.

When he had proposed that they go to Lovita, she hadn’t believed it at first. He demonstrated how serious he was, however, by getting packed up and ready to leave in the blink of an eye. Since they were trying to run away without attracting any attention, they had to leave most of their belongings behind. Along with the money he had saved up, he filled one large trunk with clothes and supplies, and that was it.
He told her that he had a distant relative who lived in a city on the southern coast of Lovita. They weren’t very close, but Uncle Bill had sent a telegram to him, and the relative had responded, saying that he would help them get settled down once they arrived.
Unable to fall asleep, Layla opened her eyes and turned to her uncle. “I’m sorry, Uncle Bill. If I hadn’t come to live with you…”
“Stop, Layla. I won’t hear another word of that kind of talk,” he replied, looking sternly back at her. “There is nothing in my life more precious than you, kiddo. The day you arrived at Arvis in that mail coach was the day my life began. I’m serious.” He patted her head and smiled.
He was telling the truth. Before Layla, his life had been tranquil but monotonous, and it probably still would be, if he hadn’t started taking care of her. However, he had no desire to go back to that way of life. How could he? It would be like going back to a black-and-white world after experiencing a world full of bright, vibrant colors.
“We’ll be able to make a good life for ourselves wherever we are, just like we have already,” he said.
The two of them gazed at each other for a long time as they sat in the rattling third-class compartment. Smiles spread across each of their faces, while their eyes also started to well op with tears. It was just like the first time they had sat across from each other at the kitchen table, all those years ago.

Yes, it’s going to be all right she muttered to herself.
Eventually, night gave way to dawn, and the sky started to brighten. By this point, Uncle Bill was fast asleep, snoring loudly.
She took the blanket he had given her, and placed it on him, then looked out the window again. The train was passing by a huge field filled with spring flowers in full bloom. Not wanting to look back, she closed her eyes.







It was a nighttime getaway. There was no other way to describe the gardener and his adopted daughter’s disappearance.
“Did you try the school?” Hessen asked Mark Evers when he appeared in Hessen’s office.
Mark nodded regretfully. “Yes, I met with the principal. He was distraught to hear that Miss Llewellyn had disappeared in that manner, but he didn’t have any new information for us.”
“I see. How is the search for family members going?”
“The last remaining member of Mr. Remmer’s immediate family was his older brother, who passed away two years ago. We have no information on other relatives of his.”
“Goodness, this isn’t going well…” Hessen lamented, rubbing his forehead.
The duke had returned to Arvis immediately after hearing about the gardener’s disappearance. In a calm voice he had given Hessen a concise command: “Find them.” The coldness with which he had said this made a chill run down Hessen’s spine. He would have preferred it if the duke had yelled at

him or interrogated him. His deceptively simple command was much harder to deal with.
Other than his speedy return to Arvis, there was nothing in the duke’s behavior that suggested he was especially concerned about the situation. For days after his return, he seemed completely relaxed. Observing this, Hessen had to wonder if he had been too hasty in concluding that the duke’s feelings for Layla went beyond mere lust.
“He hasn’t said anything about them since issuing the command to find them,” Mark said. “Maybe he’s given up? If not, I don’t know why-”
Before Mark could finish his sentence, the service bell in Hessen’s office rang. It was coming from the third-floor master suite—the duke’s bedroom.







Matthias had an ordinary day. He woke up early and read the newspaper while eating a simple breakfast. He listened to a report sent to him from his company’s board of directors, informing him of their concerns- about the uneasy international situation on the continent. In the afternoon, he participated in a meeting with these same directors. Among those on the board of directors, some were very worried about the worsening international conflicts, while others were not.
As Matthias listened to these two factions argue at length, he looked like an audience member watching a long, boring play. He felt that each side had a point. After reflecting for a moment, he decided that he really didn’t care who was right. Realizing that this was quite an irresponsible position for the head

of the company to hold, he laughed aloud at himself. He could sense the uncomfortable glances of the directors at the conference table, but it wasn’t easy to stop laughing.
When he finally regained his composure, the conference room had gone silent. “Please excuse me,” he said, straightening his posture. A friendly smile returned to his lips.
Everything went back to normal after that. Matthias skillfully synthesized the views of both sides and arrived at a compromise. Fleshing out the details would be the job of the specialists working under him. The company had originated in his family, but as it expanded over the years and generations, the management had been systematically subdivided. Matthias’s role was to listen to the opinions of the directors and make top-level decisions. His training and education had prepared him for this duty, and he fulfilled it without difficulty.
After the meeting, he went to a dinner party he had been invited to. Surrounded by familiar faces, he had a pleasant evening just like countless other pleasant evenings. He dined, laughed, and conversed in the same refined manner as always. He himself was surprised how peaceful the day had been.
“Excuse me, Your Grace,” Hessen finally said, having waited quite a while before breaking the silence.
Only then did Matthias realize that his butler had been standing next to him.
Hessen reported that no progress had been made in the search. Neither the gardener nor his adopted daughter had any known relatives or other connections, so it was going to be extremely difficult to ascertain their whereabouts.
As if he hadn’t heard a word Hessen had said, Matthias repeated the same command he had issued the night he had first returned to Arvis: “Find them.”

Flustered, Hessen cleared his throat a few times before weakly replying, ‘But Your Grace, as I told you-”
“There must be someone in this world who has a connection to them,” Matthias said. He slowly closed his eyes and then even more slowly opened them. He looked exhausted, perhaps because he had been busy all day since the early morning. “Use whatever means necessary. But find them,” he added, shutting his weary eyes again. He leaned his head back, sticking his well- defined chin up into the air.
Hessen had noticed that although the duke had by all appearances been free of stress since returning to Arvis, he was looking a little gaunt. He thought it better not to mention this, however, and only replied, “Yes, Your Grace, I will.’
After Hessen left, Matthias’s bedroom was dead silent. He leaned back into his chair and remained in that position for a long while. He was still wearing his tailcoat from the party, but he didn’t feel any discomfort.
I fell into her trap, he thought. He had realized this the moment he first heard the news that she had suddenly run away.
He had already been certain she was plotting something, but he had never imagined it was something this grand.
Thinking about it, he laughed, just as he had when he first heard the news. She whispered words of love to me just so that she could run away he thou g ht. How fitting for someone so fond of driving me wild.
To cut off the endless cycle of thoughts swirling in his head, he stood up and told himself. They’ll find her. They’ll find her soon.







After slowly undressing, Matthias took a longer-than-usual shower and then went to bed. The following day started off identically to the previous one. He awoke at his usual time and ate breakfast.
As his car passed through the gates of the mansion, he was suddenly hit with a deep feeling of emptiness. It wasn’t a pleasant feeling, but it wasn’t unbearable either. However, in the back of his mind, there was an uneasy feeling that if Layla was really gone for good, his whole life was going to collapse. He found his obsessiveness over such a petty thing utterly ridiculous, so much so that he burst into laughter.
Why am I fretting over such a trivial matter?
He leaned back into the seat as he continued to laugh.
Perhaps I should be thankful. I almost made an irreparable mistake over a lowly insignificant woman.
Once these thoughts stopped swirling in his head, the twisted smile on his lips faded away. The car was now passing through the shade made by the thick foliage of the trees on Platanus Road.
Gazing at the waves of green undulating through the forest, he chuckled. How ridiculous all of this is.




112.

What If.


Matthias’s memories were triggered by an unexpected source. He was riding in the car, when he happened to see a group of schoolgirls
walking down the sidewalk.
He watched them for a short while without thinking anything of it. Then, all of a sudden, he realized that the uniforms they had on were identical to the one Layla had been wearing the day she had fallen from her bicycle.
She had been wearing a blue dress over a white blouse with short, puffed sleeves, and had had a red ribbon tied around her neck. These girls had white ribbons around their necks, suggesting that the ribbon color indicated what grade they were in. Surprised that he remembered anything about Layla’s appearance during the accident, he laughed at himself when he realized how detailed his memory ©f the incident was.
Fortunately, there was no traffic on the road, and his car quickly drove past the group of schoolgirls. He thought that this would be the end of his reminiscences about Layla. But then, as the car left the city and turned onto

the road leading to Arvis, he saw a bird perched on a tree branch fly up toward the sky.
This reminded him of the time that Layla had told him that she liked birds because they were always nearby. Only now did he feel like he understood what she had meant. Just as she had said, wherever one went, there were always birds. Thinking about the fact that there were so many birds in the world caused a chill to go down his spine.
