It came back, he thought. He could feel his heart beating faster. The bird you were waiting for came back, Layla. Soon his heart was pounding so hard, he felt like it was making his whole body shake. So you have to come back too, Layla.
Suddenly he felt like Layla really might return, or perhaps that she was already back. A twisted grin appeared on his face. He knew that he was passing into delusion, but he couldn’t stop. The next thing he knew, he was running down the forest path.
As a gentle spring rain started to fall, he emerged from the forest and saw the gardener’s cottage.
White sheets fluttered on the clothesline. The cottage’s windows glowed warmly from within. Wearing an apron, Layla came running out through the open front door. As she hurriedly gathered the laundry to save it from the rain, her French braid bobbed up and down.
Look at that. She came back.
He started laughing wildly. So this is what I’ve become, he thought, realizing that his mental disturbance over the past few weeks had been more serious than he had thought. Shortly after his breathless laughter burst forth, however,

all his hallucinations vanished. Looking around, he realized that he was all alone in the cottage’s empty front yard, surrounded only by weeds and overgrown grass. He remained standing there for a while, letting the rain fall on him.
This was his first time visiting the cottage since Layla had run away. He hadn’t wanted to face the fact that she was truly gone. As long as he didn’t check, he could maintain the delusion that she was still there.
Instead of turning back, he started trudging towards the cottage. He didn’t know why. He felt simultaneous, conflicting urges both to give up and to hold onto the last thread of his lingering attachment. Right now, however, he just felt the urge to keep walking.
The shallow steps to the porch creaked as he walked up them and extended his rain-soaked hand to the doorknob. He tried turning it. It was unlocked.
Standing in front of the open door, he briefly closed and opened his eyes, then stepped across the threshold.




115.

What’s Left?






Everything looked the same inside, other than a layer of dust. Matthias chuckled to himself as he saw that they truly seemed to have left everything behind when they ran away. It was a remarkable sight, knowing as
he did how much the gardener and Layla had loved the little cottage.
He stood in the same spot, looking around, until the water dripping from his rain-soaked clothes had made a dark spot on the dusty floorboards beneath him. The rain was pounding against the roof and windowpanes, but his heavy breathing was even louder in his ears.
Once he had caught his breath, he slowly walked to Layla’s room. It also looked just like he remembered it. It was as if she might return at any moment. He turned on the lamp next to the bed, and a dim light suffused the room.
It almost felt like, if he were to call out her name, she would appear. He imagined her running into the room and staring intently at him with her

expressive green eyes. He imagined her smiling, and whispering, “I want you to love me,”just like she had whispered to him on that beautiful evening.
He stood there with perfect posture, as always. His face also looked the same as usual—his eyes were calm with slightly lowered lids, and his mouth was gently closed. However, his perfect habits, which had always been like a suit of armor to him, were now showing cracks and holes here and there.
He had frequently been having trouble focusing his eyes. His breathing was often irregular, and he occasionally found himself unconsciously clenching his fists so tightly that they started trembling. He took another deep breath to clear his mind of these thoughts.
He took a few steps forward, and his foot bumped into a large, wooden box protruding halfway out from under the bed. He immediately forgot what he had been about to do and stooped down to slide the box out from the bed. As he did this, the box’s lid fell off, revealing countless odds and ends within.
He ran his eyes indifferently over the items, until he noticed a familiar- looking jewelry box. It was the gift he had given her. He sat on the foot of the bed and opened the velvet box. Inside was the beautiful, shimmering crystal bird. As he took in its delicate, intricate beauty, he could see Layla back on that spring day in Ratse, when she had run down the museum passageway, crying out in childlike excitement. He recalled her reaching out to try to touch the bird ornaments hanging overhead. They had been too high for her to reach, but then he had lifted her up. As if she had gained the power of flight, she had been suddenly able to reach the birds.
Layla… My Layla… Were you ever really mine… ?
He snapped the velvet box shut and put it back in the box of odds and ends, and then shifted his gaze to a corner of the room, where he saw the shoes he had bought her, sitting there, neglected. She had left behind everything he had

given her, even those shoes. He wanted to smile, but his lips wouldn’t budge. His anger had turned into disillusion, and now even that had gone away.
Then what’s left? he silently wondered.
He looked around the room again. As he turned his head this way and that, droplets of rain fell from his hair onto the bed sheets and floor.
She must have left some due behind. This can’t be the end.
Unsure what to do, he ran his hand over his face, then stood up and started searching every corner of the tiny room, looking for traces of her. He searched through her closet, her desk, all her drawers, anywhere he could find. Just about everything seemed to have been left as it was, which made him feel even more anxious.
After finishing this frantic search, he stood in the center of the room, panting. The sound of the rain outside had grown even louder. Just then, he glanced down, and noticed a notebook by his foot. Its pages were open, and there appeared to be a child’s handwriting inside.
He reached down and picked it up with his cold hand. It was Layla’s diary from when she was young.
He was about to put it down again, and then he changed his mind, and began to read it. It started the day she first arrived at Arvis.
I came to Arvis today. I met Uncle Bill! He is very, very tail and looks scary but really he is not scary at all. He doesn’t yell at me or hit me. He gave me dinner. He said that I need to eat a lot of food, like a cow. So I did eat a lot, and he didn’t get mad at me. The food was so good!
I like Unde Bill. And Arvis is really pretty. Before coming here, I wished that I could live with Uncle Bill instead of going to an orphanage. I promised that I would be a ready good girl if I did. I hope that my wish comes true!

On the back of the page of that first entry were a few flattened, dried herbs and wildflowers, as well as a few clumsy drawings of Arvis’s forest, flowers, and birds.
He slowly flipped through the yellowed pages of the diary, before another entry caught his eye.
I saw the duke. He is back from school. I was up in a tree, and he almost shot me with his gun! 1 was so scared! I cried! But the duke is really handsome. And his voice is really smooth, like the duck feather I picked up by the river. I asked Uncle Bill if all noblemen are handsome, but he said no. I guess the duke is especially handsome, f don’t know why someone so handsome would do bad things.
Matthias’s heart sank as the diary entry reminded him of their first encounter. He recalled seeing what he thought was a bird up in a tree by the river, and almost shooting it before realizing that it was a little girl. This encounter had always stood out in his memory, perhaps because of the shock of having almost killed someone.
After that encounter, he had seen her from time to time on his hunting grounds. Sometimes guests to Arvis who found children cute, like Riette, would greet her. Usually she would quickly nod her head and then run away. She had seemed especially afraid of Matthias himself.
Holding the diary, he sat back down on the bed. He wasn’t sure what he was doing, but he didn’t want to stop.
In her diary entries, Layla seemed to find joy in every little thing. She rejoiced whenever she learned the name of a new species of flower or bird. She got excited whenever her uncle Bill praised her. She was happy whenever the weather was nice. She seemed ecstatic after the first time she tasted ice cream. Amid all these cheerful entries, he suddenly came upon a sad one. As he read the entry, he realized that he remembered this day as well.

Today the duke’s cousin invited me to come to the mansion. She wanted me to play with her, but I didn’t know any of the games she wanted to play. She said I was no better than a puppy and then she left. I know how to do a lot of things! But I didn’t tell her. I don’t think she would like climbing trees or running around.
All I did was sit there, but then her maid gave me a gold coin! I didn’t want to take it, but she said I had to. I was embarrassed, so I took it and ran away. Then I tripped and felt down and I dropped the coin. And then the scary, handsome duke stepped on it. I felt realty sad about having to pick up the coin in front of the duke.
But I’m not going to be sad anymore. Uncle Bill said I earned the coin and I should be proud of it. He said that if I kept working hard, I would grow up to be an outstanding adult.
It’s really weird. Everyone else always called me a stupid orphan. When I lived with my aunt, her kids said I would live on the streets and then become a hooker. I didn’t know what a hooker was, but later I found out and I got so upset that I got in a fight with the cousin who first said it to me. But he was bigger than me so I got hurt more than he did. But then my unde only got mad at me, and he hit me and said that I had to go for a day without food. I got so hungry that I picked a turnip from the garden and ate it, but then I got caught. My uncle said that I was nothing more than a thieving rat. Everyone else said that too.
But Uncle Bill is so different. He said that I will become an outstanding adult. It feels weird, but maybe he’s right. Uncle Bill is really smart so maybe I really will be an outstanding adult. I was so happy when he said that! I could barely sleep. I felt like my heart was twinkling like a beautiful star. I have to become an outstanding adult. That way I can make Uncle Bill happy. When he is happy, he laughs really loud. I love the sound of him laughing.
Matthias stopped reading, not wanting to turn to the next page.

He didn’t know why he had stepped on the coin she had dropped that day. He had never taken part in that sort of playful provocation before, but for some reason he had been drawn to act that way with her that day.
Had he been irritated at her for hiding in the tree and almost making him shoot her? That might have been the case, but it was so long ago, his memories were hazy. All he could recall is having the urge to step on the coin. Looking at that small, skinny girl with her big, green eyes, he had just felt having fun with her.
Matthias stayed in the small room in the abandoned cottage until well after it had gotten dark.
Having written long, heartfelt diary entries when she was young, as Layla grew up, her entries grew shorter and more objective—the names of flowers and birds she had discovered in Arvis’s forest; her plans to improve her grades at school or learn to ride a bicycle; her carefully planned living expenses. She mainly wrote about her time together with Bill or Kyle, but occasionally unfamiliar names would pop up as well. Mostly these were the names of other Arvis employees.
He found a few more notebooks and placed them on the bed. They contained an account of her life from the time she was a poor orphan girl being passed from home to home, to when she traveled alone to a foreign country and became a part of her uncle Bill’s family, and her childhood and adolescent years growing up at Arvis. The entries in the latest diary suddenly cut off during the previous winter, just as the end of the year was approaching. Just around the time when Matthias had finally gained possession of the thing he had most wanted to possess—Layla herself, his beautiful bird.
He searched through all her books and all the boxes in her room, but she hadn’t left a single record of anything from after that point. She had just

disappeared.
To him, those days had been the most wonderful, radiant time of his life.
To her, however, they had been the end of her life’s beauty.
He accepted that without any anger or disappointment. He felt like he had become a spectator of his own life. Everything irritated him now. He didn’t care how anything turned out. If he had been in his own bedroom, he would have reached over to the bedside table and taken a sleeping pill to knock himself out.
He switched off the lamp and left the cottage, then started strolling back to the mansion, looking just as carefree as he had been on his way to the cottage, completely ignoring the rain that was still pouring down on him.
“Y-your Grace!”
The servants were all astir at the sight of their master soaked from head to toe, but he paid them no mind and just kept walking. He couldn’t make out anything they were saying; their voices were like a distant buzzing in his ear.
Just as he was about to reach his bedroom, he was finally brought to a stop by the sight of Hessen, who had a solemn look on his face. Behind him was a young maid who was sniffling, with a frightened look on her face. She was the maid who had been assigned to look after the canary.
Without saying anything, Matthias looked at Hessen, waiting for an explanation. The butler cleared his throat and hesitantly said, “Your bird, Your Grace…” His voice was shaking feebly.
The maid’s sniffles and whimpers grew louder.
With an uncharacteristically sad expression on his face, Hessen continued. “Unfortunately, Your Grace… your bird has died”




116.

Layla.






The rain finally stopped as dawn was breaking. Matthias sat in front of the window on the west side of his bedroom and gazed at the sunrise. The window was slightly open, and a breeze full of the fresh scent of roses wafted
in. It seemed that the roses had bloomed during the rain.
Rose season had thus truly arrived, but Layla had not. And now his bird was gone as well. He turned and looked at the empty birdcage. The food and water dishes were still full, and there were bright-yellow feathers here and there. The only thing missing was the bird itself.
He turned to face the table in front of him again. There was a wooden box, inside of which the cold, stiff canary was lying in eternal rest.
In his mind, he reminisced about all the time he and the bird had spent together. That pretty little bird had won his heart at first sight. At first it had been skittish, shrieking in alarm and trying to escape from him. Over time,

however, it had become tamer and even started showing affection towards him. From then on, he had felt that the canary truly belonged to him. Its singing had brought him a great deal of joy. He had also enjoyed the sight of it chirping and fluttering around, and the feeling of its soft feathers when it would perch on the back of his hand and stare at him. All of that felt meaningless now.
He rose from his chair, walked up to the table, and gently stroked the canary’s cold, lifeless body with the back of his singers. The golden feathers of its wings still felt so soft, it seemed like the bird might at any moment suddenly open its eyes and fly up onto his shoulder.
The bird’s cause of death was unknown. It had grown increasingly ill, and then disappeared from his life forever. Just like Layla had.
He remained there at the table for a long time, as if waiting for the dead bird to sing. As the clouds cleared away, and a bright ray of morning sunlight began to shine on the box, he whispered the bird’s name: “Layla…”
The moment he had first laid eyes on the canary, he had chosen the name Layla for it. He didn’t care if it sounded ridiculous. He could be as sweet as he wanted with the bird, and it loved him in return. He liked the feeling.
He whispered the sweet name a few more times, but the cold bird’s eyes remained closed.
It was the start of a day without song.







Layla awoke in the middle of the night, feeling very sick. She had already had a slight fever, but now her whole body was aching. After barely eating anything for days, she was sapped of energy, but she still had no appetite. Shivering, she pulled the covers up over her head. The days were all right, but the nights were hard to endure.
Is this all because of him? she wondered. She didn’t want to spend another moment dwelling on the nightmarish memories of her time with the duke, so she closed her eyes tightly had clutched the ends of her pillow. No matter how hard she tried, however, she was powerless to stop her thoughts.
Eventually she gave up, got out of bed, and walked over to the window.
The stars twinkling in the summer sky filled her unfocused eyes.
Has my wish come true? she asked the cruel God above.







Amid the silence of half-sleep, Matthias heard a familiar voice as if in the distance.
“Your Grace…”
Sounding closer now, the voice continued. “You’d better wake up now, Your Grace.”

Without moving his body at all, he opened his eyes. As his eyes regained their focus, he saw his assistant standing sheepishly at the foot of his bed.
“I’m sorry for entering your bedroom unbidden. Your Grace,” Mark said, lowering his head, “but there’s not much time left until your meeting today, and as you know it’s quite an important one. so…”
“Of course,” Matthias casually replied, not at all sounding like someone who had just awoken from sleep. As he sat up and leaned against the headboard, some maids stepped forward and opened the curtains, letting in a flood of dazzling sunshine.
Showing no surprise at the fact that it was already past one o’clock, Matthias casually got out of bed. He was fully accustomed to his new, unusual daily routine.
He stood for a while in the sunshine, staring blankly. The servants had exited, leaving the bedroom as quiet as a mouse. Irritated by the silence, he whistled for a moment, and then chuckled to himself.
Oh, that’s right. Layla is gone.
He glanced at the empty spot from which the birdcage had been taken away.
She’s dead.
He calmly accepted the fact that his bird had died, as if it were just another mundane fact, like the fact that it was sunny outside.
He then started getting dressed. It was only after he had finished tying his necktie that he tried to recall what the meeting his assistant had mentioned was about. I know it’s a meeting with the board members… What is the question at issue again? Probably just another opportunity for the board members to argue with each other about how bad things are getting on the international stage.

Without even glancing in the mirror to see how he looked, he stepped out of his dressing room. He had noticed recently that his clothes had been feeling a little loose, but he hadn’t paid it much mind. He was sure that his weight loss was just a temporary thing. As far as he was concerned, it would have been a ridiculous overreaction to have his outfits re-tailored. He had decided not to take any such drastic measures.
When he reached the main hall on the first floor, his mother smiled awkwardly at him. “Looks like you’re heading out, Matthias?”
There was no reason for her to be hanging around in this part of the mansion, so he figured that she had been waiting for him.
“Yes, Mother.”
“Is it company business?”
“Yes, there’s a meeting with the board of directors.” He smiled with his usual poise.
Taken aback by the contrast between her son’s sickly appearance and his cool, composed attitude, Elise said no more.
Matthias bowed politely and left the main hall. As soon as he stepped outside, he was showered with summery sunlight. How song has it been since Layla ran away?he wondered. He tried to figure it out for a moment, then he gave up. When’s the wedding? Is it next month? Even his upcoming wedding felt like something from another world.
Mark Evers watched him staring blankly up at the sky for a while before growing concerned and walking up to him. “Your Grace…?”
Matthias slowly raised his hand to stop Mark from saying anything more, then got into the car. The documents that Mark had prepared for the meeting were sitting neatly on the seat next to Matthias. As he started to peruse them, the car took off.

It was hard to stay focused, but he tried his hardest to read through every document in the stack. This was part of his duty as head of the family business. Throughout all the years of his life, he had never failed to fulfill this duty, and he wouldn’t let today be an exception. Sure enough, he made it through the stack of documents just as the car was coming to a stop in front of the company’s building downtown.
The chauffeur hurriedly got out of the car and opened the back passenger door.
Matthias nonchalantly stepped out and walked into the building. Once in the boardroom, he greeted the directors and took his seat at the table. As he had expected, the meeting was heated from the start. It was as if the boardroom was going to be reduced to ashes before any international battles began.
They had to decide on a singular path forward before the meeting concluded. Matthias was well aware of this, and he was well aware that the responsibility for this decision fell primarily to him.
Trying to ignore the slight headache he was feeling, he concentrated on the documents laid nut in front of him. However, the complicated numbers and figures looked like a foreign language. Soon, he wasn’t even sure what he was looking at anymore. And then, he felt like he was surrounded by distracting sounds that he knew couldn’t be real. He heard the sound of the overturned bicycle’s wheels spinning. He heard the sound of the leaves rustling in the breeze, and the sound of his racing heartbeat.
He extended his bony, emaciated hand and grasped the water glass in front of him. However, the more he tried to rid his mind of the sounds he was imagining, the louder they became, pulling him deeper and deeper into his

memories until he saw Layla on the ground ahead of him, next to the bicycle from which she had just fallen.
He started walking towards her. With each step he took, his heart beat a little faster. Once he was close enough to smell the rosy scent of her body, he felt suddenly very thirsty and his lips felt dry. However, these sensations soon went away. As Layla, lying in his shadow, looked up at him, everything else disappeared, leaving only the two of them.
He was overwhelmed by the sight of the young woman on the ground in front of him, wearing her dirt-covered school uniform. He felt humbled by her presence, even though he was the one looking down at her.
He was now able Lo identify the strange emotion he had fell that day. He was also aware that he had felt this emotion every time he was with Layla, despite his best efforts to stifle it. And he was feeling it right now. She made him feel utterly weak and insignificant.
“Um… Your Grace?”
He raised his head at the sound someone calling his name, but he was no longer able to concentrate on anything. He felt the urge to say whatever necessary to get out of there, so that he could return home and take a sleeping pill. He didn’t want to worry about anything else. The entire world could disappear and he wouldn’t care. It was a wild thought, but it was how he felt.
Gazing around the dreamlike boardroom, he mustered the last of his rationality to say, “My apologies, gentlemen. In my current state, it will be hard for me to render proper judgment”
“Wh-what do you m-”
“The House of Herhardt will accept whatever decision the board of directors comes to after an adequate discussion.” He raised his deep-blue eyes

and looked at Mr. Hessen, one of the company’s executive directors. “Mr. Hessen can act as moderator.”
Not only had Mr. Hessen played a substantial role in the management of the company since the time when Matthias’s father had been head of the company, but he had also acted as Matthias’s mentor when he took over control of the company.
The boardroom, which a moment earlier had been loud and clamorous, was instantly reduced to chilly silence.
They had already heard rumors that Duke Herhardt was in poor health, but they hadn’t imagined that it was to this extent.
As the board members started to murmur to one another, the duke rose from his seat and said, “Once again, my sincere apologies.” He then scrutinized the faces of everyone present, standing with a dignified bearing wholly unlike a man who was abnegating his duty. However, there was a strange emotion in his eyes. It was as if he was being consumed by a cold fire, or that he himself had become such a fire.
He left the boardroom. He tried to button his jacket, but his hands felt numb, so he gave up and took off the jacket entirely. He then yanked off his necktie and stuffed it in a pocket of the jacket.
Mark Evers hurried up to him, but Matthias cut him off before he could say anything, saying in a hoarse voice, “I don’t need you right now.”
Taken aback, Mark hesitated, while Matthias walked through the building’s huge lobby and down the marble stairs.
Once outside, he started staggering through the dazzlingly bright sunlight.




117.

Forever Ignorant.


News that Kyle Ettman had returned to Karlsbar spread quickly through Arvis, sending its residents into confusion once again. By now, most believed the rumor that Kyle had helped the gardener and Layla escape. They thought that he had only broken off the engagement because his mother had opposed the marriage, but that his feelings for Layla remained just as strong as
ever.
Some were skeptical, but most had believed it to be true. It would have been the happiest possible conclusion to Layla’s story. However, this hope was quashed by the sight of Kyle wandering around Arvis, looking for Layla.
As soon as Chef Mona heard that he had come to Arvis, she ran outside and shouted at him, “Hey, Kyle!” When he turned to face her, she continued. “Even you don’t know where Layla and Bill are? I can’t believe it!”
He said nothing in response, but it wasn’t hard for Mona to guess the meaning of his silence.
When he had shown up in Karlsbar the previous morning, his father had told him to go back to college at once, but he had defied him, saying that he

was determined to find Layla.
“I’m sorry. Chef Mona…” he finally replied.
“Sorry?! Don’t say that. Ugh, I’m the one who should be sorry. I shouldn’t have spoken so carelessly.” She rubbed her eyes, which had started to tear up.
Kyle excused himself, and then frantically ran across the garden. As he entered the forest, he felt a vague hope that if he went to the cottage, he would be reunited with Layla and Bill. However, as he emerged from the forest, he was hit with a rude awakening.
“Layla…?”
When he saw the cottage’s weedy, overgrown front yard, his heart sank. After thoroughly checking out the cottage’s exterior and even looking around inside, he felt like his legs were going to give out under him.
“Layla! Layla! Layla!”
Some startled birds fled at the sound of his screaming, but Layla was nowhere to be found.







Matthias wandered aimlessly. He knew it was ridiculous for him to have left the board meeting with such confidence when he had nowhere in mind to go. Nonetheless, he couldn’t stop.
As he plodded down the road, he felt like he was floating, perhaps because of the sleeping pills that he had been taking every day. He had a splitting headache, and his whole body was In pain from the fever. However, this only made him want to keep moving forward.

The pain made everything else bearable. If the pain were to go away, he felt like his memories of Layla would swallow him up. This filled him with a sense of dread unlike anything he had experienced before.
So come back, Layla.
He sighed and chuckled at the same time.
You worked so hard to transform me into the miserable being I’ve become, so wouldn’t you at least like to come appreciate your handiwork? Come back. Come back and laugh at me. Come see the man you destroyed. You’ll enjoy it. Smile at me once more as my queen. Please, just come back, please…
Gritting his teeth, he picked up his pace. Soon he was on the road that led to Arvis. When he turned the corner onto Platanus Road, he saw the quiet spot where he had last seen Layla.
Despite his staggering gait, he kept moving steadily forward until he reached the spot where he and Layla had faced each other for the last time. Once again, he felt like he could hear the sounds he had heard that day. They were like music to his ears.
Panting, he lowered his bloodshot eyes and looked at his shadow. Layla wasn’t there. She had disappeared, and it now seemed that she would never return. As he finally accepted that fact, he found himself bursting into laughter. He felt like crying, but all that came out was laughter.
After passing through Arvis’s gates, he once again headed towards the cottage where Layla had lived.
After their tense first encounter, he had seen her occasionally while he was in the forest hunting. Each time, she had acted frightened and had hidden or run away from him.
Irritated by this, he had once spurred his horse so that he could block, her path and prevent her from getting away. For a moment, she had stood frozen

with fear, and then had scampered off the path into the forest weeds, tripping and falling over at one point but then getting up immediately and continuing to run away.
Once she was sure that Matthias wasn’t chasing after her, she had returned to the path, and then looked over her shoulder at him. Despite the great distance between them, he could sense that she was glaring at him.
As he chuckled at this, she had started walking briskly back towards the cottage, kicking sticks and stones in her path to vent her anger.
He had found this amusing, so he had continued to tease her like this occasionally. He had stopped this after she had undergone a sudden growth spurt and no had longer looked like a little girl. After that, he had thoroughly ignored her, until the summer day when he had encountered her and seen that she was now a fully grown woman.
Thus, it couldn’t be said that he had fallen in love with her at first sight. It was hard to pinpoint exactly when it had happened. Maybe there wasn’t a precise point in time. Just as she had gradually grown up, his feelings had gradually evolved as well. The little girl whom he had enjoyed picking on had grown into a young maiden who had gotten on his nerves. And then that young maiden had grown into a beautiful woman who had dominated him fully.
If he didn’t know when his love for her had begun, he certainly didn’t know when it would end.
So what am I supposed to do without you here, Layla?
He looked around with wary eyes. He was surrounded by memories of Layla. All of those countless memories felt wonderful and precious to him. And yet, as he traced over them in his mindP he chuckled again.

She had constantly tried to get away from him, and had cried in fear when she hadn’t been able to. If the whole idea of them becoming lovers was just a ruse she had devised in order to exact her revenge on him, then that meant that she had never really been his. Her tears and suffering were the only sincere thing he had ever witnessed from her. This made him feel unbearably miserable and empty, and yet he couldn’t let go.
He couldn’t stand the fact that such a lowly, insignificant woman had shaken his life to the core. This was why he had tormented her. When he made her cry, he felt relief, as if her tears were confirmation that his life was safe. However, he had never for a moment wished that she would disappear.
Looking back on it now, he felt ridiculous. If she had bothered him so. why hadn’t he just gotten rid of her? He had never once considered doing that. He wanted her to stay, crying by his side. Forever, as his Layla.
After wandering around aimlessly for a while, his gaze came to an abrupt stop at the end of the forest path. The man who had stopped there was staring back at him.
It was the man who Layla loved—Kyle Ettman.







Kyle remained calm. It was strange. He had thought that he would have been ready to kill the duke on sight, but now that he was actually looking at him in the flesh, he felt no violent urges, but only cold disdain.
He slowly started walking one step at a time towards Matthias. Suddenly he remembered Layla with a tear-covered face saying, “I love him.”

He knew she wanted him to believe that it was just a lie she had told to protect him, but he had seen through that. When he had gone to the cottage and seen the two of them together, he had known at once that she really was in love with the duke. Otherwise, there was nothing the duke could have done to make her accept his affections. She would have rather died. That was who she was.
“I love him.”
It was for that reason that those words had caused a deep pain in his heart that had never gone away.
For all he knew, even she hadn’t realized that she was telling the truth. The duke likely hadn’t realized this either. But Kyle had. Only Kyle had known. And yet, he had lost Layla, while the duke had been able to hold on to her, without knowing what he had. And then, the duke had brought so much pain upon the woman who loved him, he had ended up losing her as well. It was truly ridiculous.
Kyle unclenched his fists and started striding forward. Once he was close enough to make out the duke’s face, he stopped, surprised. The duke looked too composed to be drunk, and yet he
didn’t look like himself al all.
“Considering that you ruined Layla’s life, you look surprisingly joyful, Your Grace.” Kyle said, using sarcasm to suppress his anger.
As he feeling regret? If so, that’s all the more detestable.
