“What did she say?” Bill asked once the neighbor was out of earshot.
“She asked if I was out on a walk with my father. So I said yes,” she replied, looking sheepishly up at him.

Father… ? Bill mulled over this word for a moment, then laughed it off. That poor girl gave her body to the duke in order to buy my freedom. I don’t deserve to be called her father. Still, I like the ring of it… Father… While he felt a little guilty, hearing her call him her father filled him with pride.
Is she going to keep calling me that… ? he wondered as they continued to walk to the square. He continued to think about it as they ate lunch and went on a walk along the seaside. Just as he was about to ask her if she intended to keep calling her his father, he looked over at her smiling face and lost the nerve to say anything. He had a tingling feeling in his chest. It felt somewhat like he was being tickled with a feather, and somewhat like he was being stabbed with a knife. Either way, he felt very guilty.
Just as he was starting to feel tears welling up in his eyes, they arrived at the beach. Clean, white sand extended for miles up the coast. There seemed to be some sort of lively summer festival going on that weekend. The vendors’ colorful stalls and parasols made it look like a flower garden.
They bought ice creams and ale them together. Lately Layla had been so hesitant to eat anything, Bill had worried that she might be sick. She didn’t hesitate at all in devouring the ice cream, however.
They started walking down the beach. Layla looked very happy and excited as she walked with her shoes and socks off. They laughed merrily. Neither of them could remember when they had last had such a good time. They decided to focus on the present. That was all that really mattered, after all.
As the sun was setting, they returned home and finished off their day. The following day wasn’t much different.
What does it matter what she calls me? Bill thought, looking at Layla across the breakfast table on Monday morning. No matter what she calls me, the fact remains that we’re family.

Soon, he noticed that she kept glancing at him furtively. “Layla, is there something you want to talk to me about?” he asked.
Now that he thought about it, she had been glancing at him now and then since the previous day.
She paused for a moment, then replied, “No… I don’t. We should get going.
It’s time to leave for work.”
She was smiling brightly, but he still felt suspicious.
Something was clearly on her mind. What could she be hiding? He tried to think what it could be, but nothing came to mind. Meanwhile, they reached the square, which was the spot where they always parted ways to go to their respective workplaces.
Normally the square was full of bustling energy at this time of morning, but today for some reason it was quiet and solemn. A large crowd of people was gathered around the bulletin board in front of City Hall. They seemed sad and morose, as if they were at a funeral.
“I’m going to go see what’s going on,” Layla told Bill.
As she entered the crowd, she heard mournful sighs and sobs all around her. She discovered the reason for this as soon as she got to the front of the crowd and read the notice on the bulletin board.
A wartime mobilization order had been declared, ordering all men eligible for conscription to enlist in the military.







“If he has a serious illness, wouldn’t it be better to postpone the wedding? I know that sounds embarrassing to you, but you don’t want a sickly groom walking down the aisle,” Countess Brandt said, frowning at her daughter.
The ice cubes clinked as they held their glasses of punch.
“He’s not ill,” Claudine replied, nipping her mother’s concern in the bud while looking angrily at the large garden next to the terrace.
“You said he hasn’t been eating or sleeping and has lost a lot of weight,” Countess Brandt replied. “Matthias always used to take good care of his health. If he’s not ill, what’s the matter with him?”
“It doesn’t matter. Even if he does have an illness, we can’t postpone the wedding again, Mother,” Claudine firmly replied. As far as she was concerned, the decision to postpone the wedding the previous summer had been a grave mistake. If they had just pushed forward with the wedding at that time, she wouldn’t have had to be dealing with any of the problems facing her now.
Matthias had said that he would give her until the following weekend to come up with a way to frame the situation in the Brandt family’s favor, such that it would look like they were the ones choosing to call off the engagement, not him. Claudine had practically screamed at him for this, but he hadn’t batted an eye. His composure had been truly disturbing to witness. Whenever Claudine thought back on that interaction, it filled her with anger and fear.
She had told him exactly how she had found out about the way he had helped Mrs. Ettman stop Layla and Kyle from getting married. She had also

told him about how she had guessed about his relationship with Layla and how he had been tormenting her.
She had revealed all this to him with the expectation that it would upset him, but he had reacted with no more than a dry chuckle, and then had repeated his declaration that their engagement was over. He had said this with almost inhuman coldness. Maybe he is sick after all, Claudine mused. But it’s not a physical ailment. He just has s total lack of compassion.
“I’m worried about him, Claudine,” Countess Brandt said as she slowly fanned her furrowed brow. “The men in the Herhardt family have tended to die young. I know you think he’s not in poor health, but-”
“I’m going to marry him, Mother. The wedding is happening, period.”
She hated Matthias so much that it gave her the chills just to think about him, but this only made her all the more determined to marry him. The ultimate revenge she could take on him would be to make him her husband.
But was that still possible?
Now that she knew just how ruthless he was, it seemed like he was capable of anything. If she didn’t use the time he had given her and come up with a way for her family to save face, then he might well announce the breaking off of their engagement himself.
How dare he do that to me because of that lowly girl?
Just then. Claudine was snapped out of her introspection by Marie, her maid, who had just frantically run up to her. “C-countess Brandt! Miss Claudine!”
Marie had never behaved like this before. Her face had gone pale and her whole body was trembling.
“What’s the matter? Why are you shouting?” Countess Brandt asked in a scolding tone.

Marie broke into tears and replied, “It’s war! They say that war has broken out!”




121.

Two Pieces of News.






While the war started between only two countries, it rapidly spread across the entire continent. It all started when Lovita, which led the Southern Alliance, declared war on the Berg Empire, the strongest of the northern countries. Berg then declared war on Lovita in response. Due to the complex system of treaties and alliances across the continent, the rest of the countries were soon swept up in the war as well, dividing the continent into
two opposing forces.
On the same day that Berg’s emperor stood in front of the citizens and resolutely proclaimed the mobilization order, Matthias received another piece of news. He learned that Bill Remmer’s Lovitan relative resided in the city of Sienne, on Lovita’s southern coast.
Prior to the war, Lovita had been a neighboring country that anyone from Berg could visit as they pleased.

Overnight, it had become an enemy state, making further investigation impossible. Matthias didn’t mind this, however, because he already had all the information he needed. Now that he knew where Layla had run off to, he just needed to find a way to go there himself.
“Are you serious, Captain Herhardt?” Matthias’s commanding officer, a general, asked, looking skeptically at him. “As I’ve already mentioned, that area will probably become a ferocious battlefield.”
“Yes, I know.”
Matthias’s excessively calm reply baffled the general all the more. As an officer, if Matthias wanted, he could easily obtain a post at a quiet supply unit, away from the vanguard. The general had recommended this to him. And yet Matthias had instead insisted on being sent to the front line.
“I’m going to go to Sienne.”
Taken aback by Matthias’s insistence, the middle-aged general eventually let out a dry laugh.
With Matthias, the Berg Empire’s Sixth Infantry would be tasked with breaking through the right wing of the front line and advancing southward to capture Lovita’s naval base. Their ultimate target was Sienne. If the Berg Army could seize and occupy the naval base there, it would greatly weaken all the forces in the Southern Alliance.
“If that’s really what you want to do, I guess there’s no sense in trying to stop you,” the general said, signing his approval on the documents Matthias had brought. “I suppose I shouldn’t be surprised. You are a Herhardt, after all,” he added, as if that name explained everything.
As the Berg Empire had rose over many generations to become the richest, most powerful nation on the continent, it had fought countless wars, and the Herhardt family had readily offered its sons to these wars. The young men of

the Herhardt family fought on fiercer front lines, and won more glory and honor, than even the princes of the imperial family. All aristocrats in Berg knew exactly how that history and honor had been made.
Matthias saluted the general and left the office. Some aristocratic officers started whispering to each other when they saw him, but he paid them no mind, and casually passed by them. He looked like someone out on a leisurely afternoon stroll. Once he was out of earshot, they started gossiping about him at full volume.
“I could’ve sworn I heard that he was gravely ill and was staying at home to recuperate. Was that a lie?”
“No, I’m pretty sure it’s true that he was sick… But why did he look so happy just now? Is the House of Herhardt in the munitions industry or something?”
“I’m sure they are. What industry don Y they have business in? But even so, he’s about to be sent off to the battlefield, and he looks like he’s on his way to a picnic. Is he right in the head?”
“Maybe he thinks that as a Herhardt his destiny will save him from death on the battlefield. Although things didn’t work out so well for the previous dukes in his family, of course.”
They continued exchanging snide remarks long after he was out of sight. They couldn’t understand his blithe attitude, given that they were all bound by destiny to go risk their lives for the sake of family honor.
“Calm down. Duke Herhardt isn’t our enemy,” one of the officers finally said.

With that comment, the group’s overzealous criticism of Matthias quickly died down, and the men returned to a frightened silence. No one spoke for a long time after that.




“How could this be happening? I can’t believe it,” Elise von Herhardt said, breaking into tears again. “In these prosperous modern times, how can a barbaric war break out, Matthias?!” She looked at her son with bloodshot eyes. Dowager Herhardt and the other relatives present looked similarly distraught. Only Matthias, the one who was actually about to head off to war, looked more or less the same as normal, maintaining a calm, dignified bearing
with a polite smile on his face throughout the interaction.
“The war front is south of the Berg-Lovita border, so the fighting won’t reach Arvis. I know the war seemed to break out suddenly, but war clouds have been looming for many years. Each country’s military plans were already secured, and our company already had a contingency plan in place.”
He was speaking just as calmly as he did when participating in business meetings.
“It’s inevitable that the resources branch of our company will face some damage, but it’s predicted that our overseas oilfields will be safe from the aftermath of the war. The loss won’t be huge. The board has put the company’s plans to develop the swamps northeast of Karlsbar on hold, but the project will resume once the war has concluded.”
Matthias went on like this, delivering a detailed report on all the actions he had taken to deal with the international turmoil that had resulted after the outbreak of war threw the stock market into confusion.

The day that war was announced, Matthias emerged from his cabin. He looked well-groomed and not at all sickly, and didn’t seem particularly concerned about the earth-shattering news. He just calmly began preparing to do his part for the war. His face was still noticeably gaunt, but he had regained the lively glint in his eyes, so he no longer looked ill.
“And since each of the branches of our family’s company has its own management structure, things should keep running smoothly even with my absence. We have also built a system to prepare specifically for this.”
“The company is one thing, but won’t we have to rush the wedding now?” Dowager Herhardt asked, having regained some of her composure. “With the current situation, it doesn’t seem possible to hold the ceremony at the scheduled time, so we’d better wrap things up before you head off to war.”
“If you’re worried about an heir…” Matthias paused for a moment and looked at the various members of his family. “I won’t be able to defer my departure very long. There’s no guaranteeing that I would be able to father an heir in that time, so I don’t think it would make much sense to push forth with a hasty wedding.”
“But, Matthias…”
“Suppose that Claudine and I got married and then I went off to war without having fathered a son, and then failed to return. Claudine would be a lonely widow who had only lived with her husband for a few weeks at most. In my opinion, that would be much too cruel a thing to do to Claudine and the Brandt family.”
Even as he was speculating about his own possible death, he showed no signs of fear or anguish. He was showing all the poise and virtue that he had been raised to display.

“Of course, I should emphasize that I do see it as my duty to return home safe and sound. Rest assured that I will do all I can to make that happen, Grandmother.”
A gentle smile appeared on his face as he said this. However, his tone was resolute and stubborn, as if to indicate that there was no room for reconsideration. While everyone else remained silent, he continued his report.
“However, if the worst does happen, I have prepared a contingency plan to help you find a worthy heir among the collateral family line.”
Elise had always been proud of her son, but as she watched him calmly delivering this report to the family, she felt a pang of guilt. Sure enough, he was a perfect duke, just like his father and grandfather had been. Viewed as an ordinary person, however, was his life truly complete?
As she mulled over this question, her tears started to fall again. However, she couldn’t bring herself to ask the question that was burning inside her: Do you really have to go off to the battlefield? They were Herhardts. The family name always took precedence over personal feelings.
“When my late husband, your grandfather, was duke and war broke out, he didn’t hesitate in fighting for the empire and giving his life,” Dowager Herhardt said, gazing at her grandson.
Generations of Herhardts had spent centuries building up the reputation that the family enjoyed today. And they had accepted all the duties and responsibilities that came with that honor.
“The same was true of your father,” Dowager Herhardt added. A look of grief flashed across her face as she thought of her son’s death, before she regained her composure. “Those sacrifices make me proud to be a Herhardt. My husband and son were brave and honorable men, and I will never stop feeling proud of them. But even so, Matthias…” Her voice started to quiver

slightly. “I would like so much to see you grow old. Don’t you think Arvis needs at least one portrait of an older, silver-haired duke, among all the young dukes adorning its walls?”
“That’s a good point,” Elise remarked, smiling along with her mother-in- law. “You inherited your hair from my side of the family, Matthias, so if you want gray hair, you’ll have to live for quite a while longer.”
She casually pointed to her own hair. Even in her middle age, it was lustrous and black.
“Yes, Grandmother, and yes, Mother,” Matthias replied, smiling at each of the former duchesses in turn. “I will do my best to make that happen.”







“You must be so happy. You’ve managed to evade marriage once again,” Claudine quipped.
Count and Countess Brandt had left the room, leaving only Matthias and Claudine in the drawing room.
The wedding was being temporarily postponed. Although Claudine was not happy about it, her family’s decision had not changed. If Matthias were to go off to war right after the wedding and die in battle, Claudine would be left a childless widow. In that case, the Herhardt family’s aristocratic title would be transferred to a collateral member of Matthias’s family, making Claudine’s and Matthias’s marriage all but meaningless.
Count Brandt thus insisted that the best course of action would be to postpone the wedding once again and hope for the best in the future. After all,

there was no shame in postponing a wedding when a war was going on. The biggest danger was the Herhardt family, which might have wanted to push forward with a wedding no matter what in order to create an heir. Luckily, however they had said that they would respect the Brandt family’s opinion.
Respect— that word had stung Claudine’s ears when she heard her father say it with relief.
“I don’t know about that,” Matthias replied. “Who would be happy at such a tragic event as war breaking out?”
The unhinged demeanor that he had shown on the day he first told Claudine that he intended to break off the engagement was gone. By all appearances, he seemed to have fully returned to his normal self. Nonetheless, Claudine could sense that inside he hadn’t changed a bit.
“As I told you that day,” she said, “I will not allow our engagement to be broken off, no matter what anyone does or says. I will patiently await your safe return, so that you can finally become my husband.”
“Using the excuse of war to cleanly break off the engagement is the best way for you and your family to save face.”
“Now you’re worried about me and my family? When did you become so selfless, Duke Herhardt?” she scoffed.
As always, he stared back at her with cold, emotionless eyes. Those blue eyes looked completely inhuman to her now.
“You probably want to blame Layla’s flight on me. You think that if you break off our engagement, then she’ll return to you. But you’re wrong. It wasn’t because of me, it was because of you.” Her eyes were starting to fill with tears. “If you take away the nice exterior of your Herhardt name, you’re just a monster underneath. A cold-blooded monster.”




122.

The Way to the Ocean.






By now, Claudine found herself just as detestable as she found Matthias. She knew that her relationship with him was over, but she wouldn’t admit it. Instead, she kept getting nastier and nastier with him. However,
realizing this only made it harder for her to stop.
If she was going down, it was only fair that she bring him down with her. “You will never get her back. If she’s anything like me, I bet she’d rather die
than go back to a monster like you,” she said.
“That wouldn’t be so bad,” the handsome monster replied, maintaining his calm smile even as Claudine grew more and more upset. “If she dies, at least she can’t run away from me anymore.”
“So you’re willing to kill Layla if she won’t go back to you?” “I am.”
“Are you out of your mind?”

“Maybe.”
She stared at him wide-eyed as he said these disturbing things while maintaining his gentlemanly composure.
“It sounds like you’re threatening me to do as you say if I don’t want to fear for my life,” she said.
“Don’t be silly,” he replied, frowning in mock indignation. “Why would I threaten you, when I’m the one who is calling for our engagement to be broken off? Naturally, I will bear the brunt of the criticism for the reputational harm I’m causing you and your family.”
He was now speaking more politely, with a gentle expression on his face.
This was even more irritating to Claudine than his earlier attitude.
“What if I still don’t change my mind, even after you’ve absorbed all the criticism? Then what will you do? Kill me?” she asked.
“I doubt it will come to that,” he replied, rising from his seat. “There are many ways to keep someone alive, while making them suffer a fate worse than death.” He pulled back his shirt cuff to check his watch, then smiled at her. “Of course, I trust that it won’t come to that, either. I know you’re smarter than that.”
“You don’t know me.”
She started to cry in a way that she hadn’t cried since she was a little girl. She had cast aside her true love, Riette, and was instead enduring life with this cold-hearted monster. She had lowered herself to the lowest depths of depravity in order to get rid of the monster’s mistress. She had done all this just so that she could attain the envied position of duchess. As she reflected on this, passionate tears continued to flow from her eyes.
“You don’t know anything about me. You have no idea who you’re dealing with.”

She put her face in her hands and continued sobbing. Someone watching from afar would assume that they were witnessing a woman crying over the sadness of her fiance leaving for war.
Matthias stood for a while, looking down at her, then politely bowed his head and left. As he exited the Brandt mansion, the bright, clear sky dazzled his eyes. He chuckled to himself.







That summer, on the day that there was originally supposed to be a lavish wedding, Duke Herhardt instead reported for duty as an officer in Berg’s Sixth Infantry. His unit’s destination was Sienne, the city on the southern coast of Lovita, famed for its beautiful ocean.
The retching sounds continued for a while before finally subsiding. Kyle stood up unsteadily, then staggered a few steps to sit and catch his breath in the shade of a nearby tree. As the army surgeons busily went in and out of the field hospital, a strong scent of blood and antiseptic wafted off of them.
Kyle felt another wave of nausea coming on, but luckily it died down on its own. It seemed that his body had given up, now that it had nothing left to expel.
A surgeon approached him and asked, “You feeling any better?” The question snapped Kyle out of his daze. “Not really. Have a seat”
The surgeon sat down next to Kyle and offered him his canteen. The surgeon had graduated from the same medical school where Kyle was studying. Of all the men in the unit, he was the only one who looked after Kyle.

Accepting the canteen from him, Kyle took a swig of water, swished it around in his mouth, and then spit it out. Then he took a large gulp of water and swallowed it. As his mind became clearer, the screams coming from inside the barracks grew clearer as well. It sounded like injured soldiers were waking up after surgery.
“How much longer until we get to Sienne?” Kyle asked in an exhausted voice as he gazed up at the treetops. By this time of year, the trees back in Karlsbar were already starting to change color, but here in Lovita they were still green.
“At our current rate, we should be arriving there very soon,” the surgeon replied, patting Kyle’s shoulder to comfort him. “I know it’s tough, but try to hang in there, Kyle. Luckily, our side is doing much better in the war. If our luck holds up. we might be home by winter”
Kyle had been hearing optimistic promises like this every day, while the bloodshed and gore only grew all the time. Nonetheless, he nodded. If the gruesome injuries he was seeing in the field hospital were what winning looked like, he didn’t want to imagine what things looked like for the losing side.
The war had turned his life upside overnight. Shortly after the shocking mobilization announcement, he had been conscripted, as had many other medical students from his program.
The impression that the war had been unexpected was quickly dispelled. The empire was carrying out a detailed war plan, which they had already had in place prior to the declaration of war. The task of drafting millions of troops, training and arming them, and then sending them to the front lines was accomplished in the blink of an eye. It was frightening how quick and efficient the process was. Once Kyle arrived at the front line, he realized that behind the

scenes, the empire had been planning for war for a long time, while waiting for just the right time and pretext.
He was in a complete daze. By the time he came to his senses, he was already at the battlefield. He had been assigned to be a medic for a unit that was attacking the southern line. He had barely completed one year of college, but since he was a student at the empire’s best medical school, he was occasionally summoned to the field hospital to help out in the operation room. This tended to happen when a lot of wounded soldiers had just come in, as was the case today.
Just as Kyle and the surgeon were about to go back into the operating room, their superior office stopped them and said, “You two—head over to the commanders’ barracks.”
“Is there an injured officer?”
“They say that a company commander has been shot. Sounds like he has some other minor wounds, too. Anyway, given that he hasn’t been taken to the operating room, it can’t be too severe, so you two should be able to handle it. Take care of it and report back here.” He then hurried back into the field hospital.
Unlike the surgeon sitting calmly next to him, Kyle looked visibly shaken. As he went to the storage room to get a first aid kit, he kept thinking to himself, Please let it be him… Please let it be the duke…







Layla squinted her eyes as she watched the line of trucks exiting the museum warehouse. Her hand was trembling uneasily as it gripped the strap of her bag. For several days now, the same thing had been happening. Precious fossils and gems from the museum’s collection were being transported away somewhere. The museum director had told the worried employees that it was all just a precaution, but it was clear to everyone that something out of the
ordinary was going on.
Once the trucks had disappeared down the road, Layla started walking. Most of the vehicles in the city had been commandeered by the military, so the roads were quiet. On the other hand, groups of refugees were gathering all over the city.
Sienne’s mayor had declared that the military forces in the Southern Alliance would turn the tides of the war very soon, and that Sienne itself would be safe. The refugees, however, claimed otherwise. They were saying that Berg’s army was driving southward at a ferocious pace, and that Lovita had abandoned the northeastern front and sent all its forces to the western front in order to defend the capital. Thus it seemed like Lovita’s high command might be planning to leave Sienne defenseless against a Berg attack.
Of course, the citizens of Sienne vehemently disputed this prediction, claiming that the king would not give up the city so easily, since its naval base made it a key point for distributing military supplies. The fierceness of the

citizens’ denial came from their deep fear of attack. Everyone knew this, but no one would state it openly. Bill and Layla were no different.
Layla went to the market, then hurried back home. As the war dragged on, distribution chains had been severely disrupted, causing the cost of food to rise day by day. The amount of money that used to cover a week’s food for two people, now wouldn’t even cover enough for one person.
Before starting on dinner, Layla went to her room and lay down on the bed. Her back had been bothering her a lot recently, making it hard to stand up for very long. When she took off her support belt, it felt a lot better, but it didn’t seem like she could continue like this for much longer.
Her mind wandered from one thought to another, and soon she fell into a light sleep. Shortly thereafter, she was suddenly awakened by the sound of a loud boom.
Did someone just drop something heavy upstairs?
As she casually sat up, rubbing her eyes, there was suddenly another boom. This one was even louder. Its thunderous roar echoed through the streets outside. At the same time, she heard Bill screaming for her.
“Layla! Layla!”
Before she had time to respond, he had opened her door and run up to her, with a terrified look on his face.
“We’ve got to get out of here. Layla! Right now!”
There was no time to explain, so he scooped Layla’s frightened body into his arms and started running out. Just as they made it to the street, another deafening explosion reverberated all around them.
It was an air raid.
The company commander from the cavalry regiment had only had his shin grazed by a piece of grenade shrapnel, but he was crying like a baby. It was

hard to imagine how such a faint-hearted man had risen to the rank of a commander.







Kyle let out a silent sigh and turned around. Absentmindedly gazing around the room, his eyes happened to settle on the man he hated: Duke Herhardt. Or, as he was known here. Major Herhardt. Regardless of the title, he was someone that Kyle wanted to kill more than the enemy. Disappointingly, he appeared to be unscathed.
As soon as the antiseptic was applied to the cavalry commander’s wound, he started screaming again. Perhaps because of the noise, Kyle became even more aware of Matthias von Herhardt, who was sitting with his legs crossed, smoking a cigarette, watching his fellow soldier shriek in agony as if he were watching some light entertainment.
“Hey, medic! Don’t just stand there, go attend to the major!” an officer shouted at Kyle.
Only then did Kyle realize that he had been glaring at the duke. He reflexively responded to the officer and walked over to the duke. Even from up close, he looked completely uninjured, much to Kyle’s dismay.
They stared at each other until Kyle broke the silence, asking, “Will you show me your wound, Major?”
Matthias took off his military coat and slowly raised the left sleeve of his combat fatigues. Kyle frowned when he saw what appeared to be an

improvised bandage, soaked in blood. It was more blood than he would have expected from a minor wound.
“May I take a look?”
Matthias responded to Kyle’s question with a quick nod. He was acting indifferently enough to make Kyle feel silly for harboring such animosity towards him.
The duke was always like this. When Kyle found out that they had been assigned to the same unit, he was very upset, but the duke paid him no mind, as if Kyle were no more notable to him than a pebble or twig on the side of the road. Kyle would have preferred not to have to see the duke at all, but the high command, knowing the relationship between the Ettman and Herhardt families, had done Kyle the dubious favor of assigning him to the same unit. So now Kyle would have to spend the whole war as a subordinate of the man he hated.
With well-practiced movements, Kyle unwrapped the bandage. When he saw what was underneath, his frown deepened. As it turned out, the officer with the bullet wound was not the one who was screaming in pain over a bit of shrapnel, but rather the calm, quiet Matthias von Herhardt. Kyle managed to remove the bullet, but the wound remained.
“I think you’re going to need stitches,” Kyle said, with an expression that showed mixed emotions.
Matthias, meanwhile, was just as unemotional as always. Nodding, he replied, “As you wish.”




123.

That Name.






“You don’t find that painful at all?” Kyle impulsively asked after he finished applying the antiseptic to the duke’s wound.
The surgeons had been summoned away to other barracks, and the other officers had all left after being treated, leaving Kyle and the duke by themselves.
Kyle wasn’t asking out of concern for the duke’s pain. He was just taken aback by the way that, even with a bullet wound in his arm, the duke was acting as calm and indifferent as if he had been witnessing a stranger’s misfortune.
The duke stared at him without saying anything in response. There was a faint smile on his lips. It took all of Kyle’s self restraint to keep himself from jamming the scissors in his hand into the duke’s neck.

Here on the war front, the duke seemed like a completely different person from the man Kyle had seen the last time he was at Arvis. Or rather, the duke seemed to have returned to his usual self.
When Kyle had heard his father expressing worry about the duke’s health, he had felt a glimmer of hope. Perhaps the duke was finally feeling some modicum of guilt over his treatment of Layla. It wasn’t any of Kyle’s business, but he still hoped this was the case, for Layla’s sake. If the duke, whom he knew she loved, was still remorseless, Kyle would feel very sorry for her. However, it now appeared that he was still wholly unrepentant.
Once he finished stitching up the wound, Kyle took a step back, gritting his teeth to suppress his anger. The duke casually lowered his sleeve and stood up, then poured some brandy into a glass and turned around. He looked less like a soldier in the middle of a war than like the upper-class gentleman he had always been.
“Shall I pour you a glass?” he asked.
Kyle almost burst into laughter. “You and I aren’t exactly the type to drink and have a good time, Major.”
“Then let’s drink and have a bad time.” Kyle frowned. Is he messing with me?
Looking amused, the duke smiled as he poured a second glass and handed it to him.
Out of anger, Kyle downed the glass in one gulp. The strong brandy burned his throat the whole way down.
“You’re in quite a good mood for someone with a bullet wound, Major.” “Of course I am.” The duke set his glass down after taking a sip, then slowly
put his coat back on. “We’re almost to Sienne,” he added in a soft voice.
That bastard, Kyle thought, shuddering, as he set down his empty glass.

The duke then finished his glass as well. Even though his wound had to be quite painful, he was showing no sign of suffering, other than slightly slower movements than normal.
It appeared that the outstanding gentleman was also an outstanding soldier. He slept well, ate well, and killed well. The fact that he had been promoted from captain to major only a few months after joining the war attested to his great skill.
Sometimes he seemed truly elated. At such times, Kyle could understand why the enemy soldiers called him a demon. It seemed that Kyle wasn’t alone in this impression, either, as other soldiers seemed to have a hard time relating to him. Even his superior officers seemed to fear him.
Regardless, though, he was a capable commander, and going into battle with him meant a better chance of coming back alive. For that alone, he was afforded definite respect and recognition.
Although, of course, he himself didn’t seem to care about this at all.
Then what’s he so pleased about? Kyle wondered, but he couldn’t think of any reason why the duke should be happy that they were nearing Sienne.
In truth, “happy” didn’t feel like the right word for it. The duke’s mood seemed strangely lifted, but he still looked cold and serious. Kyle was just glad that he didn’t have to face him as an enemy. If the duke kept his spirits up like this, by the war’s end, the Herhardt family would likely have contributed another hero to the annals of the Berg Empire.
“You can go,” the duke said, signaling the end of their short conversation. Kyle brusquely saluted him and left. As he walked along the dark road,
crickets chirping all around him, he looked up at the starry, foreign sky and prayed. First he prayed for Layla’s safety. Then he prayed that the next bullet would pierce the duke’s heart.







It was after midnight when people finally started emerging from the church basement where they had taken shelter. Now that the air raid had ceased, the city was enveloped in serene darkness. Even amid the darkness, however, an ominous atmosphere remained, emanating from the bell tower in the square, which had been destroyed in the bombing.
“I can’t believe that really happened,” Bill muttered.
While the Berg army was advancing by land, word had circulated that Beloff, one of the other great powers of the Northern Alliance, was going to use a fleet to blockade and bombard Sienne by sea. Not wanting to believe this, most had dismissed it as a lie spread by the enemy, but now the sad truth was plain to see.
“At least those Beloff bastards did us one favor. Now that noisy bell won’t be bothering us anymore,” Bill said, trying to lighten the mood since Layla still looked frightened.
She pushed up her glasses and looked at the ruined tower, then giggled weakly, which he was happy to hear.
“You’re right,” she replied, trying to smile even though her heart was still pounding anxiously. “And I was just getting used to it. I’m going to miss that thing.”
At first, they had thought that they had lucked out in finding a nice, clean apartment for a surprisingly low rent. It hadn’t taken them long to figure out why the rent was so low, however. It had been because of the bell tower in the

square. Each hour, the bell had rung out at an earsplitting volume. But now, the bell tower was gone. Now they had a cheap and quiet apartment. Taking this lighthearted perspective brought them some relief.
“All right then, get on my back. Let’s go home,” Bill said, crouching down for her.
Only then realizing that she didn’t have her shoes on, Layla looked frustrated. “No! I can walk. These are thick socks.”
“Don’t be ridiculous. Get on my back. If you keep at this, we’ll be out on the street all night.”
“No, really! I want to walk. I’m going to walk. I don’t think I’ll be able to sleep unless I walk. Okay?”
While they were frantically fleeing the air raid, she had realized some things that she had been ignoring.
She wasn’t wearing her support belt, and her dress was made of a thin material. She had intentionally bought clothes that were a bit large for her, but she was worried it was still plain to see.
I should tell him. I know that. But how do I spring it on him here, in this situation?
Just as she thought she was about to burst into tears from the guilt, Uncle Bill stood up, brushing himself off, and casually said, “Fine. You sure are a stubborn one.”
“I’m sorry, Uncle Bill.”
“What are you sorry for? Are you sure you’ll be all right?”
He looked down at her woolen socks with concern. She started walking confidently forward to reassure him. He laughed and nodded at her approvingly.

They slowly walked down the street, holding hands. Besides the square’s bell tower, the other buildings hadn’t suffered much damage. Layla commented that the Beloff fighter pilots were just as incompetent as the chef at the restaurant across from their apartment building, which made Bill laugh heartily. They both tried to keep smiling as much as they could.
“They won’t be back, so don’t worry,” he lied.
“Okay, I won’t worry,” she replied, joining in on the lie.
The crickets chirped in the darkness as they continued towards home. “Uncle Bill?”
“Yeah?”
“How about we name things that we like while we walk?” “Things we like?”
“Yeah. Thinking about things we like will make us feel better. Let’s take turns.”
“What’s this nonsense?”
“I’ll start. First for me is… you, Uncle Bill!” Her face seemed to glow under the starry sky.
“All right,” he replied, “then for me it’ll be… our family’s child.” “Child? You mean me? I’ve been all grown up for a long time now.” “All grown up? Phooey. You’ve still got a long way to go, kiddo.”
The quiet summer night was filled with the sound of various other things she liked, with which he was also quite familiar: birds… flowers… trees… ice cream… mystery novels… the first snow of winter.
Each time it was Bill’s turn, however, he kept returning to the same response: Layla… Layla… Layla.
She was the treasure of his life.
“That’s cheating, Uncle Bill! You can’t keep giving the same answer.”

Despite her complaint, that name was the only response he could honestly give.
He had heard the news of the air raid while walking home from work. As he frantically ran home and pulled Layla into his arms, he had realized that he felt like her parent.
As long as she survived, he didn’t mind dying. If the chance arose to give his life in order to save hers, he would happily take it without hesitation. As long as she could live a good life, he didn’t care if he lived or died.
His eyes shook as he looked at her. The calm, innocent look on her face made his heart sting even more.
Before she could look up and see his expression, he quickly raised his head and looked forward.
He had a very strange feeling, but he wasn’t sure why. Was it because of the cruel world that was trying to kill this poor girl? Was it because of himself, and his failure to be a decent father to her? Or was it because of his regret over fleeing Arvis without getting a chance to kill the duke…?
“Uncle Bill?” she said, squeezing his hand.
He snapped out of his daze and saw that they had made it to the apartment building. Not liking the nervous look that she was giving him, he forced another hearty laugh.
“It was just like you said, Layla. Talking about those silly things made me feel much better,” he said, lying once again.
“Me too, Uncle Bill.”
Luckily, she didn’t seem to realize he was lying. She smiled at him. As always, her smile was very pretty.







When word came that the advance cavalry battalion had secured the bridges and roads, an order was given to all brigades that had been divided into the north and the east to advance.
The infantry company led by Major Herhardt was tasked with breaching the center of the enemy fortress after an artillery bombardment. When the sound of the mortars breaking the fortress walls stopped, it was their cue to advance.
Matthias gazed at the sky ahead, which was full of dense clouds of dust. The sound of the huge siege mortars tearing down the walls of the fortress had been loud even from the distant location where his infantry were waiting. Each time a mortar shell flew and hit its target, another section vanished from the stone wall which had guarded the city for ages. Once the fortress fell, it would be their gateway straight into Sienne. They might be able to capture the city in as little as two weeks, or a month at most.
“I think they’ve just about finished!” a scout shouted as he looked through binoculars at the fortress under artillery fire.
The infantrymen started to feel tense as their time to fight drew close. At once, they all turned to look at their company commander. The heavy silence was soon pierced by the blaring note of a bugle, signaling the beginning of their assault.
With no shift in his facial expression, Matthias immediately started marching. His company’s mission was to head behind the fortress, annihilate

any enemy soldiers lying in ambush, and plant the imperial flag on the newly occupied territory. If their mission was a success, the next flag they planted would be in Sienne.
A faint smile appeared on Matthias’s lips as he muttered, Layla. However, his eyes remained serious as they looked southward towards the distant spot that he had to occupy.
Not long earlier, they had received word that Beloff’s combat planes had carried out an air raid on Sienne. The other Berg soldiers had rejoiced at the news of a successful raid, but Matthias’s heart had sunk.
Layla had to survive until he could find her and kill her. Until then, he didn’t want anyone touching her, whether they were an ally or an enemy, or even God Himself.
Thus, Matthias planned to go to her as quickly as possible, even if that meant becoming a monster, or a demon.




124.

One Goal.






The soldiers’ boots pounded in perfect rhythm as they ran across the bridge.
As he awaited the troops, who had split into two groups on either side of the fortress’s gates, Matthias climbed up onto a portion of the wall that was still standing. Standing amid the thick, smell of mortar smoke and enemy soldiers’ blood, he looked down at the town below.
He swiftly scanned the ruined roads and buildings, before halting his gaze on the town’s chapel. Its spire and roof had been blown off, but its walls were still standing strong, as if they had been protected from the mortar shells by God. It was of course a place that the deeply religious Lovitan soldiers adored, and likely many of them were hiding inside.
Stepping over the corpses of the enemy soldiers who had been killed in the artillery assault, he ran down the remains of the stone wall. At his command,

the scattered members of his company quickly and cautiously began to surround the chapel.
Matthias, who did not believe in God, pulled the pin from a grenade and tossed it through a broken stained-glass window, thus starting the battle. Right on cue, he heard screams from inside, and soon, panicked enemy soldiers began to flee from the building. Matthias raised his gun and took aim at them.
The ringing of the bell in the still-standing bell tower began to fill the streets, along with the sound of gunfire.







The museum shut down. This was only to be expected, and yet it came as a surprisingly large shock to Layla. This was less because she had lost her job, and more because it was a frightening sign that the war was reaching every corner of the city.
Without trying to, her mind kept entertaining terrifying possibilities. What if there was another air raid? What would happen if the Berg army really occupied Sienne? When would the war end?
Feeling despair at these unanswerable questions, she eventually fell asleep. She was awoken again by the feeling of Uncle Bill gently caressing her furrowed brow.
“Uncle Bill…?”
“Sorry, Layla. I wasn’t trying to wake you.’
“Oh, it’s fine” She smiled as she grabbed his hand before he could pull it away. “Did you get off work early? It’s not evening yet”

“I thought I’d stop by here on my lunch break. I brought something for you.’
He bashfully pointed at a paper bag he had set down on her bedside table.
Detecting the faint scent of fresh peaches, she started beaming. “You brought me a gift?!”
“A gift? It’s just some peaches,” he muttered, though it was clear that he was delighted by her reaction.
In the middle of work that morning, he had suddenly thought of Layla. Ever since the air raid, the museum had been closed, and she had been spending every day alone in the apartment. She hadn’t admitted it, but he could sense that she was very lonely and nervous.
He wished that he could do something, anything, for her-end the war, make her dreams come true, reunite her with her love. He would do all those things if he were able to, but all he could do at this point was bring her a few peaches.
“I want you to eat one of these while I’m gone. Got it?” “Yes.”
“Don’t just say that. I really want you to start eating well again. The more chaotic things get around here, the more important it is for you to eat plenty of food to keep up your strength. I want you to eat lots and lots, like a cow. Okay?” he asked, raising his voice. He had noticed that she was looking especially gaunt today.
“Of course!” she replied, giggling as she nodded profusely.
The expression on her face reminded him of when she was a little girl.
“I don’t know if I can eat quite as much as a cow, but I’ll eat a lot, Uncle Bill.”

“Good. That’s a good girl. When I come back this evening, I’ll bring lots more delicious food for you.”
“Wow! Are we having a party?”
“A party? Well… sure. Let’s have a party.” He smiled as he petted her hair.
“I have something to tell you, so I’ll wait until our party tonight.”
“All right! I have something to tell you, too,” she replied, firmly grasping his hand to steel her resolve.
She was planning to tell him everything tonight. She didn’t want to chicken out anymore.
“I’ll be back in no time, kiddo. Get some rest and I’ll see you this evening.” Smiling brightly, he turned to leave.
Just as he was about to close the door to her room, she shouted, “Uncle Bill! Uncle Bill!”
Startled, he quickly turned back around, and saw that she was giggling like a mischievous child.
“Just kidding! Stay safe out there, and I’ll see you this evening. Uncle Bill.” Having put her glasses back on to see his face, she waved goodbye.
“I see you’re still a little kid,” he grumbled before waving back at her. “See you soon, kiddo.”







Another bullet hit its mark. The enemy officer, who had been shooting at Berg soldiers until a moment earlier, collapsed to the ground before the smoke had

stopped rising from the barrel of his gun. Major Herhardt lowered his gun. His face showed no particular expression.
’’Over there! They’re running away!” shouted a soldier who had been searching the streets after the battle.
Two soldiers in Lovitan uniforms were fleeing as fast as they could towards the street corner. Noticing that there was a warhorse waiting at the stop towards which they were running, Matthias narrowed his eyes.
Rather than chasing after the soldiers, he raised his gun again and pointed it in the direction of the horse. More precisely, he aimed at the window of the store the horse was standing in front of.
Startled by the sound of the glass breaking behind it, the horse started galloping away. As the soldiers who had planned to make their getaway on that horse floundered, Berg soldiers caught up and surrounded them.
Matthias gave a hand signal to tell them to wait, and then he started approaching. There was a middle-aged man looking at the sky with a distraught look on his face. He was a general and was the commander of the fortress’s defense force. Stopping in front of the group. Matthias nodded courteously to the enemy general. “This battle is over.” he said in Lovitan, which he spoke fluently but with a thick accent. “Wouldn’t you agree?”
The general let out a deep sigh. If he surrendered, the battle would be over. The remaining Lovitan soldiers would become prisoners, but at least their lives would be spared. However, if they let the Berg soldiers take the fortress, what would become of Sienne?
He mulled over this dilemma, even while he knew he had no real choice. Meanwhile, the officer who had been quietly seething next to him took out his gun.
Noticing this, a Berg soldier shouted, “Major!”

A gunshot rang out.








Even though she had no appetite, Layla got up and ate one of the peaches Uncle Bill had bought for her. As soon as the sweet juice touched her taste buds, her hunger came rushing back to her.
Should I eat another one? Or should I save it ?
She looked pensively at the basket containing the remaining peaches. When she finally made up her mind and picked up another peach, she heard a familiar, booming noise outside. Startled, she dropped the peach and it rolled under the table.
In her shock, she vainly thought. No. it was just my imagination I’m just hearing things because I’ve been so afraid…
Before she could finish her thought, however, she started screaming instinctively.
The explosions were getting so close, she could feel the vibration in her body. It seemed like the floor was starling to shake, and soon she heard the sound of objects in the apartment falling and breaking.
She crawled under the table and hunched up into a ball. Her heart was pounding furiously.
Instead of sobbing with fear, she tried to remember what she had learned: “When there’s an air rates, leave the apartment building and seek shelter in the nearest basement”

I can do it, she thought. I’ll go to the church basement again, just like Uncle Bill said. Yes. I just have to run out of here and make it to there, and I’ll be safe.
As the sound of windows breaking across the street started to ring out, she emerged from under the table and ran frantically towards the apartment’s front door. Out of the corner of her eye, she could see combat planes flying in the sky outside. The roofs of the buildings across the street were half destroyed.
Gritting her teeth, she started hurrying down the stairway with quivering legs.







In the silence following the gunshot, one man fell to the ground, dead. Dark blood flowed out of his head, staining the dirt. His still-open eyes looked like bottomless pits. His gun, which he hadn’t had the chance to fire, was soon soaked in his own blood.
Matthias looked down at him. There was no expression on his face, which was splattered with the blood of the man he had just killed. When the smoke had cleared from the barrel of his gun, he put it back in its holster, and then looked at the general again. Several of the other soldiers gulped nervously.
“This battle is over,” Matthias said, repeating his earlier statement exactly. He extended his hand towards the frightened-looking general, as if he wanted to shake his hand.
“Dear God…” the general muttered, looking up at the sky before shutting his eyes. After a moment, he opened his eyes again and resignedly placed his

baton in the outstretched hand of young Berg officer standing before him.
A general’s surrender—it was an official acknowledgment of defeat. It wouldn’t be long before a Berg flag would be flying on the Sienne shore.
The general’s bloodshot eyes filled with tears. Matthias, who had been watching him from a step away, bowed politely. The Lovitan general returned the gesture.
“Sorry about him,” Matthias said, glancing down at the dead officer before turning away. It was quite a dry, calm tone with which to talk about a person he had just killed.
Matthias and the other soldiers with him took the Lovitan general, who was now their prisoner, and entered the town square.
The rest of the Berg soldiers cheered loudly when they saw them. To Matthias, however, the whole thing felt like something from a distant world that had nothing to do with him Victory, glory, honor—they all felt meaningless to him now, as they had since the war had begun. From the start, he had only ever had one goal, and victory was merely a means of reaching that goal.
Layla, he muttered. Little by little, he was getting closer to her.
He raised the enemy general’s baton and declared victory. As soon as he did, the Lovita flag was lowered from the flagpole in the square, and the flag of the Berg Empire was raised in its place.
Even as the enemy general was offering his congratulations, Matthias’s mind thought of no-thing but Layla. Even if he had lost her forever, she would be forever his.
Layla was safe. He was sure of it. Of course, it was ridiculous for him to have such certainty. They were in the middle of a war. Anyone could die at any

moment. No one could guarantee the safety of a fragile, defenseless woman living in a city that was being constantly bombarded by air raids.
However, it wasn’t just any woman, it was Layla. His Layla. Thus, she was safe. It was a matter of faith for him. He needed no evidence to know it was true.
As the citizens started to come out from hiding and gather in the town square one by one, Matthias announced that the occupation forces would guarantee the safety of the citizens, as long as they didn’t commit any hostile actions or put up pointless resistance.
Standing at the edge of the podium, he looked up at the evening sky as a flock of birds took flight. A faint smile appeared on his bloodstained face.
They were almost to Sienne.




125.

It Will Never Stop Being True.





Even once it was clear that the bombing had stopped, no one was eager to go outside.
As if the previous air raid had been just a warning, this time Beloff’s planes had showered the city with much more ferocity. From the basement, there was no way to know what things looked like up at ground level, but it was clear that the damage would far exceed a single fallen bell tower like last time.
A baby started to cry, breaking the tense silence in the dark, stuffy basement. A number of small children then started crying as well, soon followed by the sound of adults sighing in exasperation.
Layla rubbed her cold hands together and tried to catch her breath. Her stomach, which felt like it was tied up in a knot, gradually started to relax as well. Just as she was regaining her composure, she became suddenly aware of an intense pain in her knee.

She couldn’t remember clearly how she had gotten to where she was now. After barely managing to make it out of the apartment building, she had found the street packed with people panicking over the attack. Someone had then bumped into her and she had fallen to the ground, losing her glasses. Just as she had found them and put them on again, another deafening explosion had rung out from a nearby street.
Ignoring the blood flowing from her scraped knee, she had started running, not stopping until she had safely reached the church basement. It wasn’t clear to her how much time had passed since then, or what had happened in that time.
“We can go back up now! The planes are gone!” shouted a young man who had gone upstairs to see the state of things at ground level.
Even after hearing this, however, no one moved. After a short feeling of relief, the basement was filled with even louder cries and sighs.
Layla was the first to get up. She walked over towards the staircase, where a dim lamp was burning.
I have to find Uncle Bill.
This was the first thought that came to her mind when she regained her senses.
“Are you going to be all right?” a man asked her, noticing that she was alone.
She nodded and hurried up to the street. The smell of dust and explosives permeated the clear night air. Smoke was drifting from the remains of ruined buildings all around. It appeared that the seaside village and harbor had been hit even more heavily than the city.
She took off her glasses with quivering hands. After rubbing her eyes vigorously, she put her glasses back on, but the scene before her had not

changed at all. One of the lenses of her glasses was cracked, but she didn’t mind. In this moment, she preferred not seeing things with too much clarity.
Uncle Bill probably took shelter somewhere around the harbor. Should I go look for him there? Or would it be better to go back to the apartment and wait for him there?
After dithering for a moment, she started walking back to the apartment.
She was missing a shoe, but she didn’t care.
If I can just find Uncle Bill, everything will be all right. So I just have to get to- “Layla!”
Just as she was drawing close to the apartment building, she heard a familiar voice. Turning to look, she saw Allen, the man who had helped her and Uncle Bill find their apartment. He worked at the harbor with Uncle Bill, but for some reason he was alone.
“Where’s Uncle Bill? Did he go up into the apartment already?” she asked, limping towards him. “You and he must have evacuated together, right? I was worried, but I’m so glad to see you. Uncle Bill is up in the apartment, right? He must be so worried about me. I’d better go see him.” She was speaking faster than normal.
Allen finally broke his silence, saying, “Layla, don’t be too shocked when I tell you this.” His voice cracked as soon as he started speaking.
The world seemed to start spinning, but Layla kept her eyes fixed on Allen. “Bill is… well, he’s in the hospital.” The tears he had been holding back
started to fall. “You’ve got to go to him, Layla. Right now!







Hearing a vague buzzing in his ears, Bill opened his eyes. For some reason, his vision was blurry, and he felt like he was submerged in deep water. Eventually he would feel horrible pain throughout his whole body, as if all of his bones were broken. At this point, though, he could hardly feel a thing.
“Uncle Bill! Uncle Bill!”
The hazy buzzing noise in his ears was pierced by the sound of Layla breathlessly sobbing and calling his name. He turned his head and looked in the direction the sound was coming from. Strangely, he wasn’t able to move any other part of his body, but he could manage to turn his head.
Layla… Why is she crying like that? He wanted to console her, but he couldn’t get his voice to come out properly. Seeing people in white clothes busily walking to and fro, he could tell that he was in a hospital. Oh, yeah, maybe I did get hurt a little.
His memories abruptly cut off after the explosion at the harbor warehouse.
He had been carrying a box of mortar shells to a warship. There had been an evacuation order, and all the workers had started running frantically. Going with the flow of the crowd, he had run straight across the warehouse. Just as he thought he had made it outside, a deafening noise had rung out, as if the sky were falling. It had reminded him of the noise the mansion’s generator had made when it exploded.
If only I hadn’t broken that damn thing, I wouldn’t have made Layla do the things she did, he had suddenly thought with regret. He had then felt a great

deal of pain, before losing consciousness. He had no other memories after that point, other than the vague sensation of being buried underneath something.
Don’t cry, Layla.
Layla was now struggling and wailing. He could see that she was holding his hand, but he couldn’t feel a thing.
I’d better get up, he decided. However, no matter how he tried, his body wouldn’t cooperate with his will.
Meanwhile, the doctor ran over and pulled Layla away from him, then said something to her, after which she froze.
Stupid doctor, what sort of nonsense is he scaring her with?
The image of Layla’s frightened, no longer crying face painfully imprinted itself into Bill’s wavering consciousness.
Just because he’s a doctor doesn’t mean he knows anything. It’s all right, Layla.
This is nothing.
He opened his lips to laugh, but all that came out was a faint moan and the sound of his labored breathing.







“I m sorry.”
This was the doctor’s response after Layla desperately urged him to operate on Bill right away, crying as she promised that she would find a way to pay the surgery bill.
“There’s no point in operating on him in his current state. It’s a miracle that he’s held on to this point at all.”

What is he saying? Layla shook her head, unable to understand what the doctor was getting at.
After being taken to the hospital, she had found Uncle Bill lying in a bed, covered with blood and bruises. A doctor had told her that he had been severely injured when the waterfront warehouse he was working in collapsed during the air raid.
She couldn’t believe what she was seeing. Nor could she accept what the doctor was telling her.
