seemed to be infused with the fresh scent of flowers and herbs. Every single day with her had been beautiful.
I can’t let this happen. He twisted his body with all his might. I can’t leave her alone in this cruel world. I can’t…
“Uncle Bill!”
A few men in white clothes ran over and grabbed him. Layla put her little hand to his cheek. Tears flowed from his eyes, wetting her hand like rain on a flower petal.
“I was so happy when you wanted to be my family, Uncle Bill. I was full of joy, every day. Truly. We were always a real family, and we will keep being one, right?”
Bill’s eyes filled with tears. Of course. Of course we will.
He wanted to respond to her, but her face was gradually growing hazy.
“If we’re born again in another life, I’ll find you and become part of your family again. It will just be a brief parting, and then a reunion.” She pressed her lips against his bushy beard. Amid her sobs, she whispered, “I love you, Dad.”
He was barely hanging onto consciousness now, but he heard the last thing she whispered loud and clear. As he stopped his painful struggling, a sad smile spread across his bloodstained lips.
Suddenly he remembered a leisurely summer evening, perhaps the same year she had first come to him. They had walked through the woods and along the riverside. The breeze had shaken the little girl’s hair and clothes as she walked alongside him, reciting the names of flowers and trees that he had taught her.
She had excitedly run a short distance ahead, picked up a shiny pebble and a white feather, and slowly walked back to his side. Then she had furtively

placed her hand into his huge hand. Her tiny hand had felt very warm as it struggled to wrap around his, only making it around a couple of his fingers.
She had looked up at him with hesitation, but when he had chuckled, she had started smiling shyly and squeezed his hand more tightly. They had continued to walk along the riverside until the purple twilight had given way to darkness.
Yes, that’s when I knew it.
All of a sudden, he realized something that he had known in the back of his mind for a long time, but had been too embarrassed to admit to himself.
Just then, as if God had changed His mind, Bill was able to utter a single sentence: “I… love you.”
There we go. That wasn’t so hard now, was it?
Along with a deep sense of relief, he could then feel the energy quickly draining from his body.
When I wake up, first thing I’ll do is go buy a bunch of tasty food. We did say we were going to have a party. Her and me. I have so much to tell her, too. Maybe we’ll stay up all night, drinking and chatting and having a good time. Well, maybe not drinking. She can’t have alcohol anymore, so I’d better get some apple juice or something instead. We can clink our glasses together, just like the day we first became a family.
Smiling, Bill whispered again, “I love you, my daughter.”
When I wake up, maybe I’ll be embarrassed I said that, but oh well. It’s not so bad. After all, it’s true. And it will never stop being true.




126.

A Beautiful Day.






News about Sienne’s fall spread quickly throughout the city. Posters announcing the imminent arrival of the occupying forces were posted
in various spots.
Layla hurried towards a street corner where a murmuring crowd of people was gathered in front of a notice board. The message on the poster was more or less what she had expected. It indicated that all weapons and communication equipment were to be rendered to the authorities, and that it was strictly forbidden for anyone to slander or attack the occupation forces. The overall message was that unconditional surrender and compliance were the only way to survive. It also indicated that no one was allowed to leave the city without a pass authorized by the general of the occupying forces.
The others in the crowd were clearly agitated, showing signs of fear, despair, and anger, but Layla was in a daze. She felt nothing particular about

what she had just read. The war felt irrelevant, as did everything in her life.
After being pushed aside by other people eager to read the notice, she trudged back home. She had been in this state ever since Uncle Bill’s funeral. Each day had passed by indifferently, as if she were dreaming. She felt like a dried-up plant in a discarded flowerpot.
She knew she couldn’t go on like this, but she couldn’t help it. It took all her willpower just to muster the strength to visit Uncle Bill’s grave once a day.
She plodded up the steps and entered her apartment, then stood for a long while, feeling the breeze blowing in through the windows, which had been broken in the air raid.
The sky was clear and blue. There was yellow dust floating amid the crisp autumn sunlight. By all appearances it was a beautiful day, but she felt nothing but utter loneliness.
To resist acknowledging the fact that she was once again completely alone in life, she shouted, “Uncle Bill!” After a moment, she forced a smile and shouted again even louder: “Uncle Bill!!”
She imagined hearing his heavy steps as he walked up the stairs and opened the door. For a moment, she could almost hear him laughing and greeting her: “Hi, Layla!” However, the apartment was filled with nothing but oppressive silence.
Suddenly she missed the fallen bell tower. Its deafening ringing would have made things a little more bearable. In fact, any sound would have been preferable to this silence.
She felt like running out of the apartment but lacked the energy to do so. Instead, she went to her room and flopped down on the bed. Her bedroom window was the only one that hadn’t completely broken in the air raid, but it had been cracked, and it was only a matter of time before it would break.

I need to go buy some new window panes. But is that even possible in the current situation? What will I do when winter rolls around?
She lay motionless like a doll, staring into space as thought after thought passed through her mind.
Then it occurred to her that she was out of food as well. Her last meal had been some stale bread that she had choked down two days earlier. Suddenly she was hit with a wave of intense hunger, but she lacked the willpower to get up.
The threat of the occupying army’s imminent arrival had thrown the city into disarray. On the way back from the cemetery she had passed by the grocery store. It had looked like a battlefield, with desperate citizens fighting over the little that was remained. She had no intention of entering into that fray, but she also knew that it would be irresponsible to simply starve. It was then that she remembered what Allen had given to her.
The weekend after Bill’s funeral, Allen and his family had left Sienne. He had told Layla that they were headed to the capital, which was still safe, before the arrival of the Berg army would make travel impossible.
He had apologized to her repeatedly, telling her how sorry he was for leaving her alone in Sienne. However, Layla didn’t blame him. She was just the adopted daughter of a distant relative of his who had shown up out of the blue one day. The kindness he had shown her already was enough for her see him as a good person. She didn’t expect him to take her along with him when he was struggling to escape with his family.
Anyway, she had no desire to leave the city. No matter where she went, she could never be by Uncle Bill’s side like she wanted. Seeing Allen unable to lift his head and face her made her feel even more guilty.

Before leaving Sienne, he had stopped by the apartment to bid farewell to her, and had given her a large bundle of necessities —food, fabric, and various odds and ends. She had felt very grateful for this and had thanked him profusely. He had stared back at her for a moment, rubbing his bloodshot eyes, then left. With that, yet another good person had departed from her life.
She took a jar of pickled apricots from the bundle. She carefully avoided looking at the small box full of Uncle Bill’s belongings from the warehouse. She felt like if she were to look inside that box, things would really be over.
Sitting down at the kitchen table, she ravenously ate all the apricots in the jar, not even thinking to move them to a dish first. Wiping the sticky juice from her mouth with the back of her hand, she started laughing bitterly. She was still hungry and sleepy, and she hated herself for still wanting to live.







Sienne’s last line of defense fell far more easily than expected. The fortress had been the last thread of hope for the Lovita army. Once the soldiers learned that it had been destroyed, they lost all their morale. After conducting a formal defensive battle, they retreated, giving up a key strategic point in the south. The decision to retreat was a wise one. If they had continued fighting to protect the already-crumbling front line, the capital’s defenses would have been put at risk of collapse.
The Berg troops passed through the wide- open city gates and advanced towards the square. The cavalry led the way, followed by a procession of military vehicles carrying the general and other commanding officers.

Sienne’s mayor stood meekly at the gates, greeting the occupying army with a white flag. The city’s safety had been guaranteed as long as they accepted the terms of the occupying army and surrendered unconditionally. This seemed to be their only option.
Matthias took in the unfamiliar city from his seat in the back of a military vehicle following behind the cavalry. All around there was rubble from buildings destroyed in the air raid, and yet Sienne seemed generally calm and peaceful. Perhaps this was an illusion created by the clear sunlight, and the fact that the color palette of Lovita’s environment was much brighter than Berg’s.
The army proceeded slowly through the city, as if they were appreciating the spoils of the war, before coming to a stop at the square.
The general got out of his vehicle and went up to the platform that had been erected in front of City Hall. The other commanding officers followed in a line behind him. Matthias walked with leisurely steps in this line, loosely holding his officers cap. When he reached the top step up to the platform, he gently placed the cap on his head. Dressed in his blue-gray uniform, he looked clean and elegant, not at all befitting the brutal nicknames he had been given since joining the war.
In a powerful voice, the general began his lengthy speech. It was a ritual he had repeated each time they had occupied an enemy city, but today he was showing much more passion than usual. He seemed quite excited about having reached the goal ahead of schedule, but Matthias wasn’t especially happy about this. However, under such a beautiful autumn sky, he felt like he could accept anything.
His heart was full of a generosity and composure he had not felt previously. It had been like this since the moment he had first set foot in Sienne. And why shouldn’t it be? This was where Layla was, after all.

Standing with proper military posture, he looked down from the platform at the countless people gathered below. While his eyes looked fierce, as though he were a predator searching for prey, his lips held a tender smile, as though he were facing his love.
This contradictory expression seemed to be mildly disturbing the officers around him, but he didn’t care. In fact, he seemed completely uninterested in how they were feeling. It was a strange atmosphere. He felt like he had sunken into an abyss, and yet also felt vaguely elevated.
After the long speech finally concluded, the crowd started to disperse from the square. Removing his officer’s cap again and holding it in his hand, Matthias slowly descended from the platform. His slicked- back hair was as flawlessly neat as his uniform.
Having regained his health on the battlefield, he was back to his usual appearance. The fact that he had entered the war in a weakened state and then grown stronger and healthier day by day had greatly contributed to his notoriety.
Just as he had reached the bottom of the stairs, an aide came running up to him, shouting, “Major, I found it!” He had a look on his face like that of a proud student who had just completed his homework.
Matthias slowly turned to face him.
As the major’s shadow fell upon him, the aide flinched and took a step back. Viewed from afar, he looked slender, but up close it was clear that he was not only tall but imposingly muscular. The deep blue of his eyes always caused a chill to run down the aide’s spine, just as it was doing this time. “S-so, as you requested…”
“Where is she?” Matthias asked, urging him on in a relaxed tone of voice.

The aide hurriedly handed him a note with an address written on it. “This is the location of the home of Layla Llewellyn, the young woman who moved here from Berg last spring.”





Layla finally got out of bed in the early afternoon, after lying there like a corpse all morning. She ate a small amount of boiled beans with some stale rye bread, and drank some hot water with sugar. It wasn’t enough to fill her belly, but it was enough to get her moving.
By the time she had bathed and gotten dressed, the sun was shining warmly. Standing in front of the broken window, she looked down at the street. It was unusually quiet, perhaps because the occupying forces had entered the city. Even though their safety had been guaranteed, the citizens of Sienne still feared the enemy army.
She put on some thick socks, then put on her shoes and tied the laces securely. It was warm outside, so she decided not to wear a shawl. As she emerged from the apartment building, her steps were as calm as ever.
The sunlight, which seemed especially bright today, dazzled her eyes as she stepped out onto the empty street.
Would it be better not to go? she wondered, noticing that there was no one else around. After deliberating for a moment, however, she started walking.
She was more afraid of the suffocating silence inside her apartment than the occupying forces. Normally, the noise of the street made her feel better, but not today.

It’s going to be all right, she told herself. To calm herself down, she tried to think about the things she liked. However, she quickly realized that she was unable to bring their names to her mind.
Just as she was starting to chuckle about this, she heard the sound of footsteps approaching.
Not seeing anyone’s shadow but her own, she quickly turned her head in the direction of the sound. There was a military officer slowly walking towards her. The sun was behind him, so she could only make out his silhouette. When he saw her, he stopped walking, and the street was silent again.
Is there a curfew or something? she wondered. Her dazed, distracted mind soon regained its focus, and she started to feel afraid.
For whatever reason, the officer just stood there, staring at her. Even though he was quite far away and she couldn’t see his face, she could feel his intense gaze.
Her heart started pounding uneasily and her breath quickened when she realized the source of her fear: there was something familiar about him. As impossible as it seemed, the officer standing at the end of the road felt very familiar to her.
Suddenly the face of a man she hadn’t thought about in a while flashed into her mind. This greatly increased her fear, and she turned and started walking quickly away from the dark figure. Watching her hurry away, the officer started walking again.




127.

Like a Child, or Like a Demon.






Matthias moved at an unhurried pace. The moment he had first caught sight of Layla, his heart had started pounding furiously. With each step
he took, however, it had calmed down, eventually slowing below its original rate.
He recognized her immediately. There was no way that the woman standing in front of him was anyone else. He was certain he was looking at his Layla.
Just as he was about to call out her name, she turned around and started fleeing him. Yes, once again she was fleeing him.
He found her vain attempt at escape cute, so he decided to play along. He was going to need some time anyway, if he didn’t want to simply snap her slender neck at once.

Because she was unable to move very fast, chasing after her felt like a leisurely stroll. He felt like he was walking on air, much like he had felt during the spring when his head had been clouded with sleeping pills.
She paused momentarily when she reached the end of the alleyway she had fled down. Beyond the trembling woman, he could see a long road, a sandy beach, and the emerald-colored ocean. It was even more beautiful than he had imagined.







Feeling flustered, Layla stepped out onto the beach. Her feet sank into the sand as she walked, making it difficult to retain her balance, but she knew she couldn’t stop. Nor could she look over her shoulder.
The officer continued to chase after her at an unhurried pace. She had thought that once she emerged from the alleyway onto the beach there might be someone who could help her, but now she realized that was a foolish thought. The beach was as empty as the street, and was silent other than the lazy waves lapping at the shore, and the occasional cry of a seagull.
What could the man want with her? She had no idea. If she had broken the occupying force’s rules, she would have expected him to simply give her a warning or arrest her. Instead, he just kept chasing after her, not saying a word. That was the most confusing and frightening thing of all.
If he wanted to, he could have easily caught her by now. He was clearly a hardy, strong soldier, and she wasn’t even capable of running. So why hadn’t

he caught her yet? She felt like she was about to burst into tears. Why wasn’t he catching up to her?
Her desire to look back at him was overpowered by an even stronger desire to avoid seeing his face. She was frightened by the prospect that her absurd hunch about his identity might prove to be correct, but she was equally frightened by that it might turn out to be a delusion.
She had felt this way many times since coming to Sienne. Whenever she saw a tall man with black hair, her heart would sink as she momentarily believed it was the duke. Trembling, she would flee. However, it had always turned out to be her imagination, every single time. This time was likely no different. All she had to do was look over her shoulder and check. For all she knew, there was no one chasing after her at all.
Eventually she reached the wet sand at the edge of the shore. There was nowhere left for her to escape, short of walking into the ocean.
She came to a stop and looked down at the sea foam touching her shoes. Oddly, the realization that she had nowhere left to escape made her feel more calm. She knew well how to wake up from a dream. All she had to do was open her eyes.
Praying internally, she raised her eyes to the distant sky and slowly turned around. The man was standing on the beach, keeping his distance as he gazed at her. The autumn sunlight shone brightly on his face, making his identity impossible to miss.
It was Matthias von Herhardt, her brutal but handsome nightmare.







For a long while, the gentle waves continued to flow in and out as the two of them stood motionless, staring at each other.
A soft, cool breeze started to blow, making the hem of his trench coat flutter before passing onto her as she stood with her back to the ocean.
He took in the sight with relish. Her blonde hair, fluttering like a beautiful pair of wings, her trembling, tear-filled green eyes, and…
After slowly crawling over the rest of her body, his eyes came to a sudden halt. Her flimsy dress had been blown against her body by the breeze, making her shape distinctly visible. While most of her body was skinnier than before, her belly looked noticeably larger.
Cocking his head slightly, he narrowed his eyes, keeping them fixed on the same spot. As if to confirm that he wasn’t imagining things, the breeze started blowing even harder. Her belly, which had always been completely flat before, was now unmistakably plump.
As the smile faded from his lips, the defenseless Layla put her slender, trembling arms over her belly. She reflexively started to back away, but there was still nowhere for her to go.
Once he was certain of what he was seeing, he started to laugh. He could feel his mind gradually growing clear, as if he had just awoken from a long sleep. Suddenly, everything that had brought him to this moment—the war breaking out, his enlistment, and the almost deranged passion with which he

had fought—felt like a faintly remembered dream. None of it seemed real. He felt like he had been floating along in a river, carried by the current.
For the first time since the day he had sunk in the Schulter River, he felt like he had finally come out of the water, into the place where the glittering sunlight shone against the rustling forest leaves, and the birds sang their beautiful songs.
Layla…
As he whispered that name, his smile grew more relaxed. Layla… My little bird, Layla…
With his hands behind his back, he stood up straighter. That elegant, gentlemanly posture stood in stark contrast to the delighted smile on his face. As she beheld this, Layla’s eyes started to tremble.
Her mouth went dry, and her breathing quickened. She could feel the baby inside her start to kick, as if it was frightened as well. All she could do, however, was desperately keep trying to hide her belly with her arms.
There was no way she was seeing things or having a nightmare—Matthias was standing in front of her. He had somehow come to this place that she thought he would never find.
“Hello, Layla,” he whispered, smiling. His smile was like that of a child, or a demon.





“Shouldn’t you try to stop him? You said it was a pregnant woman, right?”
The soldier’s voice quivered as he whispered his question to the aide. The aide, who had been nervously biting his lip, frowned and replied, “Of course I

want to stop him. But how am I supposed to do that? Break into the major’s room and rescue her?”
“Maybe not that, but you’ve got to do something. What have you gotten yourself into?”
“I don’t know! I really don’t know, damn it!”
The aide grew more and more distraught as the conversation continued.
Major Herhardt had asked him to find out a woman’s address, and he had readily complied. He had figured that she was simply an acquaintance or distant relative of the major. Little had he suspected what was really going on!
Rumors that the major had abducted a woman spread quickly throughout the unit. At first, everyone assumed it was a joke. While he had gained several brutal nicknames during the war, and was even jokingly rumored to be descended from a vampire, Major Herhardt’s reputation was clean when it came to women. In fact, many of the other soldiers had wondered if he lacked desire for women altogether.
Thus it was incomprehensible that he would openly abduct a woman from the occupied area and drag her back to his room. However, it soon became abundantly clear that this ridiculous scenario was real.
Carrying a woman in his arms, Major Herhardt arrived back at the hotel on the square that had been designated as the officers’ quarters. He was moving with confidence, as if he didn’t care at all about all the people watching him. Wrapped in the major’s coat, the woman was soaked from head to foot, as if she had been underwater.
She was so small, she had looked like a child at first. Viewed up close, however, she had luckily turned out to be an adult. However, it was still quite disturbing that the major was lusting after a woman in what looked like an

advanced state of pregnancy. Especially considering that she struggled and resisted the whole way to his room.
Unable to ignore her frightened cries for help, the aide had mustered all his courage and stepped in front of the major, blocking his way. Once there, however, he had felt too intimated to say anything. All that the major had said to him was “Move,’’ but that single word was enough send a chill down the aide’s spine. After hesitating for a moment, he had stepped aside, and Major Herhardt had continued on to his room, the door of which he had then shut forcefully and locked.
That was all that the aide had witnessed directly. It wasn’t hard to guess what had happened after that, but he didn’t feel like entertaining such unpleasant thoughts.
“Should we report him to a superior officer?”
“You really think they’d do anything about it? He’s a Herhardt.” “That’s true…”
Their conversation started to fizzle out without a conclusion.
“He’s never shown any interest in other women. Why is he suddenly obsessed with this one?” the aide asked, overcome with guilt.







Meanwhile, a medic entered the mess hall. It was Kyle Ettman. He had just returned from a field hospital, and he had not yet heard the news that had

turned the entire unit upside down. Sitting down at the table, he noticed the agitated look on the aide’s face, and calmly asked, “Hey, what’s the matter?”
“Come to think of it, you know the major, don’t you? Has he always been like that?”
“Like what?”
“The noble Duke Herhardt just abducted a pregnant woman and dragged her to his room. It’s been causing quite a stir around here.”
“What the hell are you talking about?” Kyle asked. He looked very upset, just as the other soldiers had looked when the aide had first told them about what happened.
“Judging by your surprise, I guess he’s not usually like this?” the aide replied.
“What’s gotten into him, then? Did he lose his mind on the battlefield? If I had known what he was planning, I would’ve lied and said I couldn’t find her. I feel like it’s my fault.”
“What did you do?” Kyle asked.
“As soon as we got here, he gave me the name of some woman and asked me to find out her address. Who knew he was planning to do something like that? I mean, who is she? Why is he acting like that over her?”
As Kyle listened to the disturbing things the aide was telling him, a cold look fell over his eyes. “That woman, the woman the major had you find… Do you remember her name?”
“Of course I do. I just found her earlier today. Her name is Lay-” “Layla,” Kyle quietly said, cutting him off.
Everyone’s eyes widened.
“Layla Llewellyn,” Kyle said more loudly. His voice was starting to shake, as were his fists.

“Huh? How did you know that?”
Kyle almost stopped breathing when he saw the look of surprise on the aide’s face. So that’s why the duke was obsessed with Sienne. Because Layla was here. I can’t believe it. But it must be true.
“Hey! Kyle! Say something!”
“That bastard…” Kyle muttered hatefully. Then, all of a sudden, he rose from his seat and started running upstairs before anyone had time to react.




128.

A Heavenly Yet Hellish Time.






After a flurry of activity, silence descended on the hotel room. Knowing that there was nothing she could do, Layla hid herself in this silence,
while Matthias continued to gaze at her with an amused look on his face. “Wh-why… why are you here?” she asked in a shaky voice.
She wasn’t dreaming. She had no doubt about this fact anymore. Even the worst nightmare would pale in comparison to what she was now experiencing. There was only one person in the whole world capable of making her waking life more nightmarish than a nightmare, and that was Matthias von Herhardt. Thus, she was certain that this was reality. He had come and found her.
“Why am I here?” he asked, still smiling at her. “I came here to kill you.”
The cheerful-looking expression on his face as he whispered this gave her goosebumps. She stared back at him, too frightened to move a muscle.

“You know better than anyone that I don’t accept deals that I get nothing out of, Layla. If you’re going back on our deal, you leave me no other option.”
Watching with amusement as she trembled speechlessly, the duke let out a chuckle. His gleaming blue eyes lent a chilling quality to his laughter.
“Of course, I hadn’t anticipated this wonderful surprise,” he added, slowly lowering his narrowed eyes to her round belly. “I must admit, it does give me some pause.” He stood up and walked in front of her.
“Don’t touch me! I’m not your mistress anymore!” she shouted, coldly pushing his hand away from her cheek before wrapping her arms around her belly to hide it.
He shifted his gaze from her arms, which looked very fragile and slender, to her green eyes, which were filling with tears. Those eyes looked just like they had looked the last time he had seen her, before she had abandoned him and run away.
Gently pouting, he put his hand against her cheek again and said, “Layla, if you shout like that, won’t it startle our baby?”
Shuddering, she pushed his hand away again and replied, “It’s n-not your baby!”
“Really?” he scoffed.
“I have… a husband here! I got married…. Here in Lovita.”
He quietly watched as she struggled to come up with something to say. She looked back at him with a determined look in her eyes. This is getting to be very interesting, he thought. Well, it was already quite interesting back at the beach.
Nodding, he switched on the lamp on the small table next to the sofa. The room, which had been dark because the curtains were drawn, was suffused with a dim light. He walked back in front of her, and she reflexively leaned further back into the seat and hunched her shoulders.

He slowly closed and opened his eyes as he gazed at her face, which was illuminated by warm light of the lamp. Her moist eyes were red and trembling. She couldn’t have deceived a child, and yet she was openly lying to him.
“Who is he?” he asked, cocking his head slightly. “Your husband, I mean.”
Her lips started to twitch as a look of intense deliberation came over her face. Behind the cracked lenses of her glasses, her eyes were trembling nervously as well.
“Think carefully before you answer,” he said, touching the hair that was covering her cheek. “If you offer up the name of some man at random, Layla…” Looking into her frightened eyes, he slowly brushed her hair from her cheek. “That man will die.”
She shivered at the softness of his touch as he made this cold-hearted pronouncement.
“Because I will kill him.”
He gently clutched her chin and lifted it up. Forced to make eye contact with him again, she started biting her lip.
“It’s not your baby!” she shouted, letting a torrent of emotions rise from deep inside her. “My baby doesn’t have a father.”
“What?”
“It’s my baby! Just mine! No one else is involved!” He sneered at her, which made her even more angry.
“Layla, I think you’re a bit too obsessed with the idea of being a saint,” he said, chuckling. “What happened, did you conceive through the Holy Spirit?”
“Is it God’s baby, then?”
“I guess I’m your god, then. What an honor.”
He chuckled a little louder. Meanwhile, his eyes remained coldly fixed on her face.

He didn’t need any further proof. The moment the wind had blown her dress against her body and he had seen the outline of her belly, he was certain that the baby was his. There wasn’t the slightest doubt in his mind. The woman who had abandoned him was carrying his baby.
As he held her distraught face, he gradually put more strength into his grip.
Then he started caressing her cheek, and whispered, “Layla…”
The tips of his fingers trembled slightly as he gently ran them across her lips, nose, and the edges of her eyes.
The unfamiliar sensation caused her to look up at him again. Just then, there was an incredibly loud banging sound at the door.
“Layla! Are you in there? Layla! Layla!” It was a familiar voice.
“Kyle…?” she said. She turned away from Matthias and looked at the door. “Is that really you, Kyle? Are you here too? Did you get drafted? Kyle, how did-”
“Stop,” Matthias said, pulling her face back towards him. Kyle… Kyle… Kyle…
It grated at his nerves to hear the woman who despised him plaintively calling that name. Once again, he felt utterly low and worthless.
He was someone whom the emperor himself looked at as an equal, and yet somehow this woman was effortlessly able to make him feel like a nobody. The fact that he had longed for this feeling of subjection made him feel even more despondent. It was a sweet enough feeling to make his teeth chatter with contempt. It had been like that from the start.
“Please, Layla.”
His voice cracked as he spoke, which made her feel even more disconcerted.

“If you say that name one more time, I might have to put a bullet in Kyle Ettman’s head.” His whisper sounded like a pained groan. “So please, don’t say it again.”
She could sense that it wasn’t an empty threat. Her face went pale. The blank look in his eyes made her feel dazed. He wasn’t the type to joke about things like this.
She closed her trembling lips, whereupon he patted her head appreciatively. It was a very gentle touch, and yet she felt like he could change his mind and strangle her at any moment.
He turned away from her and started walking towards the shaking door.





