Matthias stood in the same spot until Kyle had been dragged out of sight. While the other witnesses were highly alarmed, Matthias alone remained utterly calm. He soon left them in the hallway and stepped back into his room.
Layla, who had been standing just behind the door, flinched and took several steps back. Her lips, which had been about to shout Kyle’s name, shut again, making no sound.
Smiling contently, Matthias wrapped his arms around her as she staggered.
Embracing her like this gave him the feeling that the last piece of his puzzle was being put into place. He liked it.
She didn’t try to resist as he sat her down on the bed and knelt at her feet. He then calmly removed her soggy shoes and socks, which had gotten soaked when she had vainly tried to run into the sea to escape him.
As much as she feared water, she feared him even more. But he had managed to catch her nonetheless. This had caused a combination of pain, joy,

and disillusionment to rise inside of him. Reflecting on it now, he felt the same mix of feelings once again.
She sat obediently until he tried to touch her belly, at which point she obstinately pulled away from him. Looking up at her tear-filled eyes, he smiled. Rather than try to feel for the baby again, he firmly clutched Layla’s cold ankles.
For a long while, he quietly gazed at the woman for whom he had felt so much passion that he had wanted to kill her. No matter what state she was in, his queen looked dazzlingly beautiful.
It was a heavenly yet hellish time.




129.

Chain.






“Don’t be needlessly stubborn,” Matthias said, looking down at the untouched meal on the table. His long eyelashes cast deep shadows on
his face. “You’re being selfish, Layla. Won’t you think of the baby?” He stood up and walked in front of Layla.
Once she realized that there was no point in struggling or asking to be set free, she had become completely passive, just sitting there like a doll. While he was there, she would neither eat nor speak. Instead, all she did was sit and glare at him. When that grew tiresome, she would fall asleep in place.
After several hours passed, she finally broke the silence, hoarsely saying, “Don’t you dare say anything about my baby. You don’t have the right.”
“Whatever do you mean?” he asked, smiling indifferently. With a firm but gentle motion, he placed a fork in her hand. “Layla, I never neglect my responsibilities. You must be aware of that.”

“No. My baby and I have nothing to do with you. So you have no responsibility towards either of us.”
“If you don’t like calling it my responsibility, then think of it as God’s will.” He pulled her hand with the fork in it above her plate. “It’s the will of the same God who made the baby inside you.”
Under the power of his strength, the tines of her fork smoothly pierced a bite-sized piece of meat. While she remained stubbornly motionless, he returned to his seat and continued eating.
She glared angrily at him for a moment, then flung the fork with the meat on it to the floor and stood up. Frowning at the noise, he slowly raised his eyes to look at her.
“If you’re going to kill me, then get it over with and kill me already. Stop beating around the bush!” she shouted.
“If only I could,” he replied, sighing. He stood up, picked the fork up off the floor, and set it down on the table. “If it were just you, I would’ve already granted your wish, Layla. Unfortunately, however, you’re with child, so I won’t be able to do anything for a while.”
After sighing again, he left the room. When he returned a short while later with a new fork, she was standing in front of the window, shaking like a cornered mouse, with a steak knife in her hand.
“You seemed to have gained energy while I was gone. Are you starting to like it in here?” he asked.
“Stop it,” she snapped back. “Get away from me. Let me go right now.” “But Layla, you should’ve used your head just a little more.”
After gently setting the fork down on the table, he walked up to her. She raised the knife, as if she was really going to stab him.

“My pistol is right over there, after all,” he said, looking with mock pity at the bedside table, where he had left his holster with his pistol in it. “And yet you picked that weapon instead? If only you’d been as resourceful as you are passionate.”
“Do you think that if you treat me like this, I’ll become your mistress again? That will never happen. Never! I’d sooner die than go back to that life.”
“You really think that’s all I want?” he asked. His response made her freeze.
“Do you still view me as a fool who can fall for his mistress’s tricks?”
Her face went blank as she looked at him. The man standing in front of her didn’t seem like the Matthias von Herhardt she knew. That was one thing of which she was certain, no matter how confusing everything else was.
“You killed that fool, Layla.” The smile vanished from his face and was replaced by utter coldness.
“If that’s not it, then what is it?” she asked. “What do you want from me?” Even though she knew there was nowhere for her to escape, she backed up,
pressing her back against the window frame. As she did so, the knife dropped from her loose, trembling hand, falling onto the carpet.
“I’ll let you know what I want once I decide on it,” he replied, snickering. “Until then, eat.” Acting like a gentleman escorting a lady, he then led her to the table and sat her down. “You need to feed my baby, after all.”
As he looked down at her round belly, she flinched uncomfortably, as if there were a bug crawling on her.
“I told you, it’s not your baby.”
“I fully understand and respect your wish for me to kill you, but I won’t be able to grant it until the baby has made it safely out of you.” After looking down at the knife on the floor and shaking his head, he lifted his own knife

and cut up the steak on her plate. “Looking at you right now, I’m unsure whether even that will be possible. So eat up.”
“I’m sorry, but my stomach is incapable of taking in food when someone like you is nearby,” she replied, remaining obstinate even as she felt like crying. He chuckled at her behavior. She reminded him of an ill-behaved puppy.
He didn’t mind it. He was glad that she didn’t realize how much he enjoyed this side of her personality. If she were aware of the way it made him feel, she would have stopped the behavior at once.
“Fine, keep up the act, then,” he said.You’re a great actress, Layla.” He took a sip of water and cocked his head as he looked at her. “Just remarkable. Your eyes, your smile, even the way you behave like a cat in heat. It’s truly a flawless performance.”
“Stop it!”
“It’s a compliment, Layla. Anyway, your virtuosic acting is the reason that little surprise is growing inside of you. In other words, your acting skills saved your life.”
“Don’t talk about my baby like that!”
“Oh, was I wrong about that? Did the baby come from sincere love? If you say so, I’ll gladly accept the correction.”
Rubbing the long stem of his water glass, he observed her expression. Blushing severely, she was back glaring at him. He chuckled at her reaction. “So try again. If you put on such a great acting display in order to escape from me, shouldn’t you put on an even better display in order to save your life?”
“I won’t fall for your tricks anymore,” she replied. “This isn’t a trick, Layla. It’s a generous offer.”
“Until you let me go, I’m not going to drink one drop of water. That fact will never change, no matter what.”

Occasionally she had wondered about him, thinking that perhaps he had grown. Now she felt ashamed to have even considered the possibility. He was the same as he had always been. He was tormenting her once again, just as cruelly and boldly, without showing the slightest sign of guilt or remorse.
“I think it will have to change,” he remarked, narrowing his eyes. “You see, Kyle Ettman is currently imprisoned, as punishment for his outburst. He seemed to have lost his reason over you.”
“H-he’s in jail? Kyle is? How could you?!”
“You can’t blame me for that, Layla. This is the military. Rules are rules.
There’s nothing I can do about it.” “That’s a lie! It’s all because of you!”
“In any case, Kyle is in prison, and he’s probably quite hungry by now. I’m not the reason he was imprisoned, but I am the reason he’s not being fed. It was my special request.” Sitting back in his chair, he crossed his legs and smiled. “Until I see you eat, Kyle won’t eat either. However much food I do see you eat, Kyle will get that much food as well. If you keep starving yourself like this, though, what can I do? Private Ettman will starve as well.”
“How can a person be so cruel? Now that Uncle Bill is gone, you’ve captured Kyle so that you can keep threatening me in the same way as before!” Her hands trembled as she clutched the edge of the table, not with fear, but with anger.
Bill Remmer... ? Matthias’s eyes twitched briefly when he heard her mention Bill’s name, but he quickly regained his composure.
Just then, she bolted up out of her chair. It wasn’t hard to guess what she was intending to do. She took a deep breath, and then yanked the tablecloth off the table, causing all the food and dishes to fall to the floor. While the sound of

broken dishes and clattering utensils echoed through the room, the two of them stared silently at each other.
“Poor Kyle,” he finally said, sighing. He seemed amazingly relaxed amid all this commotion. “I guess he’ll be going another day without food.” After clicking his tongue in mock dismay, he took the napkin from his knee and wiped the sauce off the toes of his boots, then stood up.
The icy silence of the room was then filled with the sound of him walking to the door and unfastening the chains on it. That was soon followed by the sound of the door opening and closing again, and then the chains on the outside of the door being fastened and locked.
Layla, who had been standing with her fists tightly clenched, then collapsed back into her chair.
The warm afternoon sunlight shone on her and the mess that she had been left alone in. Bothered by the way the sunlight was glittering in the fragments of the dishes on the floor, she closed her eyes.







The aide looked uneasily at the locked door.
Since abducting the woman and putting her in his room, whenever the duke left the room he would thoroughly lock it, using a chain and padlock. It seemed that the duke had truly become unhinged since entering Sienne.
The aide let out a deep sigh, then carefully unlocked the padlock. He didn’t look forward to the sight that he knew was going to greet him upon entering the room, but he couldn’t go against his superior’s orders.

After clearing his throat, he gently knocked on the door and said, “I’m coming in for a moment, Miss.” He then opened the door and stepped into the room.
The woman, who was sitting in front of the window that looked out onto the square, flinched and turned towards the door. The mess in the middle of the room had been cleaned up, but her face looked even paler than before.
“What’s that…?” she asked, narrowing her eyes at the covered tray the aide had placed on the table.
“You should eat this,” he said. “You have to eat to live, after all. And you should think of the baby. This has absolutely nothing to do with Major Herhardt.”
The aide was doing his best to lie convincingly. If he were simply to let the woman go, everything would be resolved, but he knew he couldn’t do that. He didn’t understand why the major had gone to such trouble to drag her here and lock her up in the first place, but he knew it was his duty to carry out his orders.
“I’ve brought you this food without the major’s knowledge. If he were to find out about this, I’d be in a lot of trouble. He should be returning in about thirty minutes. You’d better finish it before then,” he said, reciting the lines the major had given him verbatim.
The woman stared blankly at him for a moment, then looked at the tray again. It seemed that she didn’t intend to beg him to let her go today. He was glad about this, as it had been very awkward having to turn her down previously.
Perhaps she had given up? It would make things much easier for him if she had, and yet it saddened him to think about it.

After nervously fidgeting for a while, she said, “So, could you tell me… Is Kyle really in jail?”
“Kyle? You mean Kyle Ettman, the medic?” “Yes, that’s who I mean. Is he?”
“Ah… yes, yes he is. He’s serving a sentence for insubordination.” When he saw the woman’s eyes fill with tears, he felt uncomfortable and looked away, before adding, “It w-won’t be a long sentence.”
The woman nodded appreciatively at this last comment, though her face was still marked with deep sadness. “Could you bring this food to Kyle instead, then?”
His eyes widened at this unexpected request. “Pardon?”
It was hard to imagine something more foolhardy than to bring the food sent by the major not to its designated recipient, but to the man who was in jail for defying the major. If he were to do that, he was sure he would be immediately locked up right next to Kyle Ettman himself.
“I’m afraid I won’t be able to do that, Miss.”
“Oh… okay,” she replied resignedly. “I’m sorry for asking. And thank you for bringing this.”
The courtesy and submissiveness with which she was treating him made him feel even more guilty, so he hurried out the room. As he was closing the door, he was happy to see that the woman was lifting the cover from the tray. It was a sandwich and a glass of soda water. He had been surprised that an aristocratic officer like the major even knew of such cuisine, but he had faithfully followed the order. Hoping that the woman would eat it, he closed and locked the door again.




130.

I Guess I’ve Really Lost It.






When Matthias arrived back at the room, Layla was sitting by the window, looking exhausted. The sun had set, but she had not turned
on the light, so the room was dark.
After locking the door, he sauntered towards her and switched on the lamp. Just as he was about to draw the curtains, he was surprised to hear her speak first.
“I ate,” she said. “The brown-haired soldier brought me a sandwich and I ate it.”
“So?”
“So… you should feed Kyle too,” she replied with a determined look on her face.
He stared back at her for a moment before snickering as he leaned against the window frame. He had expected her to make this request, but it still

bothered him to hear it come out of her mouth.
“I’m not lying,” she continued. “I really did eat. If you don’t believe me, you can check for y-”
“I believe you,” he coldly said, cutting her off. “But that’s not a reason for me to let Kyle eat.”
“But you promised!”
“Did you forget, Layla? I told you very clearly that I would give Kyle as much food as I saw you eat with my own eyes.”
He was growing even calmer as his words became colder. Her face went pale as she stared at him in disbelief. “What do you expect to gain from being so cruel? What happened to the lofty pride of the noble Duke Herhardt?” She staggered up from her chair and walked in front of him. “You abandoned your new bride and joined the war just so that you could chase after your mistress who fled you out of hate. What a pathetic man you’ve become. I guess you’ve thrown your pride away, huh?”
She was still frightened of him, but right now her anger was overpowering her fear.
“I don’t know. Have I?” he casually replied, taking off his cap and setting it down on the table with a smile. Far from seeming angry at her, he seemed carefree and amused.
He’s unhinged, she thought, suddenly remembering just how strange and unpredictable he was. She felt so much hatred for him, her teeth almost started to chatter.
“What are you thinking?” she asked. “What are you planning to do? Are you just going to keep tormenting me? Is that how you’re planning to get revenge on me for deceiving you and running away? Do you think you have the right to do that?”

“As the father of the baby you’re carrying, surely I have at least that right, don’t you think?”
“How dare you say that?! I can’t stand to hear you mention my baby at all!” She wrapped her arms around her belly and stepped away from him. “You have nothing to do with me or my baby. So leave! Go back to the duchess and live your life with her!”
She gritted her teeth to stop herself from breaking into tears.
Don‘t expect anything, she had been telling herself over and over again, ever since her improbable reunion with him. Now she feared that her expectancy was starting to grow again. If her heart were to break once more, she didn’t think that she could stand it. However, he had always treated her horribly.
“Think logically about this, Layla. How do you expect to survive as a single mother in the middle of a war?”
He was now standing right in front of her. The smile was gone from his face.
“My baby and I will do just fine on our own,” she replied. “We can do anything, as long as you’re not around. I find you much more detestable than the war!”
“Layla Llewellyn.”
“Don’t touch me! Don’t lay a finger on my body! You’re dirty!” Shuddering, she batted his hand away.
“Dirty…?” he asked, chuckling.
Stepping back away from him until she was in the corner of the room, she replied, “Yes, dirty!” Despite her fear, she was shouting vehemently. “I hate you so much. You’re dirty. You always were, and you always will be. There, is that a good enough response?”







A voice rang out in the hotel basement storage area, which had been turned into a makeshift jail cell. “Kyle! Hey, Kyle!”
Kyle had been sitting on the floor, leaning against the cold, stone wall, staring at his feet. He looked up indifferently at the sound of his name. Through the bars of the cell, he saw the familiar face of his friend, the army surgeon, and said, “Lieutenant! What are you doing here…?”
“What do you think I’m doing here? I was worried about you. As soon as I heard what happened, I knew I had to come check up on you.” Sighing, the surgeon walked up to the bars and crouched down. Surveying the cell’s interior, he noticed at tray of dinner sitting untouched in the corner. Just as he had heard, it appeared that Kyle was refusing to eat.
“You should eat the meals they bring you, Kyle. Being stubborn like this won’t get you anything. It’ll just make you weak.”
“What happened to the woman the duke… I mean, Major Herhardt brought in? Has she been let go?” Kyle asked. None of the other soldiers would answer this question for him, and the concern in his voice was palpable.
“Well…” the soldier replied, looking uncomfortable. After letting out a long sigh, he continued. “She’s still with the major. But I don’t think he’s doing anything bad to her. I think he’s just holding her prisoner.”
“That bastard…”
“Kyle, get a hold of yourself. We’re at war right now. No matter what personal feelings you might have about the major, he’s your superior officer.”

The surgeon was surprised to see Kyle overcome by anger, when normally he was so well- behaved. “Anyway, Kyle, you’ve got to eat. How about you eat your food, regain your reason, and then apologize to the major? That’s the only way you’re getting out of here anytime soon.”
“No. I’m never apologizing to that guy.”
“Why are you acting like this, Kyle? It’s not like you. Who is that woman, anyway? And what’s her connection to the major?”
Rumors had been spreading even more quickly now that both Major Herhardt and Kyle Ettman were behaving erratically.
The perfect gentleman Duke Herhardt, the son of his family’s doctor, and the beautiful woman that had come between them—it was a scandal that no one could resist talking about. The fact that none of the concerned parties were offering any details only made the gossipers more eager to speculate.
Kyle gulped and looked away, saying nothing. Major Herhardt hadn’t answered any of the surgeon’s questions, and it seemed that it would be no different with Kyle.
“You should really eat. All right, Kyle?”
Eventually, the surgeon stood up resignedly. The lights from the storage area hallway and the shadows of the cell’s bars flickered over the cold food on the tray.
When the soldier took one last look over his shoulder before leaving, Kyle was still sitting in the exact same spot.







Matthias gazed at Layla as she stood in place, staring blankly. As he watched her, his emotionless face gave way to a smile, which then faded. After this happened a few times, he walked towards her.
Firmly clutching the hem of her skirt, she stared straight at him as he approached. Suddenly, thoughts of Claudine had entered her mind. The more she tried to avoid thinking about her, the more clearly her memories presented themselves to her. She winced as she thought of the shame and humiliation the duke’s fiancee had made her feel after she had found out about everything. It had made her feel miserable, especially since she hadn’t been able to refute any of her accusations. She had thought that she had forgotten about all of that, but it seemed that she hadn’t.
All throughout the late spring and summer, whenever she was sitting on the cold floor of the bathroom or huddled in a corner of the museum storage area, suffering from horrible morning sickness, she would think, Their wedding is fast approaching, and later, They must be married by now. Such thoughts came to her mind each time she had to hide the fact that she was pregnant.
As the fetus had grown inside of her, so had her sadness. Even after war suddenly broke out and turned the world upside down, her fear of the war still didn’t completely eliminate the sadness. This was especially true when she had a sudden craving for some specific food, or when she saw a sweet newlywed couple with a baby. This would always make her think of the duke and

duchess, whom she assumed had not only had a lavish wedding, but were likely expecting a baby of their own.
At such times, she would whisper to her belly, It’s all right, Baby, it’s all right. Mommy’s going to love you double. So don ’t worry. This was how she had managed to endure things so far, and she wanted things to stay that way. There was no way she was going to allow herself to be taken in by the duke again and let the baby inside her live through the same pain and sorrow she had gone through.
Matthias stopped one step away from her. His face was cold and expressionless. She couldn’t help but flinch, but she didn’t avert her eyes.
“Layla, how are you going to put up with the child of the dirty man that’s standing before you?”
“My baby has nothing to do with you!” she cried just as desperately as ever, even as she was growing extremely tired of his word games.
“I guess you really love that baby, huh?” he asked with amusement.
Instead of saying anything in response, she answered him by putting her arms over her belly to cover it.
“A woman who loves the baby of the man she hates…” After looking back and forth between her face and her belly a few times, he burst into dry laughter. His eyes remained frighteningly serious even as he laughed. “How funny.”
“You’ve really lost it, haven’t you?” she asked. “Yep,” he replied, putting his hands to her face.
She tried to turn her head away from him, but his strength was too much for her.
“I couldn’t have made it here in my right mind,” he continued. She was taken aback by his calmness.

“Something I really like about you, Layla, is the way you always put your weaknesses on display for me.”
“What are you talking about…?”
“No matter how much you hate me, no matter how dirty or detestable you find me, in the end, you’re always still mine.”
“No. That’s not true.”
“Yes it is. Because I will take the baby.”
Because he was speaking in such a gentle voice, she wasn’t sure she had heard him right at first. Now he started laughing as if he were suddenly overcome with joy.
“Once the baby is born, I’ll let you go. Don’t worry, I won’t kill you. You can run away wherever you want. You’ll just have to leave the baby behind.”
As the haze lifted from her mind and she grasped what he was saying, her resistance intensified. She tried pushing him, hitting him, and scratching him, but he wouldn’t let her go.
“Let go of me! Now! How dare you threaten to take my baby?! I’ll never forgive you! Never!”
“Calm down, Layla. You’re going to disturb our beloved baby.”
He lowered his hands from her cheeks and grasped her flailing arms. She kept trying to struggle, even after it became impossible to move her arms anymore.
Without exerting much strength, he was able to put his arms around her and lift her up. She was quite small even though she was pregnant, and she fit snugly in his arms as always.
He set the exhausted woman down on the bed and smiled warmly. It was the first sincere smile that had appeared on his face since she had disappeared in the spring. Even if she would never forgive him, he had found a way to hold

on to her. The relief that this brought him allowed him to finally breathe freely.
Her lips twitched for a moment, but she didn’t say anything. Instead, she just shed thick tears. He removed her cracked glasses from her face and set them down on the bedside table, then stooped over and kissed the moist edges of her eyes. Like an animal licking its wounds, he lapped up her tears. I guess I’ve really lost it, he thought as he savored the briny taste. Well, that’s line with me.




131.

The Path to Salvation.






Matthias awoke very early, before the sun had risen. With his eyes still adjusted to the dark, he looked around the room. The first thing he
noticed was the empty spot next to him in the bed. It was odd—-Layla had definitely cried herself to sleep right there, but now there was nothing but darkness in her place.
As soon as he realized this, he reflexively sat up. First he looked at the door, and saw that it was still securely locked. This brought him some relief. The door was the only way out of the room. Thus, she was still in the room, unless she had jumped out of the window…
At this unpleasant thought, he bolted out of bed and ran towards the windows. Both windows were shut and locked, but his uneasiness lingered. Just as his heart was starting to pound rapidly, he found Layla in an unexpected spot.

He sighed at the sight before him. She was curled up on the sofa, fast asleep. When he saw the thin blanket she was using for warmth, he couldn’t help but chuckle. He could almost hear her saying, “Don’t touch me. You’re dirty.” She found his presence loathsome enough that she couldn’t stand to lie next to him, and yet back in the spring she had smiled so beautifully at him. She had given herself to his embrace and whispered sweet nothings in his ear.
And yet, despite all those apparent signs of love, she had hated him all along.
As he quietly reflected on this thought, his face showed no expression. He had already been aware of this, he just hadn’t fully accepted it till now.
Where was the line between sincerity and deceit with her?
Ever since she had first disappeared, he had been mulling over this question, and now he finally knew the answer. It had all been deceit with her. He had loved her, but she hadn’t loved him back at all. Nonetheless, his memories of the show of love she had put on were still vivid, which only made him feel more miserable.
He gently closed his eyes for a moment, then opened them and carefully lifted her from the sofa and moved her over to the bed. She fidgeted a bit, but luckily did not wake up. Even in her pregnant state, she still felt remarkably light in his arms.
He placed her down on the bed very gently, then slowly pulled the covers over her body. Instead of lying down next to her, he pulled up a chair next to the bed and sat down in it.
Her face looked calm and innocent as she slept. It occurred to him that this was the way her face looked when she was interacting with anyone other than him. To everyone but him, she was a bright, strong, kind young lady. He was the only person in the world who was more accustomed to seeing her cry than

smile. And yet even so, she was going to have his child. He felt silly for fixating on that fact, but he couldn’t help it.
He didn’t understand how she could hate him so much, and yet love his baby. Regardless the reason, though, it was his only remaining hope. He knew that because of her love for the baby, she wouldn’t die or run away. Thus, if he could keep the baby near himself, she would become his again.
The baby would thus function as the perfect set of shackles, the perfect birdcage. That was all the baby was to him. He felt no particular emotions towards it. Other than its usefulness to him as a way to hold on to Layla, it was meaningless to him.
As he thought about it, he realized that there was one emotion he felt about the baby: regret. He bitterly regretted not impregnating Layla sooner. If he had, she never would have been able to run away in the first place.
As the anxiousness left his face, he started smiling like a child holding piece of candy. From the moment he joined the war, to the moment he finally entered Sienne, he had been wishing for Layla’s safety. She had to remain safe from harm until he got to her, so that he could kill her himself. He didn’t want to kill her out of hatred or resentment. Rather, it was out of love. Love had always been the reason he wanted to kill her.
He knew that her love for him had been a lie, and there was no way to forget that. Nonetheless, his love for her was as strong as ever. It felt like an unstoppable force inside him that threatened to devour the rest of his life. The only way to put an end to this overwhelming feeling, he thought, was to kill her with his own hands. Thus, until he could do so, she had to survive. It was imperative that she not die by any other cause.
With each passing day, his love for her had only grown stronger. And with that, his desire to kill her had grown in intensity as well. This desperate

pattern had continued to this day. And yet, the moment he had laid eyes on her, standing in front of the beautiful sea, he had realized that he couldn’t kill her. He wanted to kill her. He had to kill her. And yet, he couldn’t kill her.
As he was reeling in the mixture of despair and joy that this realization had brought him, he had noticed her pregnant belly, and had known at once that it was his salvation.
Without any way to get her back, his only option had been to kill her. Now, however, this miraculous baby opened up a new path for him. It meant that he wouldn’t have to kill her after all. If he could make the baby his, Layla would be his once again as well.
He slowly extended his arm and put his hand to her face as she continued to sleep. Moved by the warmth of his hand, she gently rubbed her cheek. Just like she had done during those moments of feigned excitement and love, those deceitful acts that had broken his heart.
Trying to suppress the urge to grab her face and pull her towards him, he continued gently caressing her cheek.
He knew she was going to keep hating him forever, but he didn’t mind. He didn’t mind that she would never forgive him, either. As long as he was going to possess her again, he didn’t mind about anything else. Anything was fine. No matter how painful the choice might be, it couldn’t be any more devastating than the time he had spent alone without her.
As the dawn slowly brightened, he sat for a long time, gazing at her. Soon he started to feel a faint sense of hope again. Perhaps Layla could become his duchess. The baby would then naturally become his successor, and would gain all the power and honor of the Herhardt family. And that wasn’t all. There were countless other things he could offer Layla and the child. Thus, perhaps one day she would have a change of heart, and her feelings towards him would

change. Even if this wouldn’t happen for a long, long time, he was prepared to wait it out.
Once the sun had fully risen and morning light was streaming in through the windows, he slowly stooped down and kissed her cheek.
“I love you,” he whispered to the woman who hated him. I’m so happy that I don’t have to kill you. I’m so happy that you’ll be mine again, Layla.





They ate breakfast in silence. Not a word passed between the two of them. The only sound was the clattering of utensils and dishes. Layla chewed and swallowed her food with a cold expression on her face, while Matthias watched her with amusement.
“I can’t believe the stubborn Layla Llewellyn has changed her mind in order to save her friend from starving. What a touching expression of true friendship.”
He gazed at her jovially as she cleared her plate.
Suppressing the urge to throw up everything she had just eaten, she quietly took a sip of water. She had no strength left to argue with the duke. It didn’t seem like there was any way for her to be free of him, short of dying. In fact, she wasn’t even certain that death would free her of him.
With Uncle Bill gone, he had moved on to Kyle. As if that weren’t enough, he was now using her baby as a way to keep her under his thumb. She didn’t want to dwell on his reasons for doing this. Whether it was out of twisted desire, obsession, or love, he was treating her like she was no more than an object for him to use or dispose of as he pleased.

“I’ve eaten now,” she muttered as she set down her utensils. “You saw it. I ate everything on my plate, right in front of you.
He nodded graciously. “Certainly. Thanks to you, Kyle Ettman will not go hungry today.”
“Are you satisfied now?”
“I am,” he replied, smiling. It was a sincere-looking smile, and it clashed with the cruelness of his behavior.
“Then let me go. I want to go home. You know better than anyone that I won’t be able to escape even if I go home.” She knew he wasn’t going to grant her request, but she still felt like she had to make it.
“You’ll just have to get used to this, Layla.”
“How am I supposed to get used to being imprisoned like this?”
“Well, you’ll have to, one way or another.” He clicked his tongue as if he were talking to an unruly child. “You’re going to be living the rest of your life like this, so getting used to it seems like a good idea, doesn’t it?”
“What are you talking about?!”
“If you want to leave, you may. I told you I wouldn’t kill you, didn’t I? You just need to bear the child first.”
Crossing his legs, he cocked his head as he looked at her. When his gaze lowered and settled on her belly, she couldn’t help but shudder.
“Are you really planning to take my baby from me?”
“Don’t you think it’s a bit harsh to put it that way? It’s my baby too, after

all.”
“How can you be so sure of that?” She covered her belly, as if she was

worried that the fetus would hear what they were talking about. Rationally, she knew there was no need to do this, but it just didn’t feel right to discuss such

unsavory matters in earshot of her child. “It’s funny that you think you’re the only man I’ve been with.”
“Oh, I see, you’re once again trying to tell the dreadful lie that it’s not really my baby.”
“There’s a lot of other men in the world besides you.”
The more serious her expression grew, the more carefree his smile became. “Is that so?”
“Yes. I deceived you. I made you believe I was in love with you. You know how good I am at lying. If I was able to fool someone as lofty and distinguished as you, Your Grace, surely I could manage to fool an ordinary man.”
She could feel herself starting to tremble out of nervousness, but she was speaking with confidence. As he listened to her, there was a wry smile on his lips, while his eyes remained as cold as the Schulter River in the dead of winter.
“Would you like to tell me whose baby it is, then? Is it Kyle Ettman’s? If so, that poor medic will have a bullet in his head before the day is out.”
“Don’t drag Kyle into this!”
“If it’s not Private Ettman, who is it? What’s the guy’s name?”
“I’ll never tell you. I know what you’d do to him if I did. What I’m sure of, though, is that your steadfast belief that it’s your baby is ridiculous. You’re a fool. You’ve been deceived before, and now you’re willfully persisting in this new delusion.”
I’m sorry, Baby, she repeated internally as she made these cold pronouncements.
This was her only remaining hope. She felt confident that he wouldn’t remain fixated on a baby that didn’t share his blood. However, he soon

regained a calm expression and chuckled. There was no trace of anger in the sound of his laughter.
“Fine. If telling that flimsy lie brings you comfort, I’ll accept it, Layla.”
“If you need to believe it’s a lie to protect your flimsy self-esteem, then I will accept that, Your Grace,” she replied, not backing down. She was feeling very frightened, but she couldn’t let him have the last word. She was also trying to ensure that he would think twice before carelessly stripping the baby away from her.
“Layla…” he whispered in a sweet tone as he slowly stood up. He then walked towards her, until she and her belly were enveloped by his shadow.




132.

The Only Way.






“There seems to be a misunderstanding here. I really don’t care about who the baby’s father is.”
Matthias stopped caressing Layla’s cheek and clutched her chin firmly. She tried to shake her head to free herself from him, but he easily maintained his grip.
He slowly raised her face to make her look into his eyes, then said, “No matter who the father is, what’s certain is that you’re the baby’s mother, and you love the baby. That’s all I need to know.” His eyes softened. “And Layla, if by some chance the baby isn’t mine, that’s all the more reason for you to keep close by it and protect it. If some other guy is father, I’m sure you understand the danger you would be placing the baby in if you were to run off and leave it with me.”

As she heard him say this, she let out a stifled cry, as if she were being strangled. Using his free hand, he started caressing her hair, as if he were petting a tamed beast.
“If it is some other guy’s child, I won’t feel great about it, but I doubt I’ll lose much sleep over it. After all, I’ll be the father of all the future children you’ll bear.”
“That won’t be happening!”
“You’re so smart, Layla, and yet your memory is so bad. I already told you, you’re going to live like this for the rest of your life.”
He stooped down to meet her gaze directly. In that moment, she understood what he had meant when he said that the old Matthias von Herhardt was dead. The Matthias who had gazed at her on that spring evening in Arvis, the last time they had seen each other there, was no more. The man in front of her now seemed like an entirely different person, and he had a wild look in his eyes.
He lightly kissed her on the forehead. Then, in a soft whisper, as if he were comforting a fretful child, he said, “Listen carefully, Layla.”
Stiffly clasping her hands together, she looked blankly at him. Her whole body started trembling.
“You are going to live with me, as my woman, bearing my children. That will be your life.”
He kissed her once more, this time on her pale cheek.
“So you’d better get used to it, for the sake of the baby inside you, which you insist on claiming isn’t mine. Don’t you think?”







Layla stepped back from the closed door, looking exhausted as she panted and tried to catch her breath. Her attempt to break through the door by hammering at it with a metal candlestick had resulted in nothing but several unsightly dents. The door remained just as firmly locked as ever.
After staring at the door for a while longer, she turned away. She set the candlestick down, sat deep in a chair, and let out a long sigh. The fetus inside her, which had gone stiff as if it were frightened by the noise, started to move again.
“Don’t worry, nothing’s wrong. It’s all right,” she said, trying to comfort it. She started stroking her belly, and she could immediately feel the fetus moving towards the spot she was touching.
She was used to it by now, but the first time she had felt the fetus moving, she had been very surprised, almost scared. Unsure what to do, she had hesitated for a moment before tentatively touching her belly. The fetus had then made another quick movement, as if in response. It was then that she had finally felt like a mother.
A wondrous, joyful feeling had filled her heart in that moment. She was a little sad that she couldn’t tell anyone about the feeling, but at least she could share it with the baby inside of her. She felt like the baby knew everything.
When Layla was in a good mood, she could feel the baby dancing joyously. When she felt depressed, the baby curled up stiffly. It was like there was a tiny

version of herself inside of her. She loved her baby with all her heart. It was a love that had absolutely nothing to do with Matthias.
Once the fetus had calmed down again, Layla put her messy hair into a ponytail, and wiped the tears from her eyes before putting her glasses back on. No matter how desperate of a situation she was in, she was a mother. She was her baby’s only family member and guardian.
She ate the lunch the now familiar soldier had left for her, and changed her clothes. Most of the things from her apartment had been moved to this room by now. It seemed that the duke really had no intention of letting her go.
Think calmly, she thought, trying to console herself as she looked around the room.
She had tried screaming for help, begging the soldiers to let her go, and now had even tried breaking down the door. None of these things had worked. Unless the duke had a change of heart, there was no way for her to escape.
She understood this rationally, and yet she didn’t want to give up. She couldn’t keep living like this forever. She had to protect the baby from the duke.
But how could she do that?
Just as her growing uneasiness was about to cause her to burst into tears, she heard the sound of the lock being unfastened on the other side of the door. She reflexively stood up and hurriedly lay down in bed. Feigning sleep, she closed her eyes and calmed her breathing. Meanwhile, she could hear the chains being lifted.
Lying curled up on her side, she clutched the edges of her pillow. A moment later, she heard the door open and then could sense someone walking across the room towards the bed.
From the sound of the footsteps alone she knew that the duke had returned.







Layla focused on pretending to be asleep. She had started doing this out of a desire not to have to look at Matthias, but at some point, her fear had increased even more. She was now truly afraid of him.
Oddly enough, this new fear had made her realize that, though she had always feared Duke Herhardt to some extent, the version of him that she had known back at Arvis had not been completely cold and emotionless. This was clear now, when she compared her memories of him back then to the way he was now. He had changed. It seemed that she really had destroyed some part of his soul.
Does that mean that my wish was granted? she wondered, recalling that she had wished that his yearning for her would become a long-term illness. Rather than being completely forgotten by him, she had wanted to leave an emotional scar with him. However, this transformation wasn’t what she had wanted.
Still feigning sleep, she bit her lip, feeling like she was about to burst into tears. She could hear the sound of the duke removing his overcoat and uniform jacket and placing them on the end of the bed. She then heard him walk a few steps, and then she felt the bed shake.
Stifling the urge to scream, she clutched the edges of her pillow tightly. He then lay down behind her back and slowly pulled her deep into his embrace.
Startled, she almost struggled, but managed to resist the urge to do so. Fortunately, he didn’t seem to want to do anything more than this. Thus she

knew it was better to endure it than struggle. If she provoked him, there was no telling what he might do.
She prayed internally for time to pass quickly, or else that she could fall asleep immediately. However, neither of her prayers were answered. Time passed slowly, and her senses only sharpened.
He kissed her hair, earlobes, forehead, and cheeks with a feather-light touch. Just as gently, his hands stroked her neck, shoulders, and waist. Through his chest, which was pressed against her back, she could feel the slow, steady rhythm of his heart beating. She felt frightened and sad.
His breathing then gradually slowed down, and his hands and lips stopped touching her. Once she was fairly sure he was asleep, she let out a silent sigh of relief. Even so, she lacked the courage to look behind her, so she just lay there in that position for a long while. His embrace felt as warm and cozy as ever, and this only made her feel sadder.
Once the sunlight started to redden in the late afternoon, she finally opened her eyes and looked at him. He was asleep, with a peaceful look on his face. The way his face looked now was just how she wanted to keep him in her memory, so that years from then she could describe him this way when she told her child about its father.
Of course, she had no intention of hurting the child by telling the full truth. Instead, she was going to say, “Your father passed away while you were still in my belly. But he loved us so much. ”
Even though she planned to lie like this, she wanted to convey some modicum of truth as well. Perhaps she would describe his handsome smile and blue eyes, or his gentle voice. She wouldn’t go as far as to bring up any actual memories of her time with him, but she would describe brief moments and impressions like that. However, that was for a later date. Not now.

Gritting her teeth, she withdrew herself from his embrace. She knew full well that there was no hope of escaping. Even if somehow she could get out of the room, soldiers would probably catch her before she could get out of the hotel. If she were lucky enough to evade even them, she would be stuck in a city occupied by the Berg army. One way or another, she would end up back with him. Still, however, she didn’t want to give up.
Just then, her eyes stopped upon something that brought her a faint ray of hope: the clothes he had taken off. Perhaps he had left the key in them!
As soon as this thought occurred to her, she hurriedly got off the bed. Just as she was reaching for his overcoat, she saw a colorful paper box on the bedside table. She was sure it hadn’t been there before. He must have just brought it. Only after opening it did she realize that she should have left it alone. It was a slice of cake. It was very pretty, and was topped with pink whipped cream.
Her hands started to tremble as she looked back and forth between the slice of cake she was holding and the sleeping duke. Her breath quickened, and her heart started pounding. A faint moan escaped her lips as she reflexively stepped back. Before she knew it, she had dropped the cake slice, and it had gotten on her clothes. She tried to clean herself up, but this only made it worse. By the time she came to her senses, her hair, hands, and even her shoes were smeared with cake.
She shook her head in distress, then quickly wiped her hands on the hem of her skirt and started searching all the pockets of his overcoat and uniform jacket. She knew it was pointless to do this, but she still wanted to. She wanted to get out of the room, even though she knew she would almost certainly get caught again.

Just as she was coming to the sad realization that the key was nowhere to be found, she heard a warm voice calling to her: “Layla…”
She raised her frightened eyes and saw Matthias sitting up in bed, leaning against the cushioned headboard. After narrowing his eyes for a moment, he smiled and slowly retrieved the key from his pants pocket. Holding it for her to see, he said, “Here it is.” As he slowly closed and opened his eyes, the smile disappeared from his face. “This is what you were looking for, isn’t it?” He stared at her with eyes like those of a hungry beast.
Fearing that he was really going to kill her this time, she reflexively picked up the pistol sitting beneath his overcoat. Before she realized what she was doing, she was pointing it at him. The chilling feel of the cold metal against her hands made her want to burst into tears, but there was no going back now.
“You’ve gotten much better at choosing your weapon. I guess my advice did you some good,” he said in a kind tone of voice, as if he were praising a child. He seemed incredibly calm for someone who had a gun pointed at him.
“The key. Give me the key right now,” she said, still pointing the pistol at him as she took a few steps towards him.
Twirling the keyring around his finger, he leaned back against the headboard. “Now you’ve got to undo the safety, so that you can pull the trigger.”
“G-give me the key!” she shouted, choking back tears.
However, he just kept watching her with an amused look on his face, as if he were a spectator at an entertaining play. “Undo the safety, Layla.”
“You think I’m joking? If you don’t give me the key, I’ll shoot! Give it to me right-”
“Go ahead, shoot,” he said, snickering.

Gritting her teeth, she undid the pistol’s safety catch to show that she was serious.
Not wavering in the slightest, he said, “Good. Now kill me.” He tossed the key up in the air and caught it. “That’s the only way you can escape from here.”




133.

A Sad, Frightening Dream.






After telling Layla to shoot, Matthias closed his eyes and sat motionless, as if he was really going to let her kill him.
She gulped nervously and adjusted her grip on the pistol. She had never shot a gun before, but she had watched plenty of hunting in her day. She had seen the very man in front of her brutally kill countless defenseless animals. Now she had the opportunity to pay him back for all that cruelty.
Gritting her teeth, she placed her trembling index finger on the trigger.
Even though she was standing still, aiming the pistol, she was panting like someone who had just sprinted a long distance. Tears had started to fall from her eyes as well.
A feeling of emptiness suffused first her mind, and then her whole body. Suddenly she felt very uncertain. She didn’t know what this frightening, cruel,

yet friendly man wanted from her. She didn’t know what would become of her and her baby. She didn’t know what to do, and she had no idea how to decide.
Finally, she broke down and burst into tears. He then opened his eyes and saw Layla, covered in pink whipped cream, sobbing as she held a pistol she didn’t know how to use. She looked both pitiful and audacious. To him, however, she just looked beautiful. As he looked at her with a calm gaze, her crying grew louder and sadder. All the while, however, she kept the pistol aimed at him.
With a slight smile on his lips, he stood up. She started trembling so much that she lost her balance and almost fell down.
Subduing her was incredibly easy. All he did was grab her arm and give a light tug, and she collapsed forward onto the bed. He then got on top of her and effortlessly took the pistol from her. After struggling for a moment like a fish thrown ashore, she soon went limp.
“I told you to kill me,” he flatly muttered as he inspected the pistol. Just as he had advised her to, she had undone the safety catch. “If you’re not going to do it, then I’ll have to, Layla.”
As she continued to sob, she heard the metallic sound of him cocking the pistol. She opened her eyes and looked up at him. Through her tear-blurred vision, she saw that he was aiming the pistol straight at her head.
Her sobbing stopped at once. After she stared blankly at him for a moment, he asked, “Shall I spare you?” His tone sounded almost benevolent.
She nodded her head in a daze. Everything felt like a dream. Even her fear vanished.
“If I do, you’d better behave,” he added, stooping down lower towards her. Viewed from up close, his eyes were tinged with an infinite kindness.
Transfixed by the sight, she nodded once more.

He snickered, and she heard the dull sound of something heavy dropping onto the floor. She looked in the direction of the sound and saw that he had tossed the pistol down. It lay in the light of the sunset, with its detached magazine lying next to it.
Her tears continued to flow, dripping onto the sheets. As relief spread through her body, she lost all her energy. Feeling even more dazed, she heard his gentle voice whisper, “Look at you, you’re a mess.”
He patted her head just as gently, saying, “It’s all right, Layla.” As if he were handling a very fragile object, he pulled her chin to make her face him. “I’ll clean you up.
This was the last thing she remembered him saying.







When she regained her senses, she found herself sitting in a bathtub full of warm water. Viewed through the steamy air, his face looked just as kind as it had before she had blacked out.
When they made eye contact, he asked, “Too hot?” She shook her head in response.
She knew she hadn’t fainted, and yet she couldn’t remember how she had gotten here. Or rather, she did remember, but the memory didn’t feel like it had happened to her.
Leaving her alone on the bed, he had gotten up and walked straight to the bathroom. While she had listened to the sound of a bath being drawn, the sun

had set. All the while, she had remained motionless, lying on her back and staring at the ceiling.
Eventually he had returned to her and gently patted her head. Then he had proceeded to slowly remove her soiled clothes. It was a shameful thing to be subjected to, but she hadn’t resisted. Soon even her feeling of shame had faded away. She hated everything. All she wanted was for him to stop doing the things he wanted to do and let her go instead.
As she reflected on these memories, they seemed like something from a distant world that had happened to a complete stranger, not to herself. The present moment still felt that way. Despite the fact that she was sitting naked in front of the duke, she felt no particular emotion.
“Eyes,” he whispered.
She thought for a moment before grasping his meaning. Like an obedient child, she shut her eyes, and then felt his hands on her face. Having never had a dog or other pet that he would have had to wash, he was a bit clumsy, but he seemed quite relaxed as he gently scrubbed her skin.
As his hands descended below her shoulders, she flinched. She wrapped her arms around her knees more tightly, and the anxiousness slowly returned to her eyes. The splashing sounds stopped, and the two of them stared at each other in silence.
“You’ve got to behave, Layla.”
He pulled her wet hair off her shoulders and chest and tucked it behind her. She shivered, as if a cold wind had just blown over her.
“You promised.”
She was staring off into space. He pulled her face towards him. When her gaze drifted away again, he made her face him again.

Her lips twitched as if she was about to say something. Instead, she resignedly lowered her arms from her breasts. As he observed the obvious changes in her body below her bony shoulders, the look on his face didn’t change much. There was a momentary tinge of flustered satisfaction that faded quickly.
Pulling her face towards his again, he said, “I’ll buy you another slice of that cake tomorrow.”
She frowned at him. He didn’t seem to realize how strange this situation was.
“You don’t need to,” she muttered in a tired voice.
The thought that tomorrow she would have to contend with him once again made her feel awful.
“Yes I do,” he insisted, as if he didn’t really care what she said. “You like it, don’t you?”
“What do you care what I like or dislike?”
She raised her knees again and pressed her back against the wall of the tub.
Even so, she understood that she was still under his control.
He grinned in response to her question. The grin looked so friendly, it only made her feel worse. As he reached towards her again, she noticed something on his arm.
Her eyes narrowed as she looked closely at what appeared to be a scar under the rolled-up sleeve of his shirt. It appeared to be a fairly fresh wound. It registered anew with her that he was a military officer who had engaged in fierce warfare on his way to occupying this city.
When he realized what she was looking at, he snickered and started to pull down his sleeve. However, she grabbed his arm to stop him. Examining the wound like a doctor, she whispered, “That must have hurt a lot.”

He chuckled and replied, “I’m surprised to hear you say that, when moments earlier you were about to shoot me.”

“It didn’t hurt.” “You’re lying…” “Really.”
He lifted her hand from his arm and placed it back in the bathwater. Now that their brief exchange was over, the sounds of gentle splashing resumed.
She stared with bloodshot eyes at the surface of the water, wishing that he would stop. However, he continued leisurely washing her, as if he were playing a game. He showed no hesitation in touching her large, pregnant belly. It was as if he didn’t even realize, or didn’t care, that there was a baby inside.
Did he believe me when I said it’s another man’s baby?she wondered, holding her breath nervously.
The baby stopped moving as well, as if it were also feeling nervous. Matthias, meanwhile, kept casually scrubbing her body. Somehow, even though he was behaving like a servant to her, he still seemed like he was in charge.
This thought made her feel strange, and she soon closed her eyes. As she sat still, entrusting her body to his touch, her breath kept quickening.







After he finished bathing her, he toweled dry her body, put her clothes back on her, and even dried her hair for her before placing her onto the bed, which

now had a clean set of sheets on it.
A vacant look remained on her face through most of the process. Occasionally she would look intently at him for a moment, but would then quickly lower her eyes or turn her head away. Other than that, she remained calm and passive, like an unwound wind-up doll.
He dimmed the lights, picked up a necktie, and returned to her side. Lying calmly, she stared at him and slowly blinked.
He caressed her cheek reassuringly, then tied the necktie firmly around her wrists. Knowing that she would struggle, he made sure to put a handkerchief underneath to prevent her from hurting herself.
After tying the other end of the necktie to the bedpost, he pulled the covers over her body and stood up, saying, “Go to sleep.”
She struggled weakly for a moment before calming down again. The look on her face remained the same as he went back to the bathroom, took a shower, and returned. There was a blank look in her eyes, as if she didn’t know what was happening to her.
He untied the knots and freed her hands. Then he tied another knot around only one of her wrists, and tied the other end of the necktie to his own wrist. This time she just passively watched him, making no attempt to resist. She looked very tired and worn out. This was understandable, given all that she had been through today.
He turned off the lights, and then lay down next to her. Their hands, tied together as one, lay on the sheets, one atop the other.
“Don’t go,” he whispered as he slowly interlocked his fingers with her in the darkness. Even more quietly, he said, “Stay by my side.”
Her eyes twitched, but she remained silent. He squeezed her hand and whispered, “Sleep well, Layla.”

He then kissed her forehead with an intimate tenderness that felt worlds away from the war that surrounded them.
For a while, she gazed at the knot around their wrists and at his face, and then she closed her eyes.
In the midst of her deep sleep, she had a dream. It was a sad, frightening dream.




134.

An Impossible Declaration of Love.






Something had changed about the major’s woman. The aide, who had seen her just about every day, had noticed a distinct shift in her demeanor. After always begging him to help her escape, or else asking how Kyle Ettman was doing, now when he entered the room she would just sit in silence, staring
out the window.
She no longer refused to eat or tried to break down the door. She ate whatever he brought her, and then curled up in her seat with a blank look on her face. Her newfound submissiveness had certainly made his job easier, but he wasn’t sure how to feel about that.
“I don’t know how much longer she’s going to last,” he said as he locked the door from the outside.
The other soldier guarding the hallway looked similarly concerned.

“The woman is acting a little strange,” the aide said. “Do you think I should tell someone?”
“Tell who?”
“Major Herhardt, I guess…” Before the words were out of his mouth, the aide realized the pointlessness of the idea. He let out a long sigh.
Major Herhardt obviously knew the woman’s state better than anyone. And yet, he always seemed to be enjoying his time with her. Furthermore, the aide was hesitant to say anything. Whenever anyone asked any questions about the woman, Major Herhardt always reacted quite harshly. It had soon become an unwritten rule that no one was to mention the major’s woman unless the major himself first brought her up.
“I heard that the baby she’s pregnant with is Major Herhardt’s,” the soldier whispered after checking to make sure no one else was in the hallway. “So I guess that means it will be the illegitimate child of Major Herhardt? Or rather, of Duke Herhardt? And I guess she was the Duke Herhardt’s mistress before she ran away? Anyway, that’s what I heard.”
“You heard those rumors too, huh? You know what else I heard? I heard that she was engaged to Kyle Ettman, who’s the son of the Herhardt family’s doctor. But then apparently Duke Herhardt-”
“Shh!” the soldier said, batting at the aide to make him stop talking. A long shadow was approaching them from the end of the hallway.
The two men had been so engrossed in gossip, they had realized too late that the subject of the gossip had appeared.
Matthias approached the frightened men at a leisurely pace. It was hard to believe that the unseemly rumors they had been sharing were about this man, when they looked at his immaculately neat uniform, his elegant bearing, and the gentle smile on his face.

They greeted him with excessive courtesy. He responded with a quick nod before proceeding into the room where the woman was being held prisoner. The two men looked at each other and sighed before walking off, leaving the hotel hallway in silence.







Matthias placed what he had brought on the table. Layla could see plainly what it was—a stack of books—but she had no idea what his intention was in bringing it.
“You can read these if you get bored,” he said in a slow and deliberate voice, as if he were educating an ignorant child. “There are some mystery novels. You like those, right?”
She said nothing in response.
“There are some books about birds, too.”
She sat staring dispassionately at the spines of the books, still saying nothing.
Desserts, flowers, books—the things the duke kept bringing to the room didn’t fit the situation at all. However, she lacked the energy to point this out. Besides, even if she were to point it out, she didn’t think he would acknowledge the nature of the situation in the first place.
With a blanket wrapped around her belly, she took the top book from the stack and opened it. She wasn’t planning to actually read it, but rather to use it as an excuse not to look at the duke.

Sitting across the table from her, he looked at her with satisfaction. Her loosely braided hair ran down past her thin shoulder, touching her belly. He found the sight of this pregnant woman sitting reading a book in the sunlight beautiful and peaceful.
After skimming through the book’s pages for a short while, she looked up at him sheepishly and asked, “Will you let me go outside just for a bit? I won’t run away, I promise.
He didn’t like the sudden break in the perfect tranquility of the moment. “I want to stop by my apartment. And I’d like to pay a visit to Uncle Bill’s-”
“Layla,” he muttered, cutting her off. “You no longer have a home or a family.” He stood up, walked towards her, and put his hand to her face. “The only home you have now is with me. I’m your only family. I’m your everything now, Layla. All you need is me.
“No! Don’t talk like that. I’m-”
“It’s a war zone outside. It’s dangerous. You need to stay with me. I’ll protect you.”
She looked out the window in frustration before looking back at him. The bombings that the city was undergoing were no more frightening to her than the duke himself was.
“You’ve got to think of the baby,” he added.
She frowned at this advice. Whenever he talked about the baby in her belly, he always put on a warm, friendly face. He was acting like a father who sincerely loved his future child, while in fact the fetus was nothing to him but a means of satisfying his greed.
“Please don’t insult my baby like that. I hate it!” she said, wrapping the blanket tighter around herself in the vain hope that it would prevent the baby from hearing what the duke was saying.

“Insult your baby like that?” he asked, cocking his head in mock confusion. “There seems to be some misunderstanding here, Layla. I sincerely love the
baby. Thanks to the baby, I don’t have to kill you, and you and I can be happy together again.”
He was speaking in a flat tone, with no trace of hostility. His matter-of-fact attitude caused her to lose the will to argue with him any further. She hid herself in silence again.
