Saying nothing in response, Matthias let go of her hand and stood up. Just then, Kyle entered the room with a meal for her.

As the two rivals stood facing each other, the surgeon gulped nervously several times. He had heard all the rumors about Duke Herhardt and the Herhardt family doctor’s son, as well as the woman the duke had abducted. Based on what he had observed over the past several days, these rumors seemed to be largely accurate. He couldn’t openly acknowledge this, however, which made things quite awkward for him.
Luckily, the silent confrontation didn’t last long. Matthias stepped away from the bedside, and Kyle took his place. Soon thereafter, Layla opened her eyes.
A bright smile spread across her face when she saw Kyle. “Kyle… Did you bring the geometry notes?” she muttered. She seemed to be confusing dreams and reality once again.
Kyle gladly played along. “Of course I brought them. I don’t think you’d let me eat dinner with you if I hadn’t.”
“Oh, come on. That’s not true.” “Let’s eat, Layla.”
“But Uncle Bill’s not home yet.”
“Oh, he… said he’s going to be a little late.”
Kyle furtively wiped away the tears that were forming in his eyes, then smiled even more brightly.
Matthias watched the two of them from a distant corner of the room.
“Oh, I see,” Layla said, smiling like an innocent young girl. Kyle then started carefully feeding her the lukewarm porridge he had brought. It was a cozy, peaceful scene.
After watching for a while, Matthias quietly turned and left the room. Dazzlingly bright sunlight streamed in through the windows in the hallway. A bitter smile appeared on his dry lips.







Matthias re-entered the room late that night. Kyle had already departed, so the room was empty other than Layla, who was fast asleep.
As her fever had dropped little by little, her breathing had grown smoother. Thus Matthias was able to gaze at her in deep silence for a long while. There was a calm expression on her face. Perhaps she was having a pleasant dream. He didn’t know what her dream was about, but he did know one thing: he wasn’t in it.
Not wanting to dwell on this unpleasant thought, he got up and went to the bathroom. After taking a longer-than- usual shower, he emerged from the bathroom to find Layla awake. Staring quietly at the ceiling, she looked like an innocent child.
He hesitated for a moment, then walked over to her. Just as he expected, once she saw him, a look of dread spread across her face.
“D-don’t,” she said, recoiling as he lifted the covers from her body. Ignoring her reluctance, he proceeded to remove her pajamas.
As he sponged down her sweaty body, she flailed her arms weakly in an attempt to resist. Once she realized that she was powerless to stop him, she hid her face in her hands.
It wasn’t that there were no women available to wash Layla’s body. There were plenty of capable nurses at the military hospital. Matthias just didn’t want to use them. He bristled at the thought of someone else seeing or touching her body, even a woman.

He carefully washed her gaunt, trembling body. His eyes twitched briefly when he touched her pregnant belly.
Even as the rest of her body had become more and more emaciated, the fetus had continued to grow. He had hoped that she would have a miscarriage, so that she could be given proper treatment, but the fetus had stuck it out.
Observing the way it was stubbornly persisting in draining the life out of its mother, he was sure that the baby was his. As he reflected on the fact that Layla, who hated him so much, loved the baby enough that she was willing to sacrifice her own life for it, he was filled with jealousy. He shook his head, realizing how much he bitterly envied his own child. He felt the same hateful envy towards Kyle Ettman, and towards the late Bill Remmer. And not only them. He even felt the same way towards the birds, flowers, and trees that Layla loved. These thoughts made him feel miserable and pathetic, but he couldn’t stop thinking them.
Should I get rid of all of them ? he thought to himself. He knew it was an absurd idea, but it brought him some small relief to fantasize about ridding the world of everyone and everything Layla loved. With those distractions out of the way, perhaps she would finally be able to love him. It was a wild, outlandish idea, and yet he knew he was capable of carrying it out.
This led him to another realization: that at some point, his love was going to cause Layla to die. That was just how his love worked. He didn’t know any other way to love her, and he didn’t think he ever would.
He snapped out of his disturbed reflections and set aside the washcloth. A sullen look hung on his face as he dressed her in a new set of pajamas and pulled the covers back over her.
Brushing her hair from her forehead and cheeks, he whispered, “Go to sleep.”

Holding her breath, she nodded and shut her eyes. He could tell she wasn’t sleeping. She just had her eyes closed to avoid him. He knew that she feared and hated him. Nonetheless, he enjoyed gazing at her, so he stayed by her side.







Layla woke up and found herself in Matthias’s embrace. The sun was shining brightly through the windows. It seemed to be morning.
As her mind became clear, hazy memories from the night started to come back to her.
She recalled grumbling in a half-asleep state, saying that she wanted to go outside, that it was stuffy inside and she wanted to get some fresh air. She seemed to recall Matthias listening to her with an uncharacteristically concerned, sympathetic look on his face. She wasn’t sure if this had been real, or just a dream, however.
She shifted her gaze from the sky outside the open window to Matthias. He was sitting deep in a chair by the window, holding her in his arms. He was running his fingers up and down her spine with a touch as gentle as the sea breeze.
“Layla,” he whispered, seeming to have realized that she was awake. He lowered his gaze from the distant sky to look at her and once again whispered in a sweet tone, “Layla.” As always, he seemed to relish her name as it passed through his lips. “The baby is safe. That’s why I’m happy right now.” As he spoke about the baby he had tried to kill, his voice was just as sweet as when he had called Layla’s name.

Feeling a little hopeful, she looked into his eyes. I guess he doesn’t hate his own child after all. He only made that choice because his judgment got clouded in the heat of the moment. But he really-
“I’m happy, because I need the baby alive in order to maintain possession of you,” he said in an even gentler tone, crushing her hope. “As happy as I am, though, I’m also upset. I’m upset that you, who hate me so much, love my baby. I can’t stand that. I like the baby to the extent that it will function as shackles to keep you from escaping me. When I think of the baby as something that you love, however, I hate it.”
She looked at him, bewildered. Perhaps it was because of the excessively bright sunlight, but his smiling face looked almost unreal.
Am I in the middle of a fever dream? she wondered. I hope so. No father should speak that way about his own child.
“I know it shouldn’t be this way, but I want to lock you up again…..I don’t care if I have to hold the baby hostage and place you in a high tower somewhere. I’m willing to do anything in order to have you for myself. And I won’t regret a thing. I won’t regret ruining your life, taking something precious away from you, or even bringing misfortune upon my own offspring. Even now, it pains me to know that I’ve made you ill, but I’m happy that I get to have you in my arms.”
She held her breath. The baby, which had been moving energetically in response to the sound of its father’s voice, calmed down as well.
“This is just how I am, Layla. I will be a monster towards you like this forever. And one day this monster is going to make you die. I’ll still love you, though, even if I kill you. So…” Trailing off with a sigh, he grabbed a handful of her golden hair, pressed it to his face, and slowly kissed it.

Despite all his posturing, he knew deep down that he could never harm even a single strand of her hair. Even when he had resolved to find her and kill her, he had already known this. He had told himself that if he couldn’t have her, he was going to put an end to his intense longing by ending her life. Even in that moment, however, he had known he was lying to himself. She was his ruler, his queen. There was no way he could kill her.
Seeing that she was trembling with fear, he slowly caressed her face to soothe her nerves. She could tear his heart to pieces with her bare hands, and he would sit back with a foolish smile on his face and let her. If his love for her was the poison that would kill her, then there was only one thing he could do for his queen.
“Go, Layla.”
Letting the handful of hair fall from his fingers like a golden wing, he put his hand to her cheek. The sight of her face still drove him wild with desire. This made him all the more certain that he needed to say this to her. If he were to let this moment pass, he might not ever be able to say it again.
“You need to get well as fast as you can, and then leave me,” he said, forcing the painful words out of his mouth.
These words felt like he was issuing his own death sentence. Even as he uttered them, however, he maintained the smile on his face.
He loved everything about her.
He even loved seeing her when she was teary-eyed and distraught. Even though his obsession with her made him miserable, he loved the hell that he had created for himself. Even in this moment, he ardently longed to be able to live in that hell along with Layla and their child. That’s what he was. A monster who had imprisoned Layla.

However, he also knew that the woman he loved so much, the woman he desperately wanted to possess, was not just some woman. She was Layla, and was as beautifully free-spirited as a bird. She was the girl whose life he had read and read about while sitting alone in the cottage on that rainy day.
“Layla…..If I can’t be your heaven… then I will let you go.”
Even if he let her go, he had no idea how much longer his love for her would last. Probably for a long, long time. Perhaps it would continue to torment him for the rest of his life. Even so, he wouldn’t blame her for it. He would continue to love her, and would endure all the pain that the love caused him. My beloved Layla, please…
“So, go.” His voice was as gentle as a soft breeze. She stared at him in a daze.
“Fly away from me and find your heaven.”
Fly away before my love for you makes me kill you, he thought. Rather than saying this, however, he just kissed her forehead.
In that moment, Layla became certain that she was dreaming, and a smile spread across her face. The baby inside her once again started dancing like a flower petal swaying in the breeze.
It was a beautiful dream, even though it was a little sad.




138.

That Beautiful Day.






Layla’s fever had gone down, but she couldn’t stay awake with a clear mind for long. She still spent most of her time in a deep sleep.
Even though she seemed to have made it through the worst part of her illness, Kyle remained uneasy. He was still only a fledgling medical student, but he had years and years of experience watching his father work. With patients in conditions like Layla’s, one couldn’t be sure that they would recover simply because their fever had broken.
“Uncle Bill…” she whispered, fidgeting slightly in her sleep.
She seemed to be dreaming about the past again. As long as she was sick, all that Kyle could do for her was stay by her side.
What could he do once she was fully recovered? This question made him even more anxious. There was no way he could simply leave her with the duke. However, he knew the duke wouldn’t simply let her so.

He considered deserting the military and taking Layla with him. When he considered how this extreme measure would play out, however, his despair only deepened. With the entire continent swept up in war, there was nowhere they could escape to. Besides, she was pregnant.
He tried saying the name that she had been calling out for: “Uncle Bill…” His voice cracked with emotion as he said it.
If Bill were alive right now, what would he have wanted?he wondered. No matter how much he thought about it, he had no idea. One thing was certain, however. After escaping from Arvis with Layla and coming all the way here to Sienne, Bill would never have wanted her to fall back into the duke’s clutches again.
So think, Kyle, think of a way to help her, he told himself, clutching his hair. Just then, the door opened, and he saw just the person he expected to see:
Matthias von Herhardt.
Kyle intentionally disrespected him by refraining from saying hello, but the duke didn’t seem to mind. In fact, he barely seemed aware of Kyle’s presence at all. From the moment he entered the room, his eyes remained fixed solely on Layla.
He approached the bed and started caressing her cheek. It made Kyle sick to see him looking at her and touching her with such intimacy, as though he thought she belonged to him.
Just as he was about to say something, the duke spoke first: “We need to talk.”
“What is it?” Kyle replied.
“It’s not something that we should discuss here,” the duke replied. Brushing Layla’s hair back behind her ear, he stood up.
“But we can’t just leave Layla alone in…”

Before Kyle could finish voicing his objection, he saw a young military nurse step through the open doorway.
“I think we can leave her without worry now, Ettman,” Matthias said to the stunned Kyle.
“I have nothing to say to you, Major Herhardt,” Kyle said.
Matthias’s face shifted the moment he heard Kyle address him by his military title. “You seem to misunderstand me, Private Ettman. This is not a request. It’s an order. A direct order from your superior officer.”
Kyle realized too late that he had stepped into a trap.
Matthias turned towards the door before Kyle had the chance to make a reply. Kyle hesitated for a moment, and then followed him out.
They proceeded to have a surprisingly long conversation.







There was a congenial atmosphere at the meeting held at the Sixth Infantry’s headquarters. After years of preparation, the military’s plans were proceeding without a hitch, and the key goal of occupying Sienne had been achieved ahead of schedule. This seemed to definitively refute the cowards who had argued for avoiding war at all costs.
“With the Fourth Infantry advancing towards the western front, we have decided to keep the Sixth Infantry here in the occupied area for the time being, where it will function as a reserve force, maintaining the stability of the rear supply line,” General von Delman announced, standing in front of a map. The lanky, monocled man was in his sixties, but looked as hale and hearty as

someone many years younger. “Furthermore, it’s important that we quash those troublesome guerilla fighters as swiftly as possible.” His eyes narrowed as he used his pointer to trace the supply route extending from Berg to the southern end of Lovita.
Lovitans in the occupied area had been opposing the Berg military by blocking roads and cutting telephone lines. Occasionally they had gone as far as to attack supply vehicles or snipe at Berg troops, which had especially raised the general’s ire.
“We have simply been too lax in our management of the occupied area,” General von Delman muttered regretfully. He had consistently expressed the need to manage the occupied area by driving fear into the hearts of its inhabitants. The emperor and crown prince, however, had not been willing to retract their strict order requiring the military to respect the rules laid down in the international treaty.
Matthias sat upright in his chair, listening carefully as the general told of the upcoming plans and strategies. Barring a drastic change in the battlefield situation, it seemed that he would remain stationed in Sienne for at least the next few weeks.
During that time, he needed to send Layla away. Thinking about this, his breathing faltered momentarily, but he quickly regained his composure.
The general scanned the faces of all the commanding officers present, before fixing his gaze on Matthias. “Major Herhardt. As you know, the guerillas, protected by international treaty, have severed our communications. We thus need a messenger to report to the rear about our situation on this front. I believe that you will be most fitting for the job. After all, you know the emperor and crown prince well.” The look on the general’s face showed that he

had already made up his mind about this, and wasn’t interested in Matthias’s opinion on the matter.
The crown prince was commanding the Eighth Infantry in the rear. He was notorious for being hard to deal with, so the choice of messenger was very important.
“Yes, General,” Matthias replied, accepting the assignment without hesitation.
He was sure that Layla would benefit from having him gone for a while. He himself would benefit from it as well, perhaps even more than she would.
The general looked relieved at Matthias’s cool-headed acceptance of the assignment. Having heard rumors about the major’s strange behavior, he had worried about his mental stability, but it appeared that his worries were unfounded. He decided to scrap his plan to lecture about the importance of dignified behavior in aristocratic officers, and instead wrapped up the meeting a little early. It seemed that the young officers had the enemy forces to thank for this, specifically the bothersome guerilla fighters.
“The situation is quite urgent, so we should depart as soon as possible,” the general said with his hand to his throbbing brow. With that, he left the conference room.
Matthias put on his officer’s cap and stood up. After adjusting his already- immaculate uniform, he walked out of the building into the dazzling sunlight. It was another bright, sunny day. He snickered at himself for making such an obvious observation, but he didn’t lose his soldierly bearing even for a moment.
A driver rushed over to him and said, “We’re ready to depart, Major.”
Matthias nodded and walked towards where the military vehicles were waiting. His car departed from the square at once.







The unfamiliar child was still wandering through the forest, crying. He was now close enough for his sobbing to be audible from the cottage.
Is he waiting for his mother? Layla wondered as she sat on a chair on the porch. She was very familiar with the sadness that came from one’s mother never returning. If that was the child’s situation, she felt very sorry for him.
Even so, she hesitated to go find him again. She wasn’t sure why, but she felt like she shouldn’t approach him.
“Uncle Bill, Uncle Bill!” she shouted, hurrying towards Bill, who had just returned to the cottage, pushing a wheelbarrow. The skirt of her school uniform flapped in the breeze as she walked. “There’s a child in the woods,” she said.
“I know. I heard him,” Bill replied as he started to lift his dirt-covered tools out of the wheelbarrow.
“He keeps crying… What should we do?” she asked, rubbing her hands together nervously. “I think he’s waiting for his mother, but he’s been waiting for quite a long time.”
“Then why don’t you go find him and see how he is?”
“Me?” she asked, startled by the suggestion. “But… I don’t know him.” “Strange that you would say that,” he replied, laughing heartily. “Layla, do
you really not recognize that kid? I’m pretty sure I know who he is…” “Really? Who is it?”

Well…” he replied, smiling mischievously. Why don’t you go find him? You’ll recognize him once you see him.” With that, he carried his tools with him into the shed.
She thought about following after him, but then changed her mind and started walking in the direction of the boy’s cries.
When she found the boy, he was crouched on the side of the forest path, whimpering, while birds sang all around the summer woods.
After hesitating for a moment, she greeted him warmly, saying, “Hi there, little boy.”
He lifted his head and looked at her. His moist, blue eyes shone like gems. “What are you doing here alone?” she asked, taking a cautious step towards
him. “Where’s your mom?”
Who is he? she wondered. The more she thought about it, the more perplexed she became, until suddenly she froze. Her heart started pounding as she turned and looked back at the cottage.
Yesterday had been identical to today, and she could sense that tomorrow would be the same as well.
An eerie feeling spread through her body as she realized that she was stuck at the beginning of summer. The flower buds were not blooming, and the eggs in the waterfowls’ nests remained unhatched. The same was true of the mansion, where everyone was just as busy as ever preparing for Duke Herhardt’s return from service on the overseas war front.
She had wanted to live forever in this pleasant time during the early summer of her eighteenth year. Thus, she shouldn’t have known the child. After looking back a few more times, she faced him again.
I need to go home, she thought. I’ve got to hurry up and make dinner, because Kyle is coming over to study for the test with me.

As she continued to look at the child, however, she started to feel like she recognized him. More precisely, she felt like she recognized who he would be in about twenty years. He looked just like a young version of the man she hated most in the world, while also loving him as much as she hated him.
She hesitantly approached the child, gradually increasing her pace with each step.
Still crying, he started running towards her as well. Opening her arms widely, she took him into her embrace. The forest breeze enveloped them.
When she opened her eyes again, she found herself on her bicycle, riding down the road towards Arvis. She turned the corner and saw the verdant trees of Platanus Road laid out before her. There was a tall man in a Berg officer’s uniform sauntering down the road ahead of her.
As the sound of her bicycle’s chain mingled with the sound of the breeze shaking the leaves, she brushed past him.
Even though she felt like she shouldn’t, she found herself compelled to look back at him. As soon as she did, the two of them made eye contact. The sound of her heart pounding in her chest grew so loud that it drowned out all other sounds. Just then, she lost her balance and fell.
She felt grateful that the wheels of her overturned bicycle were spinning noisily. If it hadn’t been for that, the man might have heard her pounding heart.
He approached the spot where she had fallen. She suddenly realized that this had been the start of everything. That beautiful, early-summer day.
She then slowly opened her eyes. Looking down at her round belly, she rubbed it. The baby moved gently in response.
I’m sorry, she repeated several times as she sat up in bed. I’m so sorry I didn’t recognize you.

As she caught her breath and raised her head, her heart was racing, just like on that early-summer day. Her heart always raced when she was with him. She had always assumed this was because he made her frightened and uneasy. Now she realized that there was another reason.
The door opened. “Layla! You’re awake!” Kyle shouted, rushing towards her with a bright smile on his face. Sitting down by her bedside, he said, “I’m glad you’re up. I’ve got something to show you.”
She looked blankly at the paper in his hands. It was a document bearing the duke’s signature.




139.

Last Chance.






Layla remained silent for a long while. Showing neither joy nor shock, she just calmly read the document, occasionally nodding her head.
“Layla?” Kyle said, suddenly worrying that her condition had worsened again.
Fortunately, this seemed not to be the case. “What about him?” she calmly asked.
“The duke? He’s gone away.” “Gone away…?”
“Yeah. He went to deliver some news to the crown prince’s unit in the rear.
He’ll be gone for about four days.”
“Four days…?” she replied, parroting his words like a small child just learning to speak.

“In the meantime, we can leave. The duke himself approved this, so no one will stop us.”
“I see…”
“So you’ve got nothing to worry about, Layla. The duke has put down these promises in writing. That’s what the document’s for.”
Promises, Layla mouthed silently, as she looked again at the document Kyle had brought.
The document laid out the duke’s guarantee that he would provide Layla and her child with the means for a comfortable life. It also specified that he was not doing this out of either sympathy or hostility, but simply out of a basic sense of responsibility. There was even a detailed clause stating that he would not intervene in her life any further, unless she so washed.
Furthermore, the document stated that Layla’s child would be guaranteed the right to be legally considered the offspring of Duke Matthias von Herhardt, unless Layla would prefer to raise it without his name. Whichever option she preferred, he would respect her decision completely.
To this end, the document also specified obligations for Layla to fulfill. She and the child were to go to the Berg Empire, which was currently the safest location on the continent. If she wanted to move somewhere else after the war ended, he would respect her decision.
All other matters were left to her free choice. If she went to Ratse, the Herhardt family attorneys would ratify these promises.
Once again she stared at the document for a long while, hardly able to believe the duke had composed it when it contained such improbable words as choice, respect, and free.
She would need a while longer before she could fully grasp what was happening. She now realized that her conversation with the duke that morning

had not been a dream after all.
Noticing the unfocused look in her eyes, Kyle said, “I don’t think we need to go right now. How about departing tomorrow?” He didn’t want to rush her if she still wasn’t fully recovered, but he couldn’t help feeling antsy.
He had been completely taken aback by the things the duke had said to him in their conversation. He was letting Layla go. Kyle couldn’t believe his ears. He still could barely believe it. Perhaps this was why he wanted to leave so urgently. Better to take care of everything before the duke changed his mind.
The duke had instructed Kyle that he was to help Layla relocate while he was away on his mission. Since her apartment had been destroyed in the air raids, he had prepared another place for her. She could stay there until she had recovered enough to be able to handle long-distance travel, at which point she would leave for Ratse. The duke had entrusted Kyle with the responsibility of supervising her during this process and making sure she remained safe.
Since she hadn’t responded to his previous question, he asked, “Are you all right, Layla?”
She looked up at him but said nothing.
“I understand,” he said. “You must be shocked. I was shocked too. But Layla, this is really happening. There’s no need to be hesitant or afraid.”
He couldn’t get himself to calm down. He had repeatedly told himself not to get too excited, but it was no use. He had gotten Layla back! This thought alone made his heart swell with joy. He felt like he had gone back in time, back to the summer of his nineteenth year, before everything had gone wrong.
Layla set the document down on the bedside table, and then carefully got out of bed. When Kyle rushed over to help her stand, she gently shook her head to refuse his help.

As she tottered across the room, he followed a few steps behind her. Eventually she reached the door, and found that it wasn’t locked. She couldn’t believe her eyes. Her hands trembled as she reached out and touched the door. The chains and padlock were gone. All that remained were the dents and scratches she had created earlier while trying to escape. After a moment’s hesitation, she tried the knob. The door opened easily without resistance.
The focus returned to her eyes as the reality of the moment set in. She was really leaving him. She was free.







The car lurched to a stop as its wheels sank into another mud pit. This had happened countless times already.
With a look of resignation on his face, the driver said, “I’m sorry, Major.” Matthias nodded to the driver and stepped out of the car.
Excessive rains over the previous two days had made a mess of the dirt roads on which they were traveling. However, the paved roads and bridges that might have served as an alternative route had all been destroyed by Lovitan militia forces, so they had no other choice.
“The sun is about to set. I don’t think it will be safe to travel at night,” said the lieutenant who had come along on the mission, looking with concern at the reddening western horizon.
“Fine,” Matthias replied. “Our unit’s camp is located about an hour from here, so let’s sleep there tonight.”

“Yes, Major, I’ll let the other men know,” the lieutenant replied, looking relieved. He then ran towards the two soldiers who had just managed to push the car out of the mud and informed them of the change of plans.
Matthias checked his watch and then lit a cigarette. Even if he wasn’t going to be able to be with Layla anymore, he didn’t want to waste time in this matter. And yet here they were. A bitter smile appeared on his face as he realized how nervous he was. She still has this effect on me, he thought. Maybe she always will. Perhaps I’d better send her off earlier than planned.
The front was stable where they were. Even if guerilla forces caused trouble for them, the stability of the troops and supply lines in the rear meant that they should have no problem advancing. However, the crown prince had expressed concern over the unusual quietness of the Southern Alliance forces. Matthias felt the same.
The Southern Alliance had fought back during the early stages of the offensive, but now they were allowing Berg to take key strategic points with a suspicious level of passivity. This could be a sign that Berg and its allies had achieved an overwhelming victory, but it could also be a sign of an intentional, strategic retreat by the Southern Alliance.
What if, now that Berg had advanced deep into Lo vita, troops from other Southern Alliance nations were planning to enter Lovita and block off Berg’s retreat and supply routes?
The crown prince had expressed his concern about this worst-case scenario as well. Of course, General von Delman was certain that none of the countries in the Southern Alliance had any troops left to provide support, and so he would likely insist on continuing with the advance.
The key to the whole matter was held by the overseas kingdom of Ettarre. It was Lovita’s most trusted ally, but it had been reluctant to join the war,

making no commitment even after several months.
It would be nice for Berg if Ettarre continued to betray Lovita like this, but one could never be too sure, as Lovita would likely put a great deal of pressure on their ally to join the fight. If they did end up joining and launched a counterattack against Berg, Sienne would turn into the fiercest battlefield of the war. He needed to get Layla to Berg before that happened.
“We’re ready to go, Major,” the soldiers shouted.
Matthias threw his cigarette to the ground and started walking back to the car. Suddenly, then, he stopped in his tracks, and started looking around with narrowed eyes.
“Major, is something wr-”
Matthias raised his hand to stop the soldiers from approaching him. His other hand was already on his holster. Sensing the strange atmosphere, the other men hurried to prepare for battle. Just then, militia forces that had been hiding in the forest launched a surprise attack.
Gunshots rang out over the quiet country road.







After four days, the duke still hadn’t returned.
Layla stood in front of the window, gazing outside. Suddenly, she sensed someone standing behind her. Startled, she turned around. It was Kyle.
“Are you done packing?” he asked, smiling cheerfully. He had decided not to over analyze the fact that she had been spending every spare moment since the duke had left staring pensively out the window. “Let’s go, Layla. I know it’s

probably still uncomfortable for you to move around, but the place where you’ll be staying isn’t far from here, so-”
“Kyle…”
“Is that your only bag?” Pretending not to hear her, he calmly lifted her bag from the foot of the bed.
“Kyle, I-”
“You have to leave him, Layla.” He looked into her eyes, no longer smiling.
For the past few days, he had been entertaining a joyful fantasy. The war would suddenly end, and he and Layla would go to Ratse together. Once there, they would get married, and then they could raise the beautiful family that Layla had always wanted. He would gladly accept her first child as his own. Later on, they would have children together as well. In this way, they would live happily ever after…
“I’m not asking you to be with me again. I know you can’t do that, Layla.”
The fact that this fantasy could never be a reality only made it more beautiful to him, like a wonderful fairytale. His eyes grew solemn as he let go of that empty dream.
When she had called out to him in the middle of her fever dreams, he had known that it wasn’t because she had lingering feelings of love for him. He had known at once that she longed for him not as a lover, but as a friend, and as a surrogate brother.
It was their childhood together at Arvis that she missed.
As much as he wanted to believe in the sweet fantasy he had constructed for their future, he had spent far too much time with Layla, and knew her far too well, to think it was possible. He knew that she would never be able to be with him when she had another man’s child. No matter how willing he was to accept the child as his own, she would never accept him as its father.

However, even if they could never be lovers, Layla was still his dear friend.
He couldn’t stand the thought of her living a life of despair.
“All I want is for you to be happy,” he said. “And that man will never be able to make you or your child happy. Think about it, Layla. Think about what the duke has done to you. Your judgment is compromised right now because he’s been tormenting you.” He was speaking quite forcefully.
She looked down at her feet. She knew that he was right. The duke had relentlessly tormented her, leaving emotional scars that would never heal, all for the sake of his twisted lust and obsession over her. He didn’t love her. He wasn’t capable of love.
Kyle walked up to her and, taking her by the hand, slowly but firmly walked towards the door of the room. As she allowed him to lead her, she felt like she was walking on clouds. He opened the door and they stepped out into the hallway, only to find Marquess Lindman there, leaning casually against the wall.
“Are you really thinking of leaving, Miss Llewellyn?” he asked. His question made her wince.
“Duke Herhardt has granted permission for her to go. This has nothing to do with you, Marquess,” Kyle said, moving himself in between Riette and Layla.
Riette looked at Kyle with narrowed eyes. “Nothing to do with me? I could say the same about you, Mr. Ettman.”
“Step aside, please.”
“I’m not trying to defend Matthias, Miss Llewellyn. He’s wildly capricious. I’m sure you’re quite fed up with him. Trust me, I understand how you feel. But…” Riette let out a deep sigh, putting his hand to his forehead.
With clear eyes, Layla looked over Kyle’s shoulder at him.

“But Miss Llewellyn, still… he’s come all this way.” “Marquess!” Kyle grumbled.
“He canceled his wedding and volunteered for a dangerous assignment in the war, all so that he could come here to find you.” Riette clutched his hair as if he was going to rip it out.
Layla’s eyes grew focused as she looked at him. She looked very perplexed by what she was hearing. “What… are you talking about?” she asked.
“You didn’t know? The distinguished Duke Herhardt broke off his engagement, casting his fiancee aside. And despite everyone’s warnings and attempts to stop him, he volunteered to command a unit with an extremely dangerous mission, all so that he could come here to Sienne. You really weren’t aware of this?”
Riette now looked as perplexed as Layla. He couldn’t understand why the duke would have thrown everything away in pursuit of this woman, without telling her that he had done so.
“Layla and Duke Herhardt no longer have anything to do with each other,” Kyle said in a cold tone. “Don’t be swayed, Layla. The fact that the duke didn’t get married doesn’t change anything. He’s still the same Matthias von Herhardt as ever.”
“Kyle…”
“If this is about the baby, that’s all the more reason for you to escape the duke. He could never be a good father.”
At the mention of the baby, Layla’s trembling eyes became still. She looked down at her round belly. She knew that the duke’s feelings for her and the baby were anything but normal. Perhaps he was aware of this too, and that was why he had arranged to have her sent away like this.
“Miss Llewellyn?” Riette said in a pleading tone as he tried to approach her.

Kyle remained standing steadfastly between them, and said, ‘“This miraculous opportunity is your last chance.” He lowered his head and looked at Layla.
“Let’s go, Layla.”




140.

That Was All.






The team that had gone to deliver the message to the crown prince returned a day later than scheduled, in the late afternoon. At the staff meeting that followed their return, Matthias gave a report in which he described the situation in the rear and relayed the crown prince’s thoughts on
the ongoing strategy.
He also reported that one of the soldiers, who had been severely injured in the shootout with the militia, had been escorted from the garrison directly to the military hospital in the rear, and that another soldier, whose injuries were comparatively light, had simply had his wounds dressed before returning to Sienne.
It was only after the staff meeting had concluded that the identity of the latter soldier was revealed to be Matthias von Herhardt himself. Despite his

immaculate appearance, he went straight from the meeting to the military hospital.
When Riette heard about this, he rushed to the hospital, and found that Matthias’s treatment had already been completed.
“I hear you got injured. Are you all right?” Riette asked, looking with amazement at Matthias, who looked completely fine. He started to wonder if the news of Matthias’s injury had been false.
Meanwhile, Matthias brushed past him, saying nothing. A faint smell of disinfectant wafted off of him.
“Where are you hurt?” Riette asked. “What happened? And what’s going on with Layla Llewellyn? Huh? Say something. Come on.”
Matthias said very little in response to Riette’s persistent questioning, and then got up to leave. His face, posture, and demeanor looked just like the Duke Herhardt that Riette had always known.
“Did you lose your hearing or something? Come on, speak to me,” Riette said, following stubbornly behind him.
Soon they were ascending the stairs of the hotel. Riette couldn’t understand Matthias’s behavior at all. After throwing away his whole life because of his wild desire for Layla Llewellyn, who was carrying his own child, he had given her up to the doctor’s son.
Riette grew more and more irritated at the way his cousin was ignoring him, until they arrived at the door of Room 308. Matthias stared sullenly at the closed door.
Noticing the sudden shift in his cousin’s demeanor, Riette couldn’t bring himself to keep pestering him. There was a subtle mixture of resignation and anticipation on Matthias’s face. Most people wouldn’t have noticed this, but

Riette knew his cousin well enough to sense it. You bastard, he thought about saying, before changing his mind and shutting his mouth.
Meanwhile, Matthias reached out and turned the doorknob. The door slowly opened to reveal the room full of afternoon sunlight. Everything was neatly arranged. No trace could be seen of the woman who had been imprisoned there.
Matthias stood there for a while with his hand on the doorknob, slowly surveying the room with a calm gaze. A slight grin appeared on his lips, making him look as serene as the afternoon sunlight.
He had let her go, and she had left. He calmly accepted this simple fact. The wild obsession that had been raging within him ever since he had lost Layla in the spring was gone.
It was a lonely, empty’ feeling, but he could endure it. He didn’t have the urge to escape or deny it. He knew he had to get used to it. After all, this was what his life was going to be like from now on.
He quietly stepped over the threshold and entered the room.
Following close behind him, Riette asked, “Did you really do all those wild things for this? You hurt and humiliated Claudine, and then traveled into this warzone to capture and torment Layla, just for things to end up like this?”
Paying no mind to his cousin’s heated questions, Matthias casually removed his cap and overcoat.
“If this is what you wanted, you could’ve just…” Riette trailed off, forgetting the things he was about to shout. He stared in shocked silence at his cousin.
Matthias had taken off his uniform jacket, revealing a shirt completely soaked in blood. Riette couldn’t believe his eyes. His cousin appeared to be

severely injured, and yet not a hair on his head was out of place. It was a chilling sight.
While Riette stood at a loss for words, Matthias removed his blood-soaked shirt. The gauze wrapped around his waist was wet with blood.
“Did you get shot?” Riette asked.
“Just got nicked by a bullet or two,” Matthias flatly replied before lying down on the bed.
Muttering obscenities under his breath, Riette walked over to him. “With injuries this bad, don’t you think you should’ve stayed at the hospital?”
“We’re in a warzone.”
“So what? You think that means that bullets can’t kill you?” Riette shouted, unable to control his emotions even though he knew that it would have been impossible during wartime to hospitalize a patient with wounds this severe or transfer him to the rear.
Leaning against the cushioned headboard, Matthias gazed calmly at his cousin.
“You wild bastard,” Riette grumbled.
Matthias reacted with an amused grin. Despite his relaxed expression, however, he looked exhausted.
“I’d like to get some rest, Riette,” he said in a hoarse voice, blinking slowly.
Riette could understand his fatigue. After all, he had sent his woman away with another man, and was now pretending that nothing was wrong.
“You wild, wild bastard,” Riette repeated. These were the only words he could find at the moment.
Matthias snickered, and then raised his arm over his face to block the sunlight.

Riette departed, leaving the room in perfect silence. Matthias closed his eyes, and was transported in his mind back to the last night he and Layla had spent together.
After tossing and turning for a bit, she woke up and whispered, “I’m hot. ” Having assumed that she was going to be looking for Bill Remmer or Kyle
Ettman again, Matthias was taken aback by what she said.
“I’m hot,’’she repeated. “It’s study in here. ’’She threw the covers off her body like a cranky child.
He stopped her from getting out of bed, and then went to the bathroom and returned with a washcloth soaked in cold water. To avoid lowering her temperature too much, he carefully dabbed the cloth along the edge of her forehead.
Once he finished, she turned to him and grumbled, “I want to go outside. ’’Her eyes looked dazed, as if she was still half asleep.
Sitting in a chair next to the bed, he pulled the covers back up over her body.
“It’s study’ in here. I want to get some fresh air. ”
“Not right now, ”he whispered in a soothing tone as he brushed the hair away from her forehead.
“Why not?”she mouthed.
“It’s cold and dark outside, Layla. ”
“I don’t care. 1 want to go outside. I’m going to do what I want, ’’she mumbled, her eyes half-closed. “I’m going to do it because I know you don’t want me to. I’ll do whatever I can to torment vou.”
“Layla…”
“I hate you, so I’m going to try to get on your nerves.”

He gazed at her calmly as she continued to toss mild threats at him in her half-asleep state. Soon he let out a peaceful chuckle. He almost felt like they were back in the days when he had believed that she loved him.
“Go ahead, torment me, ”he replied. Her eyes grew hazier at this response. “Do your worst, ”he added.
“Really?”
“Really. ”
“Allright, I will,”she said. However, her eyes were almost completely closed.
She muttered a few more unintelligible words, and then fell into a deep sleep.
He continued gazing at her all night, until a warm ray of morning sunlight peeked through the window. Then he carefully lifted her out of bed and carried her with him to the chair by the window side. He opened one of the windows a crack, and a pleasant sea breeze blew in.
Holding her in his arms with the sunlight and the breeze gently washing over them, he leaned back in the chair and slowlv shut his eyes.
He knew that the moment she woke up, this lovely, playful side of her would be gone, and she would once again look at him with hatred and contempt. That was how she really felt about him, and it would never change.
He lowered his head towards her sleeping face and gently kissed her brow.
He felt like he was back in the days when she had pretended to love him.
He wanted time to stop. That way, he could keep this sweet, intimate time as his last memory’ with her. If he could do that, he would be satisfied. That would be enough for him.
When he lowered his arm from his face, the mellow evening sunlight was shining at a low angle. He gazed at the rose-linled sky with a peaceful look on his face.

He had let her go, and she had left. And now, only he remained. That was
all.








The soldier who had cleared everything out of Layla’s apartment handed her a small gift-wrapped box, saying, “This seemed important.” When he saw the confused look on her face, he added, “It was in the box in the kitchen. The box next to the bundle of foodstuffs and necessities, I mean.”
“Oh…!” Her eyes widened as she realized that the soldier was referring to the box of Uncle Bill’s belongings, which she had never been able to bring herself to open. She thanked the soldier and accepted the giftbox.
After moving the rest of her luggage into the house, the soldier left, and Layla was alone once again. Holding the box, which was topped with a colorful bow, she sat down in a chair by the window.
The place where the duke had arranged for her to stay was a small wooden house on a road near the coast. It seemed that the family who lived there had fled in quite a hurry, because most of the furniture was still there. This made her feel a little strange, so she decided to stay in the guest bedroom. She wanted to leave the house more or less undisturbed when she left.
I’m leaving…
It was only then that it dawned on her that she was really leaving. She slowly gripped the loose ends of the bow.
Matthias had returned to Sienne a day later than scheduled. This was all that Kyle had told her about him, and she had asked no follow-up questions.

Everything was over with him now. It was hard to believe, but it was true. The fact that he had called off his wedding meant nothing.
As she gazed out the window at the peaceful scenery outside, she thought about all the time she had spent while being held captive by the duke, when she had prayed endlessly that she could be free from him. The feelings she had felt then—the intense sadness, pain, and hatred—were all gone now. It was all over. It felt oddly anticlimactic.
She kept staring at the empty road outside until it started to get dark, and she remembered that a nurse was going to be stopping by soon. Impulsively, she pulled the ends of the bow, untying it, and opened the box with quivering hands. Inside, she found a pair of baby shoes.
So he knew… Uncle Bill knew all along. She started laughing and crying at once.
She thought back to the day Bill had petted her hair and said, “I have something to tell you, so I’ll wait until our party tonight.”
Now she knew what he had been talking about. He must have been planning to give her the present and congratulate her on the baby.
Just as he had found it in his heart to love the little orphan girl who had shown up with the mail one day, he had loved her child as well.
She wiped the tears from her eyes and stood up. She placed the white shoes neatly back in their box and put the box into her suitcase.
Suddenly, the feeling of emptiness in her heart went away, and she felt a renewed sense of purpose. I’m going to live a long, happy life. I promised Uncle Bill that I could do anything if he believed in me. Well, now it’s time for me to believe in him. He wouldn‘t want to see his daughter sitting by helplessly. I ’m going to become an outstanding adult for him!

“Let’s go, Baby,” she said to her belly, inside of which she could feel the baby moving.
She was sure that the duke was going to keep the promise he had made her. That was the kind of person he was. He was cruel and detestable, but he always kept his promises. Thus, as much as she hated him, she also trusted him.
His feelings for her were anything but ordinary, and now she realized that the same was true of her feelings for him. That was the kind of person they both were.
Looking out the window again, she saw that the sun had started to set. She pulled the curtains shut.







Matthias stood at the corner of the street near the house where Layla was staying. He came this way every’ single day, but never approached closer than this to the house. As much as he longed to see her, he knew that he mustn’t. Otherwise, he would never be able to let her go.
And yet, he still found himself returning to this spot, day after day. He didn’t want to leave. I guess my feelings for her still haven’t subsided. Perhaps they never will. But it will be all right. As long as she can live a good life, it will be all right. As long as I can catch the occasional glimpse of her from a distance.
He knew that he had to live a good life as well. After all, she might hear news about him now and then. He didn’t want her to hear that he was a broken man, even though that was how he felt whenever he saw her.

She was his first love, and would be his last love as well. Even if he was only a shell of a man, he intended to live as good of a life as he could. As long as Layla was living somewhere out there, he had to. However, he didn’t think he would ever love or desire anyone else.
He wasn’t bitter about this, though. If it hadn’t been for Layla, he might never have been able to experience true love. Even though he had lost her, he still had his memories of her. Those memories alone would give him the strength to go on living.
Hoping that the scar she had left in his heart would never heal, he finally turned away from the house. The tails of his overcoat flapped in the breeze as he slowly walked away.
When he visited the spot each day, he always showed up immaculately dressed, even though he knew he was going to leave without seeing her. After all, she might catch a glimpse of him through the window. Even if she was going to keep hating him forever, he wanted to look nice for her.
As he chuckled at himself for his petty vanity, he arrived at the square. Immediately, he sensed that there had been a sudden shift in the mood of the Berg soldiers. No longer enjoying a leisurely vacation, the men were moving swiftly and anxiously.
Furrowing his brow, he stepped into the hotel lobby. His aide spotted him and came running towards him. “Major! The Ettarre army has joined Sienne’s landing operation. An emergency meeting has been called, and all commanding officers have been ordered to attend!”




141.

I’m Here.






Alockdown order was issued as the soldiers in Sienne once again prepared for battle. All civilian travel passes were suspended, making it impossible
for residents to leave the city.
Kyle paced in front of the Berg military headquarters with a worried expression on his face. Tire sudden shift in the tides of the war had spoiled the plan to send Layla off.
