Without looking back, Matthias ran off into the dark city, as bombs continued to rain down.







Dropping her suitcase, Layla pressed her hands to her ears, but the noise of the bombs was still unbearably loud. She crawled under a small table in the bedroom.
You’ve got to get to the basement, she told herself. However, the combination of fear and her lingering illness made it hard to move.

The bombs sounded terrifyingly close. There were earth-shaking booms in all directions, along with the sound of windows shattering. Something bright outside the bedroom window caught her eye. She looked and saw that the house across the street was engulfed in flames.
“It’s all right, Baby,” she muttered repeatedly, wrapping her arms around her belly. She was trying to calm down both her baby and herself, but it wasn’t working on either of them.
Air full of smoke and dust started to waft in through the broken windows. She grew more and more frightened as the bombs seemed to be getting closer.
Calm down. Please. She tried to hold on to her composure even as her whole body began to tremble with fear. The clock! She looked in the direction of the table clock, which she had been checking obsessively before the air raid began. Unfortunately, it had fallen to the floor and broken.
There can’t be more than an hour left until the meeting time. With this air raid going on, will Marquess Lindman be able to make it here? If not, I’ll have to go to the meeting place alone. Should I wait for Marquess Lindman? But what if he can’t make it?
As she tried to decide what to do, another bomb shook the house’s foundation. At that moment, Matthias’s face flashed into her mind. Suddenly, she felt certain that he was going to come find her. After all, he always had in the past. Whether she liked it or not, he had always found his way back to her side, just like the birds. He had traveled to an enemy country and made it across a dangerous battlefield just to find her, so he would surely find her this time as well.
Amid the noise of the bombs, she felt like she could hear him saying, “Hello, Layla.”

Imagining him made her feel desperate and afraid, and yet at the same time, lurking behind those feelings was another feeling that was hard to identify.
Her feelings towards him were like two sides of a coin. This had been true since she had first encountered him.
That frightening and yet handsome young man had been pointing a gun at her the first time she had laid eyes on him. Soon after that, he had stepped on her gold coin. After that, she never wanted to see him again, and yet she had kept running into him. Each time, he had made her feel a little worse.
Holding back her tears, she crawled out from under the table and lifted her suitcase. She knew exactly where she was supposed to meet Kyle. The problem was making it there safely while the air raid was going on.
Don’t cry, she told herself as she staggered to her feet. Holding on to the walls and furniture for support, she started moving towards the door. However, her vision seemed to be growing blurrier with each step.
Suddenly her mind flashed back to the night she had cried while holding in her mouth the piece of candy the duke had given her. It was the first time she had been able to cry to her heart’s content. As much as she hated him, she had felt very safe and cozy in his warm arms as he had clumsily tried to comfort her. She hadn’t been able to believe that she was feeling so comfortable. The shame of this realization had made her cry even harder. He had patiently continued holding her as she cried and cried. She could still vividly remember the soft texture of his sweater against her cheek, and the sweet taste of the candy as it melted away to nothing on her tongue.
After her sobbing had subsided, she had looked up, and he had slowly, gently wiped the tears from her face. In that moment, all she could do was stare at him. She had been reminded of the last piece of blue candy that she

had saved from the jar her mother had given her, and how she had cried so much as a little girl while finally eating it. As she lay in Matthias’s arms while he wiped away her tears, she felt like that little girl’s tears were finally being wiped away as well. Instead of tasting salty with blood, the candy was only sweet this time. Just as she realized this, the duke had started smiling faintly.
That’s strange, she had thought. No one likes a crying orphan. That’s why I always try’ to smile for everyone. I’ve practiced smiling so much, I have the brightest smile in the world.
Not wanting to confront the strange feelings rising within her. she had turned away from him. He had then let her go, no longer trying to threaten her.
As she had then hurriedly left the cabin, that strange moment had stuck in her memory. After entering the forest, where the thick foliage blocked the moonlight, she had involuntarily looked back.
The path back to the cottage had felt incredibly long that night. As she walked along it, she had looked back several more times. Each time, her shadow had seemed a little larger. This had made her feel very ashamed for some reason. And yet the shadow had kept growing and growing. Perhaps she was still walking on that same road, with her shadow still growing larger.
She opened her tightly shut eyes and reached for the knob of the bedroom door. However, before she could open it, there was another loud boom, and she crouched down in fright.
It was then that she heard a voice calling her name: ‘’Layla!”
Even amid the deafening noise of the bombs, she recognized the voice at once.
It was Matthias.

His voice sounded much too clear to be her imagination, and it was getting closer.
