Just then, the door opened. Tears were blurring her vision, but she could see the man in front of her clearly.
Sure enough, it was Matthias.




142.

Promise.






They didn’t have much time to gaze into each other’s eyes. Layla soon started to stumble, and Matthias caught her. Lifting her up, he then hurried to the basement door. Still holding her, he flung the door open and slid down the ladder. Just as they reached the basement floor, another bomb landed nearby, shaking the earth and separating the two of them from each
other.
There were no lights on in the basement, so it was pitch black, but he found her again in an instant. She was leaning against the wall, quivering as she struggled to catch her breath.
Realizing that she was standing near a precarious shelf full of foodstuffs, he picked her up again and moved her far away from it.
“It’s all right,” he said.

The sound of his gentle voice brought her to her senses. She looked in his direction.
“Everything will be all right very soon,” he whispered, a little more quietly this time.
Even in the dark, she could recognize his eyes. Those eyes, as still as a deep pool, had inspired fear and hatred in her heart so many times, while other times they had filled her with anticipation.
Gazing into those eyes, she nodded several times, like a well-behaved child. Holding her in his arms, he sat down on a wooden box in a corner of the basement.
There was probably a lamp somewhere, but it wasn’t worth trying to find it right now. Thus the two of them remained sitting in the thick darkness, feeling one another’s breath and body heat as they endured the continuing bombardment. As time passed, their eyes slowly adjusted to the dark.
Without meaning to, she started reaching out to touch his face. As soon as she realized what she was doing, she hurriedly pulled her hand back. She couldn’t see him very well, but he appeared to be uncharacteristically unkempt. His body felt warm, and his heart was beating strongly.
He had come and found her. As this fact sank in for her, she was finally able to breathe freely. He had passed through a rain of bombs to find her. This made her feel a combination of relief, sadness, and a third emotion that she couldn’t identify. Soon, the mixed feelings turned into tears that started flowing from her eyes.
Now that she thought about it, she realized that the whole time she had been staying in this house, she had been waiting for him to come. She had willingly left him, and she had had no reason to believe he would come, and yet she had been waiting for him, like a fool.

Instead of putting her hand to his face, she grabbed his arm, which was still scarred from the gunshot wound.
“Just wait a little longer, Layla. It’s still too dangerous to go outside now,” he said, glancing at her for a moment before looking out into the darkness again. “Once the bombing stops, I’ll take you to where Ettman is waiting.” He hugged her trembling body more tightly.
It took her a moment to understand why he had said what he said. When she grabbed his arm, he had thought that she was rejecting him, that she was trying to push him away, like she had always done in the past. She tried to show him this wasn’t the case by shaking her head, but it didn’t seem like he noticed. She tried to ask if he was all right, but no voice came out. Gazing up at the side of his face as he continued to look away from her, she silently cried. The world was still collapsing outside, but in that moment all she could hear was his heart beating.
She started to reflect on who he was to her. He was a cruel man who had brutally tormented her, leaving indelible wounds in her heart. He had ruined her life without remorse. She hated him more than anyone else in the world. That was why she could never forgive him. That was why she had to live the rest of her life without ever seeing him again. It was all perfectly clear.
However, even when he was treating her mostly cruelly, he never quite went all the way. There was always a hint of softness in his eye, gentleness in his touch. Her memories of him were thus imbued with a certain degree of fondness. She had tried to ignore this, but it was no use. The feeling remained.
It was almost like… love.
The thought was absurd, and yet she couldn’t help thinking it. It really did seem like love. It was a strange, twisted, perhaps unhealthy form of love, which she had traveled a great distance to try to escape. And yet, for all that, it was

still love. He loved her, and she loved him. This had been true through all their time together.
Look at me, she wanted to say, but she still couldn’t make a sound. Instead, she put her hand to his cheek.
He looked down at her, seeming a little confused. Their eyes met momentarily, and then he looked away again.
Even while he continued to hold her snugly in his arms, he didn’t want to look at her. As she tried to attract his eyes once again, it occurred to her how much he had always gazed at her. She then realized that the time she had spent in his gaze had not merely been full of pain and tears.
Shaking her head to stop her tears, she put her hand to his cheek again and made him face her, just as he had done so many times to her.
They spoke at the same time. “Layla…”
“The baby…”
The sound of explosions continued to ring out upstairs.
“The baby… Our baby… It needs a name. I want you to name it.” Her hand started trembling slightly as she caressed his cheek.
He stared blankly at her, as if he hadn’t understood her words.
“I’m going to wait for you… Our baby and I will be waiting for you, so I want you to name it. Right now.”
She now understood that there had never really been a last chance. Perhaps it was a foolish choice. Perhaps her love for him was abnormal. But if she was acting unwisely, she didn’t want to be wise. Even if her love was flawed, it was her love. She loved him. She loved being with him.
“It’s all right. We’re going to be all right,” she said. She wanted to smile for him, but tears kept wetting her lips. “The baby loves you. Really. Since I love

you, the baby does too, so-” “Layla?”
“I love you.” Once she revealed this secret that she had been keeping both from him and from herself, it was like the floodgates of her feelings had been opened. “I love you. I don’t like you, in fact I hate you, but even so, I love you.” Crying like a little girl, she kept whispering, I love you. I really love you.
Each time she said it, his eyes shook slightly in response.
“If I hear you say that lie one more time, Layla… I’m really going to kill you. I’ll kill you and then myself, so please-”
“No. It’s not a lie,” she said, shaking her head urgently. “It was never a lie. Even when I said it before, when I thought I was fooling you, I was just fooling myself. It was the truth. I hated you more than I could bear. And yet, as much as I hated you, I loved you an equal amount. I still do.”
At some point in the past, he had become her everything. She disliked him, feared him, hated him. He made her suffer. He made her sad. She had wanted to be rid of him. She had wanted to escape him. Sometimes she had even wanted to die because of him. And yet, in the end…
“Layla, if you keep saying this, you’ll never be free of me,” he said, snickering as he winced with pain at the emotions he was feeling. Even as he winced, he looked very handsome.
She gazed at him with a gentle look in her eyes. She finally felt like she could start putting the pieces of her broken heart back together.
It was love. Perhaps she had already known this. Perhaps she had realized it deep down inside of her, on that beautiful afternoon when she had stood on the Sienne beach, when he had finally made it through hell to find her.
She continued to caress his face, feeling his forehead, nose, and lips with the tips of her fingers. Then she put her hand to his cheek again and smiled.

“Don’t discard me,” she whispered. “Return to me. If you do, I’ll forgive you for everything. I won’t hate you anymore. Then we can have a fresh start together.” She felt a quickening heartbeat. She couldn’t tell if it was his or her own,
and she didn’t care.
“We can have a fresh start without hatred or sadness, with nothing but love,” she said. “I’m confident I can do this, and I know the baby can too. Can you?”
Her tears started flowing again. If he can’t do it, what will I do?
As her heart was filling with fear and trepidation over this thought, another bomb struck nearby, shaking everything around them. However, she could barely hear it.
They sat with their foreheads touching. They could feel the warmth of one another’s breath. Soon, he put his lips to hers. They kissed each other passionately. She hugged him tightly and clutched at his soft hair. In that moment, it was like he was the only thing that existed in her world.
After a while, they stopped for a moment. Struggling to catch her breath, she said, “Promise me. I want you to promise that you’ll return to me. Soon.”
“Fine,” he said as he kissed her on the temple. “I will.”
As tears continued to flow down her cheeks, she smiled. “I trust you to keep your promise. No matter what anyone says, I’ll always trust you. So you’d better not be lying, all right?”
“I promise, Layla.” Hugging her tightly, he nodded. “I’ll return to you.” Seeing the innocent, unconditional trust in her eyes, he slowly smiled. “I’ll return to you. I promise.”







The air raid stopped just as the sun was starting to rise. Matthias ran through the ruined city, holding Layla in his arms. The meeting time had already passed. Even if Kyle and Riette stalled, they wouldn’t be able to delay leaving much longer.
Now that the bomber planes had left, the sky was filled with the faint light of dawn, along with a lot of smoke and some flocks of birds moving uneasily here and there.
Matthias was breathing heavily, but he didn’t stop or slow down. He needed to get Layla safely out of the city. This singular objective compelled him to keep moving.
The spot where Kyle would be waiting for them was the north gate of the rampart surrounding the city. Little-frequented by people under normal circumstances, the spot was especially empty now that the army was gathering to respond to the surprise attack. In the clear blue light of the early morning, all that Layla could see were collapsed remnants of the stone wall, and all she could hear was the mingled sound of the ocean waves and Matthias’s heavy breathing.
They turned a corner of the rampart, and Riette spotted them. Rejoicing, he shouted, “Matthias! Hurry! We’ve got to go!”
The ambulance was waiting right at the designated spot. It was clear from Riette’s anxious expression that there was no time to spare.

Matthias made it to the ambulance in the blink of an eye. Riette opened the passenger door, and Matthias placed Layla, his precious cargo, on the middle seat, next to Kyle. She grabbed his sleeve. Placing his hand on hers, he whispered, “Leah.”
When she realized why he was saying this name, a smile spread across her tear- moistened lips.
As he started to turn away, she grabbed his hand more tightly and said, “W- we need one more! What if it’s a boy?”
“Hmm…” He furrowed his brow as if he hadn’t considered this possibility.
After a moment, he calmly said, “Felix.”
Riette’s eyes widened as he heard the name.
Duke Felix von Herhardt was Matthias’s grandfather, the man who had brought prosperity to the Herhardt family in a rapidly changing time. By giving this name to his son, Matthias was essentially designating him as his successor, the future duke who would carry on the family honor.
Riette looked blankly at Layla, wondering if she realized the significance of the choice of name.
Nodding like a well-behaved child, she kept a stiff upper lip as she tearfully let go of Matthias’s sleeve.
He had promised her everything and asked her to marry him. She had accepted his proposal. But now it was time for them to part. It was time for her to take off and leave him there.
Damn it, Riette muttered, holding back his tears.
There wasn’t time to take in the emotion of the moment. As soon as Matthias stepped back, Riette hurried into the vehicle and sat in the passenger seat. Sitting in between two large men, Layla was almost completely hidden.

There was no time for goodbyes, so the ambulance started moving immediately. Standing in front of the ruined rampart, Matthias watched the vehicle start to drive off. Just as he was about to turn back, the ambulance came to an abrupt stop.
The passenger door opened, and Riette got out. Then Layla stumbled out of the ambulance and started running towards Matthias.




143.

Wishes.






As Layla ran wildly towards him, Matthias ran towards her as well. Once they were near each other, he put his arm around her to help her keep
her balance. She could feel that he was shaking just as much as she was. “Layla,” he whispered, unsure what else to say.
She looked at him square in the face. Her tear-filled eyes glistened like emeralds in the morning sun. She stood for a moment without saying anything, only breathing deeply in and out. Then she hurriedly untied the ribbon from the end of her French braid.
He understood why she was doing this at once.
Sure enough, she grabbed his arm and tied the ribbon to his wrist, just like the strings she had used to tie to the legs of Arvis’s migratory birds.
That was all. She couldn’t bring herself to say anything to him. She was worried that if she opened her mouth, something foolish would come out.

He looked alternately at the white ribbon on his wrist and at her face, and then smiled and nodded. It was the same smile, tinged with playful arrogance, that had taken her breath away many times before.
The alarm calling for the troops to gather started sounding again.
Her lips began to quiver, but she didn’t let her tears fall. He caressed her rosy cheek and nodded once more, saying, “Go, Layla.” He brushed her wind- swept hair off her face and stooped down to kiss her tear-stained cheek. Then he whispered, “I love you.”
Even with the siren blaring in the background, she heard his voice loud and clear, and recognized that this was their last moment together for the time being. She knew she had to return to the ambulance, where Kyle and Riette were urgently calling for her.
Matthias turned and started walking towards the siren, which was going off in the square.
As soon as she turned away from him and started running back towards the ambulance, she burst into tears. Even as she sobbed like a little girl, however, she didn’t stop running or look back. She didn’t want to make things harder for Matthias. To her reasoning, the easier it was for him to part from her now, the easier it would be for him to come back to her when the time came. Just like her beloved Arvis birds, which always returned to her without fail.
The ruined city looked ghastly in the bright morning sunlight. Before turning the corner of the rampart, Matthias looked over his shoulder at Layla. As she ran, her loose blonde hair fluttered against her back, like a bird’s wings.
He paused for a moment to take in the sight of those beautiful golden waves amid the ashen ruins. Then he turned again and started running towards what was about to become a fierce battlefield.

That morning, Ettarre’s large military officially entered the war alongside its Southern Alliance allies and broke through the Northern Alliance’s line of defense at the coast. On that dazzlingly bright autumn day in Sienne, the largest battle of the war ensued.





Downtown Ratse was full of newsboys selling extra editions of the newspaper. Claudine glanced out of the window of the cafe and saw yet another depressing headline. By now she was used to this, as were the ladies sitting across from her.
For months now, there had been nothing but bad news coming from the war’s front line. After winning battle after battle in the first stages of the war, the Berg military had lost the battle with Ettarre, and the Southern Alliance had recaptured Sienne. Ever since then, Berg had been struggling in the war, and the front line had been gradually receding northward.
More recently, the Northern Alliance had launched a full-scale attack to great success. This had leveled the playing field somewhat, but not enough for it to be cause for celebration yet. The year was coming to an end, and it seemed that the emperor’s promise that the victorious soldiers would come home before the new year was not going to pan out.
“Don’t worry, Lady Claudine. Duke Herhardt will be safe,” one of the ladies at the table said after noticing the look on Claudine’s face.
“That’s right,” another lady said. “No matter how bad things get on the battlefield, the duke will surely make it home safe and sound.”

Duke Herhardt…?Ah, yes. My distinguished fiance, Claudine thought with a subtle grin on her face.
Officially, Matthias and Claudine were still engaged, so it was only proper for her to act worried about him. She deftly maintained this charade on the surface, while inside she didn’t care whether he lived or died.
Earlier in the year, she had ardently hoped that he would return alive and become her husband, but these feelings had vanished with the changing of the seasons. She no longer knew what she wanted. Nor did she know why she was continuing to pretend like they were engaged. Sometimes she even hoped that she would hear of his death in battle. That would be the cleanest way to break off the engagement, after all.
And then what? she asked herself. Her eyes quivered at this question.
She received letters from the battlefield frequently. Naturally, they all came from Riette. She and Matthias had never exchanged letters, even before the war.
She had been getting news about Layla through letters from Riette. Upon learning that the duke had gone all the way to Sienne just to find that orphan girl, she didn’t even get angry. When she learned that Layla was pregnant with the duke’s child, she simply laughed.
Our engagement is over, she thought to herself, finally accepting what she had been in denial about for months.
However, she did not want to let the engagement end in a way that benefited the duke. He and Layla were the ones who had done wrong, so they were the ones who should suffer.
I hope they die, she calmly thought. Yes, I hope that the duke, that lowly orphan, and their baby all vanish from the earth.

If her hope were to become a reality, then the engagement would automatically be canceled, and Claudine would be able to make a fresh start without any stains in her reputation. Marrying the cousin of her dead fiance was not the best look, but it wasn’t bad enough to ruin her honor.
Yes, if they die, I’ll be able to write a response to Riette at once.
Even as she continued to participate in the conversation with the other ladies at the cafe, Claudine thought of nothing but Riette’s letter. Specifically, it was his declaration of love that was sticking in her mind.
She had thought that she had lost him forever. When she read that he still loved her, she cried tears of joy. However, she didn’t feel ready to write a response right away.
She wanted everything to be perfect. This time, she wanted a marriage full of both love and honor, not a flashy facade with nothing underneath like her engagement to the duke had been. To that end, she was ready to cry loudly at Matthias’s funeral, if he would only hurry up and die. Of course, she would be crying out of joy, not sadness.
After exchanging pleasantries with the other ladies for a short while longer, she rose from her seat.
With the war raging across the continent, lavish high-society events had ceased. It seemed like every day there was news of another young man from an aristocratic family dying on the battlefield. It was a gloomy winter, full of funerals and lamentation. The ladies were meeting in Ratse because they were all attending a funeral there.
Claudine got into the Brandt family car that was waiting for her outside the cafe, and was driven out of the downtown area. She sat quietly for a while, lost in thought, until something outside caught her eye just as the car had

turned onto the boulevard on which the natural history museum and the art museum were located.
There was a blonde woman with glasses walking down the sidewalk in front of the natural history museum. The distance was too great to make out much detail, but she looked quite familiar.
“Is something the matter, Miss?” Claudine’s maid asked.
Claudine thought for a moment, then shook her head and leaned back into her seat.
Could it have been Layla?
The last thing she had heard about Layla from Riette was that Matthias had tracked her down and abducted her, after she had escaped while pregnant with his child.
Could Matthias have sent her here? To Ratse?
Whatever was going on, Claudine didn’t want to be involved in the messy affairs of those two anymore. The only feeling towards them that she still retained was the wish that they would die in the war.
All in all, it was an ordinary day. Before going to bed, she wished that another letter would come from Riette, and that this time she would be able to send a response letter back, even if only a short one. Miraculously, her wish came true the next morning.
“Miss Claudine! A letter just arrived from Marquess Lindman!” her maid shouted, smiling brightly as she entered Claudine’s bedroom.
Claudine had been lounging in bed, sipping her morning tea. As soon as she heard that a letter had arrived from Riette, she sprung from bed with just as much excitement as her maid was displaying. It was only after she had run barefoot over to her maid and taken the letter from her that she realized how indecorously she was behaving.

Blushing slightly, she found her slippers and put them on. The maid pretended not to notice her mistress’s impropriety. Meanwhile, the bedroom door flung open without a knock. It was Countess Brandt, showing just as much disregard for proper etiquette as her daughter. “Oh, Claudine! What ever will you do?!”
Before the startled Claudine could say a word, her mother had burst into tears. In her hand, the countess was holding the morning newspaper.
Claudine’s heart started to pound with anticipation.







Layla’s heart always started to race when it was time for the paperboy to deliver the morning newspaper. She could tell that this time had arrived without checking the clock.
She paced in front of the house, anxiously eyeing the corner across the street where the arriving paperboy would first be visible.
Back on that bright autumn morning, she made it safely out of blockaded Sienne by hiding in the ambulance.
After dropping Marquess Lindman off at his rear supply unit, Kyle drove on to the military hospital past the Lovita-Berg border, where he and she parted ways. Then, with nothing to guide her but a strip of paper with an unfamiliar address written on it, she boarded a train headed for Ratse. It was just like when she had taken the train to Karlsbar as a little girl, so many years earlier.

She felt sadness surging inside her, but she didn’t let herself cry. This time, she wasn’t alone, because she had the baby inside her. And soon enough she would have Matthias as well.
She had no doubt that he would return to them. Thus, all she had to do now was fully recover from her illness, deliver the baby safely, and then the two of them could wait for Matthias to arrive. It was simple. She was sure she could do it.
Several times each day, she renewed her resolve, whispering, We can do it. In this way, she was able to make it through each day. Meanwhile, the seasons changed and the baby continued to grow inside her.
I’ll get to meet you soon, Baby. What a miracle it will be if the war ends soon enough for your father to arrive before you do!
She knew how unlikely this was to happen, but she wished ardently for it nonetheless.
As confident as she felt about the future, she did worry about the fact that if she were to die in childbirth, there would be no one to look after the baby. Of course, there was the duke’s butler and attorney, who had helped her get settled at the new house, but she didn’t know them well enough to trust them with her baby’s life.
“It’s all right. It’ll all be just fine,” she whispered to her unborn baby, just as the duke had whispered to her.
It was then that she heard the quick footsteps of someone walking towards

her.
Across the wintry street, she saw the paperboy running in her direction.




144.

A Pure, Bright Light.





“What about Claudine? How is she doing?” Countess Brandt asked in a hoarse voice as she wiped another tear from her puffy eyes.
Claudine’s maid Marie lowered her eyes, not sure what to say. She neither felt comfortable telling the countess the truth, nor lying to her. “Well…”
Luckily, the countess was in such an emotional state herself that she didn’t seem especially7 concerned about her daughter. “I’m sure she’s distraught, but she’s got to keep it together,” she said. “We all need to keep a stiff upper lip in these times. You should bring her something to eat. She’s going to waste away if she doesn’t have a proper meal.”
Breathing a sigh of relief, the maid hurried to carry out the countess’s order. Knowing full well that Claudine wouldn’t eat it, she prepared some food and brought it to her bedroom. She knocked at the door. When no voice replied, she called to her mistress through the door. “Miss? Miss Claudine?”
Instead of a reply, she heard a wild, pained groan coming from inside the room.
“M-miss! Miss!”

Forgetting propriety, Marie opened the door and entered the room. When she saw the state her mistress was in, she dropped the tray in shock.
Claudine, normally so mild-mannered, was on the floor in her pajamas, sobbing hysterically. In her hand was a letter. “Go away!” she shrieked once she noticed that her maid was there. “Leave, Marie!”
“But, Miss…”
“Please, get out of my room! Please, Marie…” She lowered her head and started beating the ground.
The maid didn’t dare approach any closer. She was perplexed. Claudine had seemed to be doing just fine for the previous several days.
Curled up on the floor, Claudine started sobbing again. The maid hovered anxiously by the door for a short while, and then left the bedroom.
Now that she was alone again, Claudine started wailing even more vehemently. She could hardly remember how she had ended up on the floor, sobbing like this. She had simply read the letter. After a sleepless night, morning had come, and then, finding the morning unbearable, she had simply picked up the letter again and read it.
I love you, Claudine.
He had declared his love several times through the course of the letter.
I know what you’re afraid of. Perhaps what you’re fearing will become a reality. But Claudine, I promise you that I’ll love you so much, you ’ll forget about all of that.
After sending that letter, in which he declared his absolute love and commitment to her, he died.
The obituary in the newspaper that the countess had brought her was not for Duke Herhardt, but for Marquess Lindman. She received his love letter and the news of his death at the same time. There had been an enemy air raid to

sever the rear supply route, and in the ensuing battle, Riette had been shot and killed.
“No… No! No!!” Claudine screamed, while behind her the pale sunlight of the winter morning flowed in through the window.
For several days afterward, she remained in a state of shock.
So that’s it, she flatly thought. You sent me that letter full of your passionate love for me, and then you died. The man whose death I wished for continues to walk the earth, while you, the man I was waiting for, are gone. You vanished before I could ever tell you I love you. Well, I love you, Riette. I love you, I love you, I love you. That’s all I had to say to you, but I never could. Why did it have to be like this? Why? She turned her eyes to his letter once more, and started reading it aloud.
When she reached the first “I love you, ’’she felt like she was sinking into a bottomless pit of despair.
