As she held the letter to her chest, she thought that she could feel warmth amid the ink and paper. It was as if the warmth of Riette’s hands had been transferred into the letter itself. When she listened carefully, she thought she could hear his wonderful voice and the sound of his laughter as well. At that moment, she felt something deep inside of her break. Whatever it was that had broken, she was certain that she would never be whole again.
“I love you!” she shouted. Struggling to open her tear-filled eyes, she looked out the window. Even without Riette, the world was full of light. It was a pure, bright light.







The image of those beautiful golden wings fluttering amid the ashen ruins stood out in Matthias’s memory. The memory reminded him that he needed to make it through whatever obstacles arose in his path, so that he could make it back to Layla.
He opened his eyes and staggered to his feet. The bright afternoon sunlight stung his eyes.
The soldier next to him, who up until a moment earlier had been exchanging gunfire with the enemy, was now collapsed on the cold, stone floor, covered in blood. He had been hit by shrapnel from a mortar shell. Blood oozed from his head and chest and flowed across the floor until it touched Matthias’s boots. It struck Matthias that if he had been the one in front of the window, it would have been him hung lifeless on the floor.
Life and death were one step apart. Perhaps the same had been true of Riette.
Looking at the dead soldier, Matthias felt like he was looking at his own corpse.
He took a moment to catch his breath, and then picked up the gun he had dropped. •Just then, another mortar shell came flying, and he was surrounded by the deafening sound of the stone walls around him starting to crumble from the explosion. However, he kept walking, undeterred.
Despite predictions to the contrary, the enemy had launched their air raid in the middle of the day. They were trying to recapture a city that Berg had

occupied a few weeks earlier. The city was the site of a junction where three railways met. This made it an important supply hub, while also making it the site where a fierce battle would inevitably be fought.
As Matthias continued to walk, he spotted an enemy soldier hiding behind a pillar, aiming his gun at him. Without hesitating, Matthias fired his gun. The soldier collapsed without so much as a yelp. Matthias stepped over the dead body and exited the tower.
As he stepped out into the bright sun above the rampart, a cool breeze enveloped him. He could see that the section of the rampart across from him was already half-destroyed by enemy bombing. The spot where he was standing would likely meet a similar fate before long.
It appeared that the enemy, finding it difficult to penetrate Berg’s defenses, had decided instead to destroy the ramparts completely in order to enter the city. This was the tactic that the Berg army had chosen while advancing onto the city, so Matthias recognized it at once.
Ettarre’s entry into the war had changed many aspects of the front line. Previously, the Southern Alliance had been at a considerable disadvantage as they had lacked siege mortars that could destroy ramparts. Now, however, thanks to Ettarre, they were armed with the same munitions as Berg, and were counterattacking with the same tactics Berg had used during its relentless advance.
“Retreat! Retreat!”
Desperate cries ordering the retreat of the troops assigned to defend the rampart could be heard among the artillery’ fire.
Matthias found it odd that the commander was issuing this order now, when over half of the Berg soldiers had already been turned into corpses. He

should have issued the order at least an hour earlier, if he wanted more people to listen to it.
Snickering to himself, Matthias quickened his walking pace. He wanted to run, but his body wasn’t cooperating.
Am I injured? he wondered. However, the incessant noise of the artillery fire was making it hard to think.
The general had not grasped the extent of the enemy’s firepower. He had assumed that they would continue fighting in the same reckless manner as before, sending a horde of foot soldiers Lo attack. Tims, Berg soldiers had been placed along the rampart to defend against this. This had turned out to be a major blunder, however. With the Berg soldiers positioned like this, it was like shooting fish in a barrel for the enemy. However, it was hard to blame the general for this. No one had suspected that the Southern Alliance possessed the same weapons as Berg until the moment the Lovita military’ fired its first mortar shell.
As Matthias’s vision blurred in the bright sunlight, he once again thought he could see those beautiful golden wings fluttering in mid-air. He had been seeing them on a daily basis since sending Layla off. Even amidst disastrous defeats and successful counterattacks, the image of those golden wings motivated him to keep going. The little bird gave him the courage and drive to survive.
“Layla,” he said in a sweet tone, smiling.
He blinked repeatedly, keeping his eyes closed a little longer each time. His urgent steps also slowed, until he was sauntering along like someone enjoying a leisurely stroll. The moment he realized this, he collapsed onto the sun- heated stone surface of the rampart.

He opened his gently shut eyes and looked back at the path he had just walked down. There was a trail of dark-red marks along the floor. It took him a few moments to realize that these marks were his own blood.
He checked his body and discovered that the left half of his uniform was soaked with blood. It seemed that he had been hit in the shoulder blade by shrapnel from a mortar blast. The heaviness of the bleeding suggested that it was quite a severe wound, but oddly, he didn’t feel any pain. He simply chuckled hoarsely at the sight of the blood. This wasn’t a good sign.
After remaining on the floor looking up at the bright sky for a while longer, he tried to stand up. However, he couldn’t muster the strength. He staggered partway up a few times, each time collapsing back to the floor. His breathing gradually grew more labored, and soon he could detect the rusty scent of blood in his breath.
Leaning against the railing, he tried to maintain consciousness. He firmly clutched his head and told himself, Stand up! However, it was no use. Meanwhile, his eyelids started to feel very heavy.
The earsplitting artillery’ fire nearby seemed to recede into the distance, becoming faint and muffled.
He felt like taking a short nap. He thought that if he could just get some rest, he would then be able to stand up and walk again.
Layla… I’ll make it back to you… Just like I promised…
Just as his eyes were almost completely closed, he noticed the end of a ribbon flapping in the breeze in front of him. It was the hair ribbon that Layla had tied onto his wrist before leaving. Since then, it had become something of a lucky charm for him.
He chuckled as he recalled the sight of her wandering through the forest and riverside of Arvis, looking for migratory birds and tying colored string to

their legs. When he thought about how much she had longed for their return, and how joyful she was when they finally did come back, it struck him that she had been a very lonely child.
Looking blankly at the ribbon flapping in the breeze, he thought about her life, and how full it was with people who never returned, no matter how long she waited for them. Finally, he understood why she loved Arvis’s migratory birds.
Layla… he silently mouthed, unable to make a sound anymore.
He had promised that he would return to her. She had told him she trusted that he would, that she would always trust him. Thus he had to return to her.
Biting his lip, he stood up. Even as his consciousness was growing hazy, the sound of artillery fire remained loud and clear.
He began to stagger along on top of the remains of the rampart, which had been reduced to a dusty pile of rubble. He could barely walk straight, but he didn’t stop moving. However, he only made it a few more steps before he fell down again.
He pressed his blood-soaked hands against the floor to support his body. Hoarse laughter continued to flow from his lips. He re-opened his tightly shut eyes, and found that his vision had become a bit clearer again. Tears and sweat collected at the end of his chin and then dripped onto the back of his hands. First one drop, then another.
He kept blinking to try to regain his focus. Each time he did, the tears dripping onto his hands grew thicker. This made him chuckle even more. The smell of blood in his breath was growing stronger.
I must survive, he whispered, just as he had been whispering Layla’s name before. I must survive for you, Layla. My Layla, you ’re waiting for me to come back to you, so I must survive.

It took even longer for him to muster the strength to stand up this time, but eventually he managed to get to his feet. Wiping his hands across his face, he straightened his posture and started plodding forward. His bloodshot eyes were still half-closed, but he didn’t let that slow him down.
He had to keep going. He didn’t want to become yet another longed-for thing in that lonely woman’s life that would never return to her. He wanted to keep his promise, so that she would forgive him. And then he wanted to start the new life with her that she had described, full of love and nothing else.
Soon he caught sight up ahead of stairs leading down from the rampart. He quickened his pace. Just as he stepped onto the first stair, a mortar shell struck nearby, causing the section of the rampart where he was to begin to collapse.
As the foundation underneath him started to give way, he saw those bright, golden wings fluttering in front of him. This was his last memory;
Layla… he whispered, smiling.







The news of that day’s battle in Lovita did not reach Berg until several days later.
A photograph of Duke Herhardt in his officer’s uniform was printed on the front page of the Ratse newspaper’s extra edition for New Year’s Eve, which was distributed quickly throughout the city.




145.

New Year’s Eve.






The morning newspaper remained folded on the table well past noon. Layla wanted to open it, but she was scared of what she might find in it.
Her hand hovered over it for a moment before she pulled it away again, just as she had done several times through the morning.
Stepping away from the table, she said some soothing words to her belly, and warmed up a glass of milk. She fidgeted with the curtains, opening them up and then pulling them ever so slightly together again, until she felt that she had gotten them just how she wanted them. Then she threw some more wood on the fire, even though it was already blazing brightly. After doing all this, she finally felt ready to open the newspaper.
Thankfully, she saw no mention of the duke on the front page. She breathed a sigh of relief, knowing that if the duke had died his picture would likely have been printed under the day’s headline. After a moment, she started

carefully turning through the pages with a trembling hand. When she reached the page that listed the soldiers who had died in the war, she reflexively closed her eyes.
Ever since reading of Marquess Lindman’s death, she had become even more frightened of the newspaper. However, she still felt compelled to read it every day.
“Bye, Miss Llewellyn. Or should I say, Duchess Herhardt?” Riette had said to her, after stepping out of the ambulance to return to his unit. “I guess the baby will have been born the next time we meet. I’ll come visit so I can see the little one. That’s all right with you, isn’t it?”
“Of course. ”
“Thanks. And… sorry. ”
“What do you mean? Why are you sorry, Marquess?” she had asked, tilting her head.
With a wry smile reminiscent of Matthias’s, he had replied, “Oh, just this and that. I’m sorry for being mean to you when we were younger. And I’m sorry for what Claudine put you through. ”
She hadn’t understood why he was apologizing on Claudine’s behalf, but there wasn’t enough time to get into a discussion about it.
After walking a few steps away, he had looked over his shoulder and shouted, “Oh! I’m going to pray that the baby only resembles its mother! Especially if it’s a boy! Think of it as my gift to my new niece or nephew. ”
That playful remark was her final memory of Riette.
She took off her glasses and set them down on the table, then rubbed her teary eyes until they hurt. Her fists felt as cold as ice.
After a while, she read the rest of the names on the list of fallen soldiers. She was relieved to find no other familiar names on the list, but she still felt

uneasy.
She decided to take a walk to calm her nerves. Moving at a slow pace, she passed from the residential area to the boulevard where the museums were located, finally reaching the park at the boulevard’s end before turning back. Now that she was near the end of her pregnancy, it took her quite a long time to walk even short distances.
“Look at Mommy. I’m waddling like a penguin. See?” she said, giggling, as she looked at her reflection in a shop window. “Oh no! I guess you can’t see through my belly. But you’ll be able to see soon enough! I guess I won’t look like a penguin then, though.” She laughed once again as she realized the silly things she was saying.
After pausing a short while to catch her breath, she started carefully walking again. Even with a war going on, there was a lively atmosphere downtown on this final day of the year. All around, there were couples and families with excited faces full of the spirit of New Year’s Eve.
“Should we go out for a special dinner tonight?” she asked her belly. “I wonder what kind of food you’ll like. Will your taste be more like Mommy’s or Daddy’s?” She gently rubbed her belly, but the baby did not move in response. This made her feel a little lonely, so she started stepping more forcefully. “Come to think of it, Mommy doesn’t know what Daddy likes to eat. I should’ve asked him.” Her sullen words turned to steam as they came out of her mouth. “You know, there’s a lot I don’t know about that guy. I’ll have to ask him about everything when he comes back to us.” With that resolution, she picked up her pace. Even so, however, she was the slowest person on the sidewalk.
Just as she was passing by the museums, newsboys selling extra editions appeared.

“Extra, extra, read all about it!”
The sound of their cries dampened the festive atmosphere at once. By now everyone knew that extra editions during wartime usually carried bad news.
Layla came to a stop, feeling an odd sensation in her belly. Her heart started pounding loudly, her breath quickened, and her hands felt frigid inside her mittens. Because of this, she dropped the newspaper she had just purchased.
She wanted to pick it up, but her arms wouldn’t move. As she stared down at the newspaper, she saw a very familiar face on the front page, along with an unbelievable headline: Duke Matthias von Herhardt Killed in Battle While Defending Strategic Point on Southern Front.
As she slowly blinked, her eyelashes cast quivering shadows on her tear- filled eyes. Suddenly everything felt strange and dreamlike. Killed in battle…? The phrase seemed meaningless, as if it were from a foreign language.
It can I be. He promised he would return to me. That means he will return to me.
But it says he was killed in battle. How can that be?
“Let me get that for you,” a middle-aged woman said, noticing the newspaper Layla was staring at on the ground. She picked it up and placed it in Layla’s hands, and then started to walk away.
Before long, however, the newspaper fell to the ground again. This time, Layla fell down with it. Moaning in pain, she tried to stand up again, but she couldn’t manage it. Her unfocused eyes remained helplessly fixed on the front page of the newspaper. Her hand shook as she ran her mitten across the face in the picture.
“Goodness! Are you all right, young lady?” exclaimed the woman who had just helped her. As the woman ran towards Layla, the eyes of everyone nearby followed her. “This young lady needs help! Please!” the woman shouted

urgently as she watched Layla pant and clutch her belly. “I think she’s going into labor! She needs to be taken to the hospital! Right now!”







“It’s a lie. I will not believe it. It’s simply impossible!”
Elise von Herhardt’s piercing voice echoed in the silent drawing room. Besides Elise, the room was full of relatives who had quickly gathered upon news of Matthias’s death. They were all looking at Elise.
“I understand how you feel,” one of the relatives said, “but you need to accept reality-”
“My son is not dead! He can’t have died! It’s inconceivable, I’m telling you!” Elise exclaimed, shaking her head so vigorously that her elegant chignon came undone and her hair spilled down onto her shoulders. She then rose from her seat and approached Dowager Herhardt, saying, “You agree, don’t you? Please tell them. Please say it isn’t so, it can’t be so.”
“Oh my…” Katharina replied. As she watched her daughter-in-law tearfully pleading with her, her own eyes started to fill with tears again. She had lost her husband, her son, and now her grandson. She cursed her own longevity as she reflected on how her long life had been full of so much sadness. “I think we need to accept it, Elise,” she said, wiping her tears with her handkerchief. “For the sake of Matthias’s honor, we need to accept that he is gone. Surely you realize that, don’t you?” The calm rationality of her words was belied by the shakiness in her voice.

Elise glared resentfully at her mother-in-law, then sobbed mournfully for a moment, and then staggered and fell to the floor.
The news of Matthias’s death had caused yet another stir at Arvis. The Arvis staff were frantically busy serving and taking care of the guests who had flocked to the estate. Hessen, as the butler in charge of the whole staff, wasn’t able to sit down at his desk until late into the night. Just as he was starting to feel the sadness that he had been repressing all day, he was interrupted by a servant rushing into his office.
“Mr. Hessen, Mr. Stauf is on the telephone. He says he urgently needs to speak with you.”
Mr. Stauf, the attorney in charge of the Herhardt family’s internal legal issues, had secretly helped Layla get settled in Ratse, as the duke had instructed. If he was calling this late at night, it had to be an emergency.
Hessen ran to the phone and picked up the receiver.
“Miss Llewellyn is at the hospital,” Mr. Stauf said. “She went into labor earlier today.”
Hessen’s eyes widened. “Isn’t this earlier than expected?”
“It is. Perhaps it was because of the shock of learning that His Grace Duke Herhardt passed away. I’ve only just spoken with the hospital. I wanted to contact you before I departed.”
“I see. Thank you, Mr. Stauf. I’ll attend to my duties at once.”
Hessen hung up the receiver with a trembling hand. A baby born on the day its father’s death is announced to the world! He closed his eyes to try to stop them from filling with tears. Should I let people know?
If the baby turned out to be a boy, it would become Duke Herhardt’s sole heir.

Revealing the baby’s existence would prevent a bitter fight from breaking out among Herhardt relatives over the question of succession. In that respect, it seemed like a good idea.
However, the duke had clearly stated that he didn’t want that. He had left everything up to Layla Llewellyn’s choice. And even if he was no longer alive, Matthias von Herhardt was still Hessen’s master.
Biting his lip, the butler stepped out into the dark hallway.







The baby was born on New Year’s Eve.
Late at night, not long before the midnight bell would ring in the new year, the baby announced itself with a high-pitched cry.
A nurse wrapped the newborn in a white swaddling cloth and placed it in the arms of its mother, who had bravely endured the pain of childbirth alone. A mixture of joy and fear spread across Layla’s pale, drowsy face.
“The baby is healthy,” the nurse said, smiling. “It’s a little small, but it has no health problems at all.”
“Really…?” Layla asked, looking worriedly at the nurse.
The baby had been through many hardships while it was in her belly, and now it had been delivered prematurely. Layla’s lips were quivering with concern.
“Absolutely. Look for yourself,” the nurse replied. She helped Layla adjust her posture so that she could hold the baby more comfortably, and then took a step back.

Layla cautiously looked down at the baby in her arms.
As she gently stroked its black hair, it wriggled its tiny body. Its skin looked very pale, perhaps because of the contrast with its dark hair.
After staring blankly for a moment, Layla grinned slightly and caressed the baby’s cheek. Her eyes filled with tears as she felt the unbelievable softness of its skin. Just then, the bell started ringing in the distance. As if in response, the baby opened its eyes for a moment before shutting them tightly again.
In that brief moment, Layla saw that they were the same beautiful shade of blue as its father’s. Tears flowed down Layla’s cheeks, wetting her smiling lips.
As she cried these tears of joy while the New Year’s bell rang in the background, she whispered, “Our baby is so pretty. Won’t you come and see? Please?”




146.

Summer Again.


Winter gave way to spring, and spring gave way to summer.
After an arduous war in which both sides suffered heavy casualties, the Northern Alliance finally emerged victorious. In many ways it was a pyrrhic victory—the gains were small and the losses were large. However, in the eyes of history, the victory would stand as another glorious achievement brightening the legacy of the Berg Empire.
“It’s a dismal sort of glory, isn’t it?” a noblewoman remarked, sighing deeply as she stood in line to greet the victorious soldiers returning to the empire. “I suppose at the very least it’s fortunate that the war ended before all of the empire’s families had their lines of succession cut off.”
The other noblewomen standing near her nodded in agreement, and started naming various young men who would never return to their families.
“Who will the Herhardt family choose as their successor?”
As soon as Matthias von Herhardt was mentioned, the conversation naturally shifted to what had become the hottest topic for gossip lately—the question of who would become the next duke.

Since Matthias had no siblings and had died without fathering an heir, the title of Duke Herhardt would have to go to a collateral member of the family. However, Elise von Herhardt was stubbornly insisting that the choice of a successor be deferred until after they had recovered her son’s body and held a proper funeral for him. Thus, for the time being, the Herhardt family remained with no one at its head.
“They really shouldn’t have delayed his wedding to Lady Claudine. If he had married her when he was originally supposed to, the line of succession wouldn’t have been tragically cut off like this.”
“Even if they had married, it doesn’t mean that Claudine would have borne him a son. Claudine’s mother never bore a son, after all.”
“That’s true, I suppose.” Everyone nodded in agreement.
“The Brandt family is lucky. If they had forced the wedding through before Duke Herhardt joined the war, all they would have gotten for it is a widowed daughter.”
“How is Claudine doing, by the way?”
“I heard that she’s going to be engaged again soon!” “Oh my, already?”
“I suppose there’s no point in wasting time now that her connection to the Herhardt family has been severed. From what I hear, Countess Brandt has been working hard to find a new man for her daughter, and apparently she’s found a good candidate.”
“No matter how good of a candidate he is, no one could compare to Duke Herhardt. Oh, I feel so bad for Claudine and her mother.”
As the women continued exchanging words of sympathy and relief, excited cheers began to ring out from across the street. The homecoming parade had

begun.








Practically everyone in the city had gone to greet the soldiers returning from the war. As a result, the park was completely empty, and Layla was able to enjoy an especially relaxing stroll with her baby. Her slow, light footsteps, the wheels of the baby carriage, and the rustling of the leaves all combined like a beautiful piece of music.
As she emerged from the tree-lined path, she extended the carriage’s canopy to protect her sleeping baby from the sun. By now, Layla had completely forgotten her worries about the baby being born small, as it was very healthy and growing rapidly. It had a gentle personality, and it hadn’t gotten sick even once. It was truly a blessing.
Layla took a circuitous route, intentionally avoiding the boulevard. The sound of her slow footsteps continued along the quiet path. She didn’t have the courage to face the parade. She still couldn’t accept that Matthias wasn’t returning even though the war was over.
“The weather sure is nice today, isn’t it?” she asked her sleeping baby. The breeze blew the baby’s soft hair.
“Summer is Mommy’s favorite season. What’s yours? Oh, right, I guess you still haven’t experienced autumn.”
The edges of her smile trembled slightly. She continued to whisper to the sleeping baby as she pushed the carriage. Before she knew it, she was back near the house.

The house had been intended as a temporary dwelling place for her until Matthias returned, but now it had become her new home. Matthias’s attorney and butler had both worked hard to ensure that everything Matthias had promised would become reality, so that Layla and the baby could live a stable life.
It’s been so peaceful, hasn’t it? I’m sure it will keep being peaceful, too. Even though he’s not here. Even though he hasn’t come back.
To stave off the sadness that was creeping up on her, she quickened her pace. Before she got very far, however, she had to stop again. Her shoe had come untied.
After letting out a brief sigh, she bent down and started tying the laces. Just as she was finishing the knot, a memory flashed into her mind out of nowhere. She recalled the day she had spent with him in an unfamiliar city, when everything had been so strange. Tire image of him kneeling down to tie her shoelaces for her was so vivid in her mind, it was like he was right there in
front of her.
She recalled his calm, blue eyes, the gentle warmth of his body, the feeling of his hands against her shoe, and the way the tidy knot he tied bounced with each step she took.
As the memory grew more and more distinct, her eyes filled with tears.
However, she didn’t let a single tear fall.
After catching her breath, she finished tying the knot and stood up. As she resumed pushing the carriage, her steps were even lighter and more lively than before, and the tears in her eyes dried away without falling.
When she arrived back home, there was a tall man in a military uniform facing the house. Her eyes widened as he slowly turned around. The two of them looked at each other with identical smiles on their faces.

“Kyle!”
As its mother cried out joyfully, the baby opened its eyes.







While Layla made some tea, Kyle waited in the sunny living room with the baby. Sure enough, the baby was incredibly calm and well-behaved, and hadn’t cried even when it first saw Kyle. For a moment, it had stared at the unfamiliar face with a look of surprise. After only a few seconds, however, this had given way to delighted laughter. Kyle was relieved to discover that, even though the baby’s blue eyes looked just like the duke’s, their personalities seemed quite distinct.
When Layla entered the room carrying the tea, Kyle smiled and asked, “‘What’s its name?”
“Felix,” she replied, smiling back at him. Then she picked up little Felix and sat down across from Kyle.
The old friends then proceeded to have an ordinary conversation, catching up with each other in a way they hadn’t been able to do during the war. He smiled as it struck him how remarkable it was that he was sitting across from her in that moment.
“I’m so glad that you made it back safe and sound, Kyle,” she said, smiling calmly.
“I feel the same way about you,” he replied, smiling back at her as he set his tea cup down. “I’m so glad that you recovered your health and had this beautiful baby.”

“Well, we promised each other, right?” “Ah yes, you’re right.”
His eyes grew slightly moist as he was reminded of their last moments together the previous autumn.
The original plan was for him to accompany her all the way to Ratse. He had obtained a travel pass for this purpose, but after Ettarre joined the war, all travel passes were canceled. He felt horrible sending Layla off when she was still recovering from her illness, not to mention pregnant. However, she cheerfully reassured him that she would be all right.
That day, as they parted at the military hospital by the border, they promised each other that they would meet again in a healthy state. She extended her hand for a handshake. The moment he started shaking her hand, he lost his emotional restraint and burst into tears. She comforted him once again, and then left. As she walked off carrying her suitcase, she knew her gaunt back was shaking, but Kyle didn’t say anything. That had been their last moment together.
“Does the House of Herhardt know about Felix?” he asked, looking at the giggling baby sitting on its mother’s lap.
She shook her head and lowered her eyes.
“Are you planning to keep raising him alone like this?” “Yes, until the duke comes back.”
His eyes widened at this unexpected response. “Layla…”
“He promised,” she said, smiling, as she stroked the baby’s hair. “He promised that he would come back to me. So… I’m going to wait for him.”
“But Layla, he’s-”
“I know. I know that, Kyle.” Her eyes filled with tears, but she didn’t lose her smile. “But I still want to wait for him. I know it sounds ridiculous, but I

feel like if I keep waiting, he might come.”
What can I say to her? She’s waiting for a dead man to come to her, Kyle thought.
He had seen with his own eyes the catastrophic ruins of the city. The ramparts that had stood for centuries had been reduced to rubble. Practically even7 Northern Alliance soldier that fought in that battle had been killed. If Kyle hadn’t been assigned to transport wounded soldiers to the field hospital, his own corpse would have likely been buried amidst the rubble as well.
There was a lot he wanted to tell Layla, but he kept his silence. After all, the war was over now, so the duke’s body might be recovered soon. Perhaps that would allow her to finally accept reality.
After that, their conversation returned to ordinary matters. However, they both knew that things could never be like before. This realization brought him an odd sense of relief.
“I should get going. My train wall be leaving soon,” he said, checking his watch as he rose from his seat.
“You’re going back to Karlsbar, right?”
“Yeah. I’m going to stay with my parents until it’s time to resume my studies.”
“I see.”
They walked to the front door, and he said, “Take care of yourself, Layla. Felix too.” And then he extended his hand for a handshake, just like he had done on that summer day when he was twelve years old, when he had first encountered that girl amid the fresh scent of roses in the Arvis garden.
He knew it was really goodbye this time. Perhaps they would run into each other from time to time, and exchange pleasant greetings. But that would be it.

He knew that was the most he could hope for, and he was finally able to accept that.
“We will. And you too, Kyle,” she replied, accepting his handshake and smiling, just as she had done as a young girl on that summer day in Arvis.
The baby looked with curiosity at the two people shaking hands, and then burst into cheerful laughter.
Without tears or anguish, Kyle then turned around and started walking down the road. A cool breeze lessened the summer heat.
Without looking back, he walked all the way to the bend in the road, and then passed out of sight.







A lavish black car pulled up in front of the military hospital in the south of Lovita. The uniformed chauffeur hurried to open the back passenger door, and then an elderly gentleman with a tired face stepped out.
The officers standing in a row to greet him all bowed their heads respectfully. “Thank you for coming,” one of them said.
The distinguished gentleman was a collateral member of the Lovita royal family, and had been ordered to visit this remote location by none other than the king of Lovita himself. When he had received the message stating that there was an important matter that the king needed investigated, he had known he had no choice but to follow the order.
A favorite nephew of the king had been severely injured in the war, and was receiving treatment at a Berg military hospital. When the Lovita royal

family asked for his speedy return, Berg had placed certain conditions on the deal. It was this gentleman’s burdensome task to come check on whether these conditions had been met. He had been selected for the task because he was the cousin of Katharina von Herhardt.
“We have already confirmed his identity through the means available to us, but we wanted a relative to give final confirmation. That is why we requested your presence,” the officer explained as he guided the gentleman through the quiet ward. “He’s in here.”
They stopped in front of a room at the end of the hallway. The gentleman nodded, and the officer knocked on the door and then opened it.
Inside the room, a young man was sitting by the window, smoking a cigarette. He stood up to greet the visitor. A breeze blew through the open window, shaking the tails of his untucked shirt. Because he was standing in front of the bright window, it was hard to make out his face, but it was clear that he was a tall, strong young man.
The gentleman clicked his tongue as he observed that the man didn’t look sick or injured at all.
He had heard that the man was an officer who had been found severely injured amid the ruins of a battle in which the. Berg soldiers had been thoroughly defeated. He was an enemy soldier, but to the medic who found him, he was simply a person who had survived the battle. He had thus been transported to the rear military hospital for treatment.
He was in critical condition, and it took quite a long time before he recovered enough to regain consciousness. Eventually, however, he had made a miraculous recovery. Then, rather than being sent to a prison camp, he had been sent to a very nice hospital. This was because of his status as a high- ranking aristocrat.

The Herhardt family had been ready to hold the funeral as soon as the war ended and they recovered the body. It seemed that this man had been playing a trick on everyone. The gentleman wondered what possible reason someone could have for doing nothing when he surely knew that everyone back home thought he was dead. Even considering the chaos of war, the gentleman felt that the doctors who believed this fraudster’s words should be dealt with strictly by the military courts.
Just as the gentleman s anger was starting to rise, the young man tossed his cigarette into the ashtray and slowly walked towards the gentleman. The gentleman gasped as he observed that the young man’s elegant posture and movements perfectly matched those of the duke. The young man gazed calmly at him, with a slight smile on his lips, and then nodded slightly to greet him.
Taken aback, the gentleman could only chuckle in response.




147.

The Color of My Happiness.






With the war over, the tight restrictions on border crossing had been loosened. The car passed through the border checkpoint without even
an inspection, and then proceeded onto the road leading into Berg. The Berg countryside, which had seen no battles during the war, was incredibly peaceful and serene. It was as if nothing had happened but the changing of the seasons.
Matthias gazed calmly out the window at the verdant scenery flowing by.
He remained silent for the entire long trip to Ratse.
After losing consciousness as the rampart beneath him caved in, he had awoken in bed in a Lovitan military hospital. As he felt the horrible pain throughout his body, he realized that he had survived.
I’m alive, he thought. I can return to Layla.
This wonderful thought brought him far more relief than the painkillers coursing through his veins. Despite his pain, he laughed with joy. Then, as the

medicine took effect and he fell asleep, he dreamed of Layla. It was beautiful.
After that, his pain gradually lessened, but he remained bedridden for quite some time. By the time he was able to move around freely, the spring flowers were in full bloom.
He was well aware that Berg newspapers had likely been reported him as missing or dead. He was also aware that, even with the war going on, it would have been possible to send a message alerting those in Berg that he was alive. However, he instead decided to wait it out, and he let two seasons pass without sending any messages to Berg.
It wasn’t hard for him to make it through that time, because by waiting like that, he was laying the groundwork so that he could openly live with Layla.
Of course, he knew it would be hard on Layla, and on his family, all of whom would be needlessly grieving over his supposed death. However, like all decisions he had made with regards to Layla, he did not regret it. Nor would he ever regret it.
“We’re almost there, Your Grace,” the chauffeur said, joyfully, as he turned onto the boulevard where the museums were located.
In contrast to his assistant and the chauffeur, whose eyes were filled with tears, Matthias had a calm, emotionless expression on his face. He nodded, and adjusted his clothes, even though they were already perfect. Then he sat up straighter in his seat and looked down the boulevard at the residential area up ahead.
It wouldn’t be long now, but it wasn’t quite time yet. He had some tasks to complete first, but they wouldn’t take too long. The Herhardt family’s Ratse mansion came into sight. A faint smile spread over his face.







Elise von Herhardt remained skeptical until the end. She understood that the chances were very low that it was a mistake, but she still hesitated to believe. After all, she had already accepted that her son was dead, and had been fully prepared to hold the funeral once his body was recovered. Now she was being told that he was coming home alive!
If this turned out to be a lie, she didn’t think she would be able to handle it. This was why she insisted that the matter be kept secret to the outside world. She wasn’t going to believe anything until she saw it with her own eyes.
Hurrying into the drawing room, Hessen said, “The car has just arrived.”
Elise and Katharina looked at each other with expressions full of nervous anticipation. The staff members in the room began to stir, and soon the slow, regular rhythm of someone’s footsteps could be heard approaching the room. As Elise watched the door of the room with bated breath, tears were already forming in her eyes. Dowager Herhardt was no different.
Both of them recognized those footsteps at once. It could be no one else but Matthias. Sure enough, after a short while, the door slowly opened, and Matthias miraculously appeared.
Katharina and Elise both burst into tears at once. Matthias slowly approached them, then came to a stop in the middle of the room, where he was illuminated by a square of sunlight streaming in through the window.
With the same calm, respectful elegance that he had displayed while bidding them farewell before he went off to the war, he greeted them. “Hello,

Grandmother. And hello, Mother. I’ve returned.”






Layla put on her wide-brimmed straw hat and tied its ribbon under her chin. She then grabbed the blanket and the snacks she had prepared and stuffed them into her bag. Once she put the strap of the bulging bag over her shoulder, she was ready to head out for the picnic. Her French braid bounced behind her as she stepped out of the house with the baby in her arms.
As if relieved that he was no longer in the cramped space of his mother’s belly, Felix was growing at an impressive rate. Layla had checked and discovered that he was bigger than the average baby his age. It delighted her to think that he was probably going to grow up to be tall like his father, even if that meant that Felix would quickly become too heavy for her to lift him.
After putting him into the baby carriage, she started pushing it and walking towards the park. It was a typical Berg summer day—the sun was bright but not too hot, and there was a refreshing breeze blowing.
The baby seemed to be in a particularly good mood, and was babbling excitedly. As they progressed towards the park, Layla would point things out, saying, “Look at that!” In response, Felix would giggle delightedly and waggle his little arms and legs.
Was Matthias like this as a baby? she wondered. However, it was impossible to imagine Matthias von Herhardt behaving with this much innocent joviality, even as a baby. Rather, she imagined that even when he was Felix’s age, Matthias showed just as much aristocratic reserve as he did now. This thought

made her giggle, which caused Felix to giggle as well. Just then, they arrived at the park.
It was a weekend, and the park was bustling with people enjoying the nice weather. As she surveyed the crowd, Layla pouted briefly at the sight of all the young couples with their babies, but then she quickly recovered her smile. Seeing his mother smile, Felix then smiled too. That was all she needed.
Adjusting the strap of her bag, she pushed the carriage towards a quiet, pretty spot by the forest. As they passed by the fountain at the center of the park, she bought a colorful balloon from a man at a kiosk. She tied the balloon’s string to the carriage, and Felix’s blue eyes widened in amazement.
Blue was no longer the color of sadness to Layla. Now it was the color of the greatest happiness she had ever known. This had been the case ever since Felix had first opened his eyes. Or rather, it had been the case since the first morning that she and Matthias had spent together, when they had lain in bed, quietly gazing into each other’s eyes. Or perhaps it had been since even earlier than that. The more she thought about it, the less certain she was of when the shift had occurred.
“Wow! The balloon is so pretty! Don’t you think so?” she exclaimed to Felix, as he gleefully followed the balloon’s movements with his eyes while giggling and babbling.
As the balloon continued to be tossed around by the gentle breeze, the carriage it was attached to came to a stop amid the shade of the park’s forest.

The sound of the baby’s laughter mingled with that of his mother, who looked at home amid the greenery of the forest.





Matthias, the masterpiece of the House of Herhardt, had miraculously returned, and he looked just the same as before he had left. Despite looking the same, however, he said something completely unexpected.
“Layla is here.”
He said this in such a calm tone, his mother doubted her ears.
He had just finished giving his account of the injuries he had suffered on the front line of the war, followed by his time in the Lovitan hospital and his return to Berg. He had also apologized for the concern he had caused by neglecting his responsibilities. Elise had been surprised, but also relieved, to see him looking the same as always after returning from the brink of death.
Now that he was speaking about Layla, however, she was utterly shocked.
Not knowing what to say, she turned to her mother-in-law.
While the two women exchanged flustered looks, Matthias continued calmly talking.
“My child is here too.”
Elise and Katharina, having barely regained their composure, reacted with even more shock than before.
“Goodness!” Elise exclaimed, almost falling from her seat.
Katharina froze with astonishment. While they had already guessed that Matthias and Layla had been in a relationship together, they had never imagined that there was a baby.

“Layla and I are going to be married,” he said, remaining just as calm and nonchalant as he had been since his arrival.
“Matthias!” Elise shouted.
Katharina let out an exasperated sigh and slumped in her chair. “An illegitimate child?” Elise asked.
“That’s certainly a cause for concern. But there are many other ways to take care of this situation.”
“Your mother is right, Matthias. Since your engagement to Lady Claudine has been cut off, there are plenty of other ladies from distinguished families to consider, so…” Katharina trailed off, clutching her aching head.
After thinking for a while longer, it suddenly struck her why Matthias had stayed alone in a hospital in an enemy country for all that time, when he could have easily contacted his Lovitan relatives. By allowing the news of his supposed death to spread throughout Berg, he had gotten something very valuable for himself. Elise seemed to have made the same realization. The two women exchanged knowing glances.
“I know this is hard for both of you to accept,” Matthias continued. He was paying no mind to the looks of suspicion on the women’s faces. In fact, he was behaving in much the same way as he had when the two women had first suspected he was in a relationship with Layla. “However, the world is changing at an ever-increasing rate. Things aren’t the way they used to be.”
“Matthias…”
“To be honest, it doesn’t matter whether the world changes or not.”
He didn’t seem to be trying very hard to persuade them. Rather, he sounded merely like he was informing them of decisions he had already made.
“If you really can’t accept it, then I won’t stubbornly insist on burdening the Herhardt name with these troubles.”

“What do you mean by that?” Elise asked, barely able to muster her voice. “I will no longer live as a Herhardt.”
“Matthias!”
“Henceforth, the name ‘Duke Matthias von Herhardt’ will refer to the honorable nobleman who died in battle while fighting for the empire.”
Goodness. Gone was the joy that Lady Herhardt had felt at seeing Matthias looking like his old self. Perhaps she didn’t know all the ways in which the world was changing, but one thing was clear: the war had completely changed her son.
He was obviously not making an empty threat. After living a perfect life with the singular aim of becoming the perfect successor so that he could carry the mantle as the next Duke Herhardt, he now seemed determined to follow a different path entirely. And Elise knew her son well enough to realize that he would do whatever was necessary to follow this new path.
Her son had seemingly come back from the dead. And now, she and her mother-in-law had two choices. They could lose him again, or they could accept the things he was telling them.
“This is the decision I have made,” he said, looking at both of the women. “I will not be changing my mind.”
Elise and Katharina glanced at each other again, then laughed awkwardly.
Unshaken, Matthias said, “The two of you need to make your choice. I will respect whichever option you prefer.”




148.

The Bird Returned.






The park in the center of Ratse was large enough to contain a lake and a forest within it. Layla’s favorite part of the park was a spot on its western
side that had the prettiest view of the lake and the forest. When she sat there, she almost felt like she was back at Arvis. Despite all the pain and sadness that she had suffered at Arvis, she still missed it dearly, and thought it was the most beautiful place in the world.
After spreading out the blanket in the shade of a tree, she took the container of snacks out of her bag. Even though it was just her and the baby, she was determined to have a perfect, pretty picnic. Perhaps Felix was still too young to be able to consciously register the memory, but she figured that the experience would inscribe itself in his heart one way or another.
There was a lot that she wanted to share with Felix—moments of happiness with his mother, even trivial ones; affectionate talk; warm looks;

laughter. She wanted him to know that no matter what happened, there was someone in the world who would love and believe in him. Uncle Bill had been such a person to her, and she wanted to be sure that Felix would see her the same way.
Until the duke came back, she intended to shower Felix with a double dose of love and affection. She would make sure that he always had plenty to eat, and plenty to laugh about, so that he would grow up happy and healthy. Even if he didn’t have a father right now, he was going to get all the love he deserved.
After feeding Felix, she gave him his rattle, which he became engrossed in playing with. This gave her the freedom to enjoy her own lunch at a leisurely pace.
Just as she was biting into the peach she had brought as dessert, the baby crawled towards her. With each juicy bite she took of the fruit, he bobbled his head in fascination.
As she caught a glimpse of his blue eyes staring at her, she froze. He resembled his father so much, she couldn’t stop memories of Matthias from flooding into her mind.
“I’ll return to you. I promise.”
Her memory of him promising to return was just as vivid as ever. “I love you. ”
She hadn’t gotten the chance to reply to him when he told her he loved her. She took her glasses off and rubbed her eyes, and then looked at the baby and whispered, “I love you. I really love you.” She could say this to her heart’s content now. It was the most passionate feeling she had. “I love you. I love you,
I love you, I love you!”
Felix seemed to like hearing it. As she kept repeating the phrase, his blue eyes twinkled, and he giggled delightedly.

After thoroughly wiping the peach juice from her hands, she lifted the baby into her arms and closed her eyes. The baby’s soft scent tickled her nose. She hugged him tighter.







The woman who lived next door to Layla was on her way back from a stroll around the neighborhood when she stopped suddenly at the sight of a man knocking at Layla’s door. “Excuse me, sir…?”
The man stopped knocking and looked in her direction.
“Are you looking for the young lady who lives at this house with her baby?” she asked, with an inquisitive look on her face.
“Yes, I am,” he replied.
“The young mother with blonde hair and glasses, right?” she asked. “Yes,” he replied.
After looking closely at his face for a moment, she gasped and said, “Oh my. Let me guess—you’re the baby’s father, and you’re just now getting back from the war. Is that right? You look just like the baby. I know we haven’t met before, so I’m sorry for acting so informal. There was just something so familiar about your face from the moment I saw you.” Before he could respond, she continued. “Oh but how do you like that? You came all the way back from the war, and you’ve missed her.”
“She isn’t home?”
“No, she left just a short while ago with the baby. She said they were going to the park to have a picnic,” she said, clicking her tongue ruefully.

Her eyes remained fixed on the man’s face. She had noticed that the baby didn’t particularly resemble its mother, so it was no surprise that it looked so much like its father. However, there was something else about the man’s face that seemed extremely familiar to her. It didn’t seem like it just because of the baby. She was certain she didn’t know him. She must have seen him somewhere before. But where?
“She and the baby go to the park a lot,” she went on. “They usually don’t come back home until dusk. You could go try to find them, but who knows, you might miss each other again. Maybe you’d better just come wait for her in my house. Would you like to?”
“No, that’s all right,” he said, glancing in the direction of the park. He walked down the steps from the front door and stopped in front of the woman and said, “Thank you, ma’am.” With that, he brushed past her and went on his way at a relaxed pace.
As he reached the end of the road, where a black car was waiting, a man got out of the driver’s seat and hurriedly opened the back passenger door. This piqued the woman’s curiosity even more. She could tell that he wasn’t any ordinary man. She kept watching the car as it drove off into the distance.
“I know I’ve seen him somewhere before…” she muttered, as a gentle breeze blew through the neighborhood on that quiet weekend afternoon.







After eating and playing to his heart’s content, little Felix fell asleep. For a while, Layla lay next to him on the picnic blanket, gazing at his angelic face.

Then, sighing with relief, she sat up. Her hair, which had become Felix’s plaything, was quite disheveled. Her glasses were covered with his tiny fingerprints, making everything she saw through them look blurry. She cautiously inched towards her bag, which was on the edge of the blanket. First she rubbed her glasses clean, then she let down her messy hair.
Felix liked her hair as much as his father did. It thus seemed that he took after his father in more ways than just his appearance, although the son was a bit more reckless than the father in terms of how he treated her hair. Whenever she was holding Felix in her arms, he would become transfixed by her shimmering blonde hair and grab at it with both hands, laughing joyously. She always felt sorry when she had to wrest it out of his grip.
She never would have thought the day would come when having her hair grabbed made her happy. Chuckling to herself about this, she started combing her fingers through her hair to attempt to tame it. Even when she put it in a ponytail or a braid, Felix always managed to grab it. The only way to prevent that would be by cutting it, but she didn’t want to do that.
Her fine blonde hair flowed down over the collar and shoulders of her white dress. Just as she was about to start braiding it, she got distracted by the sight of the late- afternoon sunlight streaming through the forest’s thick foliage. As she gazed at the light, transfixed, the still surface of the lake started glimmering with a rich, golden hue.
“So pretty…” she muttered.
The whole environment around her looked as peaceful and beautiful as a painting. The time she had spent in that hellish city, where bombs fell from the sky, now felt like a faintly remembered dream.
She pulled her legs towards her chest and rested her cheek on her knees.

Some waterfowl slew leisurely over the lake. They were migratory birds that spent their summers in Berg. A breeze brushed past the wild rose bushes in the forest, picking up their sweet scent. The rustling of the lush vegetation reminded her of the sound of her bicycle’s chain.
Suddenly, her enjoyment of the perfect, beautiful summer day was interrupted by a strange feeling.
How can it be so nice? He’s not here. He hasn’t come back.
The strange feeling slowly grew, until the sound of a baby’s crying snapped her back to reality. Felix had awoken, and was staring up at the sky, wailing. She saw the reason at once. The balloon had come untied from the handle of the baby carriage, and it had floated up and gotten caught on a branch of a nearby tree.
“It’s all right!” she cried out, trying to calm him down. “It’s no big deal.
Everything will be just fine!”
Felix stopped crying, stunned by the sudden rise in his mother’s energy level. She wiped the tears from his face, and then bolted up and started heading for the tree where the balloon was stuck.
“Don’t worry, Mommy will get it for you!”







The park was huge, and all that Matthias had been told was that she was somewhere within it. Nonetheless, it was easy for him to find her. He knew she would be at a spot near the forest and the lake, where there would be a lot of birds. He knew that she would prefer a relatively quiet spot, without too

many people around. Then again, it wouldn’t be completely isolated, either— despite the tough front she put on, he knew his beautiful bird was timid at heart.
He headed straight to a spot that fit this description and, sure enough, she was there. Sitting in the shade of the trees on a picnic blanket, she was gazing at the scenery, until her attention was drawn away by a crying baby next to her. It was only then that Matthias realized there was another person there— his child.
He stopped in his tracks to take in the sight. Meanwhile, Layla sprung up and started walking quickly towards the woods. The baby tilted its head with interest as it watched its mother.
Matthias then resumed walking towards them. He reached the edge of the blanket just as Layla reached the foot of a tree a short distance away.
He looked at her for a moment, wondering what she was up to. Then, all of a sudden, she started climbing up the tree. When he saw the balloon caught on a branch midway up the tree, he finally understood what she was doing.
He chuckled to himself. Just as he did, the baby looked up at him. He looked down, and the two of them made eye contact. He raised his eyebrows slightly. The baby blinked in surprise.
He laughed once more, a little louder this time. When he looked up again, Layla was already straddling the branch, reaching out for the balloon that was caught on it.
Without hesitation, he started walking towards the tree.







The balloon was caught near the end of the branch, making it hard to reach, but once Layla stretched out her arm as far as she could, she was able to grab it. “Look! Mommy found the…” She trailed off before she could finish her cheerful exclamation.
Near the foot of the tree, there was a man, standing up straight with one arm behind his back, looking up at her. When their eyes met, he cocked his head in a mischievous way that stood in contrast to his dignified bearing.
It was Matthias. Her mind had gone completely blank, but she was certain of that. As far-fetched as it sounded, she had no doubt. It was him. It couldn’t be anyone else.
Holding the balloon in one hand, she continued to stare at him. Her other hand was trembling slightly as she used it to keep her balance on the tree branch. As he gazed up at her with his gleaming blue eyes, a smile slowly spread across his face.
Without saying anything, he moved his left hand from behind his back. The ends of the white ribbon tied to his wrist fluttered in the soft evening breeze.
It was strange. She had been ardently missing him, thinking about him several times each day. And yet now, she couldn’t find any words to say to him. All she could do was bite her lip and stare at him with widened eyes. Also remaining silent, he slowly spread out his arms.

Her memories after that were hazy. She vaguely recalled nimbly descending the tree and running towards him. The tears flowing from her eyes blurred her vision, but she didn’t care. As she ran faster and faster towards him, her hair billowed behind her like a pair of golden wings. There was so much she wanted to say to him, but when she opened her lips, all that came out was a cry that sounded even more infantile than that of their son.
She flew into Matthias’s arms as lightly as a bird. As she did so, the balloon string slipped from her grip, and the balloon started floating away.
With eyes that looked just like his father’s, little Felix gazed at the pretty balloon as it ascended far up into the sky. For a moment, he seemed about to cry, but then his eyes went wide with amazement. In the spot in the sky where the balloon had receded out of sight, a flock of birds was flying by.
As his eyes started sparkling with fascination, he shifted his gaze to his mother and father, who were embracing each other. For a moment, he seemed perplexed, as if he was wondering if he should start crying like his mother was. Instead, he burst into delighted laughter.
As the setting sun infused the sky with a rosy hue, the flock of birds descended and landed on the lake.
I knew my bird would return, Layla thought. It was a wonderful summer evening.


149.
Epilogue 1.


The wedding was set to be held in the late summer.
Everyone was quite surprised to hear that Duke Herhardt had not died in battle, but was alive and back in Berg. However, this surprise was soon replaced by the even greater shock brought by the news of this unprecedented wedding.
The empire’s highest-ranking nobleman was marrying a lowly orphan.
Furthermore, this orphan was the adopted daughter of an employee who had worked on the duke’s estate. And as if that weren’t enough, she had previously been engaged to the son of the Herhardts’ family doctor. Soon, everyone in high society was discussing the situation with grave concern.
“I heard that the duke was severely injured in the war. Maybe the shock of that experience caused this shift in his behavior. I’ve heard that things like that are common among wounded soldiers.”
“If that were the case, Dowager and Lady Herhardt would never have approved the wedding. By the way, I heard that the duke and the woman have a baby together. But get this—the baby was born at the end of last year.”
“What?! That means that he must have been having an illicit affair with the woman back when he was still engaged to Claudine, right?”
“That’s right. In other words, Matthias von Herhardt is marrying his mistress after fathering an illegitimate child with her. Goodness.”

That summer, one could go to any aristocratic party in the empire and hear people gossiping about that shameful wedding, even while the two people at the center of the scandal stayed out of the public eye.
“This is a betrayal to us all! It’s insulting! I will not be attending the wedding. I simply can’t accept that lowly woman as a duchess!” one aristocrat said.
Such declarations were commonplace, and they were always met with agreement from the other aristocrats present. The aristocracy already felt threatened by the rapid changes going on in society. A nobleman marrying a commoner could only accelerate this process. And the fact that it was no less than Duke Herhardt committing this affront only made it worse!
However, there were other perspectives to consider. “You know, as absurd as the whole situation is, the fact remains that we’re talking about the House of Herhardt. No one can afford to make enemies with that family. They are just too dominant over the Berg business world.”
The companies owned by the Herhardt family were expanding rapidly. The influence of capitalists was steadily replacing that of the old aristocratic order, and it would have been foolhardy for anyone to declare opposition to the family leading the way in that change. Especially considering that this family was seen as the symbol of the old order as well.
While aristocrats were thus divided into two opposed camps, the problematic wedding was fast approaching. The ceremony was going to be held on an evening in the late summer, at Arvis.
Duke Herhardt shocked high society by inviting only close friends and family. Those aristocrats who had planned to shame the Duke Herhardt by refusing to attend his wedding, instead had to settle for denouncing his rude arrogance he was showing by not inviting them.

Meanwhile, the day of the wedding was still approaching.







After a nervous, nearly sleepless night, Layla stared blankly at Matthias as he lay sleeping next to her. In the light of the morning sun, his scars were even more visible. Besides the gunshot wound on his arm, which she already knew about, there were several other scars on his arms and torso.
When she first saw him in the park, he had looked just the same as always
—his face, posture, and movements were exactly how she remembered them. Because of this, it had come as quite a shock to her when he had first removed his shirt and she saw how much damage his body had incurred in the war. She had burst into tears at the sight. Just looking at the scars had made her feel pain in her own body.
“Don’t cry. Your favorite part is unscathed, ”he had said, smirking, as he pointed at his face.
Too flustered to say anything in response, she had simply punched him instead, knowing full well that she wasn’t strong enough for her fists to hurt him.
As she gazed at his scars now in the morning sun, she furtively reached out and felt them. They had faded with time somewhat, but they hadn’t disappeared. Despite the scars, he was still handsome and in great physical condition, but she couldn’t help feeling bad for him.
Soon, she noticed with a start that he had awoken, and was looking at her. “Does it hurt?” she asked.

“No. You can keep feeling them.”
Suddenly feeling bashful, she pulled her hand away from his body and said, “You should go now.”
“Go where?” he asked.
“Where do you think? To your room,” she replied, frowning, as she gestured in the direction of the door leading to their separate bedrooms.
The previous weekend, they had traveled from Ratse to Arvis, where they would be staying until the wedding. Her joy at being reunited with the beautiful scenery of her childhood was short-lived. Shortly after arriving, she was swarmed by Arvis staff members. From that point on, preparations for the wedding had been filling practically every waking hour of her days.
The staff members were used to thinking of Layla as Mr. Remmer’s daughter, so it was hard for them to adjust to treating her as the future Duchess Herhardt. It felt awkward for Layla too. After a lifetime spent on equal footing with them, suddenly she was supposed to act like their superior? She hated the thought. In fact, she didn’t think she would ever get used to it. She couldn’t tell this to anyone, however.
From the start, she had been aware that marrying Matthias von Herhardt amounted to more than just becoming one man’s wife. But now, facing the incredible amount of responsibility ahead of her, she felt a little overwhelmed.
The previous week was a blur in her memory. Even though she was in the Arvis of her youth, it felt like she was in a completely foreign world. She had to talk differently, think differently, and move differently.
Furthermore, she could no longer spend every moment of the day with her child. After she had mustered up the courage to tell Elise that she wanted to look after Felix herself, her future mother-in-law had scoffed and replied, “You are a Herhardt now, dear.”

Layla had well understood the meaning hidden behind that simple remark. She didn’t like being told she had to spend time away from her son, but she wasn’t going to whine about it. She wanted to do her best to fill her new role, and since she was doing it for the sake of Matthias and their son, she felt like she could.
However, no matter how hard she tried, her feelings of inadequacy only grew. Ultimately, she didn’t think she would be able to become the elegant queen of high society that her predecessors, the previous duchesses, had been.
She felt this hopeless feeling most acutely late at night at the end of a long, hectic day, when she was all alone in the duchess’s bedroom, which was almost frightening in its immense size and lavish decor. When she looked at the faces of the noblewomen whose portraits were hanging on the walls, she felt like they were glaring back at her with looks of reproach. She knew that she would have felt much better if she could be holding Felix in her arms, feeling his warmth and smelling his soft scent.
As the night wore on, she remained too anxious and frightened to sleep. Then, all of a sudden, she heard a sound. Looking in its direction, she almost fainted with surprise at the sight of a secret door opening. It was only when Matthias appeared in the doorway that she learned that there was a secret passageway connecting the duke’s and duchess’s bedrooms.
This was forbidden behavior. Elise and Katharina had made that quite clear to them. After summoning them to have a talk, they had expressed that, even though the couple was getting married after having a child out of wedlock, there were still certain rules of propriety that they would have to follow. Basically, until the wedding, they were to keep their distance from each other. Despite having been at that meeting right next to Layla, Matthias had

not hesitated in coming to find her that night. And she hadn’t turned him away.
She felt at ease when she was with him. It was a similar feeling to the one she got when she was holding Felix, with the added bonus that he was much bigger than their son and was capable of holding her as well.
As the sun continued to rise, she said with more urgency, “You should go.
What if we get caught?”
Lying on his side with his head leaning on his hand, he smirked at her. He found it cute that she was still acting like they were having a secret affair. He decided not to disabuse her of that notion.
“Layla,” he whispered, still sounding groggy-
She could feel herself blushing immediately. Somehow, despite all the intimate things they had done together, just hearing him whisper her name was making her feel embarrassed.
“Smile for me,” he said, as he loosely twirled a lock of her hair around his finger.
After thinking for a moment, she gently frowned and replied, “I don’t like taking orders.”
“You won’t give me as simple a gift as a smile?” “Why should I?”
“Well, because it’s my wedding day.”
At this casual reply, she gasped. After staring intently at him for a moment, she looked nervously around the room, and then looked at him again, seeming as if she was about to cry. “I feel a little scared.”
“What are you scared of?”
“I’m scared that… maybe I don’t have it in me to become a real duchess. I’m going to study and practice and work as hard as I can… but what if I still can’t

manage it? What if I become a burden to you and our son?”
“That won’t happen, Layla,” he said, remaining utterly calm in the face of her uneasiness.
“How can you be so sure?”
“Because I won’t let it happen.” He then bent forward and kissed her on the cheek. There was not the slightest hint of hesitation or fear on his face.
She gazed at him for a moment, then broke down and smiled.
He was still an enigma to her. When he kept his promise and returned, she was extremely happy and moved. However, she also felt deeply hurt by him for intentionally hiding the fact that he was alive. Especially considering that he did so for two entire seasons. Then again, she knew that he had done it all for her sake, so she couldn’t stay angry at him.
As always, there was no sign of guilt or regret on his face. It was hard to see him as a good person when he showed such willingness to do whatever was necessary to achieve his ends, even going as far as to fake his own death. She didn’t fully understand his methods, particularly with regards to how he treated her. Nonetheless, she was willing to accept him.
When she ran into his open arms at the park after he returned, she embraced not only him, but all the pain and sadness of the past, as well as all the chaos of the days ahead. She loved him. Nothing was more important than that.
He cupped her cheek with his hand and whispered, “Again.”
Once again, she reluctantly smiled, this time while looking into his eyes. If a smile was the gift he wanted, she was willing to give it to him. After all, as he said, it was his wedding day.
They exchanged sweet looks, smiles, and kisses, first innocently, then with increasing passion. The morning sun continued to rise, but their bodies

remained entangled like lovers in the middle of the night.
Before leaving the duchess’s bedroom, he said, “I have a gift for you, too.” He walked back to the foot of the bed and stooped down to kiss her forehead. Then he whispered, “You can look forward to it.”




150.




Epilogue 2.




It didn’t take long for Felix von Herhardt to win the hearts of his grandmother and great-grandmother. The very first day that Matthias brought Layla and Felix to the Ratse mansion, Elise and Katharina fell in love with him. He looked just like his father had looked at that age. The resemblance was so striking, the former duchesses couldn’t help but burst into astonished laughter. There was no way they could reject Felix when he was so
clearly a Herhardt.
“It’s what’s best for the child, Elise,” Dowager Herhardt warmly said, trying to console her upset daughter-in-law.
“Yes, you’re right,” Lady Herhardt reluctantly replied. Her mother-in-law’s words had done little to soothe her heart, but when she looked at Felix’s smiling face as he lay in his great-grandmother’s arms, she felt much better.
