and father. She now saw him this way. The hate she had once felt towards him was gone from her heart. In its place was a calm peacefulness, as pure as the spring sunlight.
After visiting a few more exhibits, they walked back out onto the street, still holding hands. As they walked, she did most of the talking. Meanwhile, he quietly enjoyed the sight of her rosy cheeks and pretty lips as he listened to her talk about various subjects—the classes she was finding most interesting, and the ones she was finding most challenging; the new words that Felix had learned; the new curtains that she had hung in the bedroom; her plans for summer vacation. The triviality of all these subjects only made him happier to hear about them.
He gazed at her intently. As she briefly closed her eyes in thought and opened them again, the shadows of her eyelashes fluttered like the wings of a butterfly. When she then resumed speaking, her eyes glinted in the sunlight, as if they were full of an eternal summer. He had wanted so fervently to possess her, he had been wiling to do whatever it took, even forcefully abducting her and threatening her. In the end, none of that had made him feel like he truly possessed her, however. His twisted desire for her had destroyed him, but now he had risen from the ashes. And now, they both loved each other. Now he truly felt like she was his.
They had spent some time far apart from each other, but now, they were finally back together, holding hands, feeling neither hate nor sadness but only love as they started their life afresh. They were walking together not just as lovers, not just as husband and wife, but as family.
Suddenly, she stopped walking and, looking up at him, said, “Oh, by the way.” Her eyes twinkled through the thin, gold frames of her glasses. “Is there anything you want from me?”

“Want from you?”
“Yeah. We’re always doing things I want to do.”
“Hmm…” He looked up at the sky as if in contemplation, then instantly looked down at her again. “Nope. I’ve got everything I want.”
It was really true. She had been the one and only desire in his life. Now that they were together, he was satisfied.
“How does that make me look?” she replied. “Now I feel like I want too many things. Am I too greedy?”
He smiled. “No. You can be as greedy as you like.” He gently caressed her rosy cheek. “I don’t mind.”
He liked it when she expressed desire for things. He liked that he was able to satisfy these desires for her. Outside of that, there was nothing that he wanted for himself. He already felt like he was king of the world when she looked at him with her clear, gentle gaze.
Still feeling a little embarrassed, she turned away from him and started walking faster. However, he was able to keep pace with her without exerting much effort.
Sometimes she felt frightened thinking about their love. Their hearts had been in conflict for so long. Would they ever be able to achieve true love together? However, whenever they spent time together like today, her worries melted away. Now she felt secure around him. She knew that in his presence, she could cry as much as she wanted, get as angry as she wanted, be as greedy as she wanted, and he would always stay by her side. After all, when she had waited for him, he had returned to her. She was confident now that, as long as she had him, she would never be alone.
She came to a stop in the beautiful light of the park’s walking path. “Still, try to think of something.” She looked up at him with determination in her

eyes. “Even the lofty Duke Herhardt must want something.”
In the face of her earnest question, he simply smiled and shook his head. “Nothing, really?” she asked incredulously. Just then, a young couple
walked past with their daughter. Layla’s eyes perked up. Turning back to Matthias, she asked, “What about… a daughter?” She could feel herself blushing as she asked this, but she kept looking straight at him.
“A daughter?” he asked, narrowing his eyes.
“Yes! Remember, back when you were giving names for the baby, you said you wanted a daughter, right?”
“Oh, that,” he said, snickering. “You said that you wouldn’t be able to live without the baby, didn’t you?”
“Yes.”
“That was why I said that.” “What do you mean?”
“If I had died in battle and you had borne a son, the House of Herhardt might have taken him away from you.”
“That was why you hoped it was a girl?”
“Yes. Contrary to my wishes, it was a boy. Luckily, though, I did make it back to you alive, so it didn’t matter. I don’t need a daughter. Little Felix is all I need.”
She cocked her head, taken aback by his response. “Still, though, think how wonderful it would be to have a daughter. She’d probably look just like me.”
A daughter that looked just like Layla? His eyes filled with interest at the thought. He was curious what Layla had been like as a young child. The thought of being able to give that lonely little girl all the love and attention that she deserved was appealing. Since he couldn’t go back in time, it seemed like a decent alternative for Layla to bear a daughter that he could love as much as he

loved Layla. However, the more he thought about it, the more he knew that, no matter how much a daughter might resemble Layla, she wouldn’t really be Layla.
Clearing the fantasy from his mind, he looked again at Layla, his little bird, and solemnly asked, “Why would I need a daughter that looked like you, when I have the real you?’’
Yes, I already have you, he thought, as he gazed at her for a long while. She was still his one and only desire in life, and he was sure this would remain true until his dying day. As she looked at him with a bewildered expression on her face, he gently kissed her cheek, then smiled.
She gazed back at him with tears in her stunned eyes. She tried to back away, but he was holding her cheek firmly in his hand.
His response seemed strange to her, but she didn’t dislike it. Soon she realized that it was just another way of telling her that he loved her.
Matthias let go of her cheek and took a step back. Layla extended her hand toward him.
“Hold my hand,” she said, looking straight into his eyes. “Right now,” she added.
Matthias chuckled at her words. Then, his large, strong hand enveloped hers. As their fingers interlocked, he could feel her squeezing back gently.
Hand in hand, they continued their leisurely walk through the rose- scented park. She talked more than usual, and he laughed more than usual. It was an ordinary afternoon, made all the more special by its ordinariness. It was the height of spring, and their love was at its height as well. After that day, many more springs would come and go, but their love would endure forever.


